\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Aimed At Slowing Down Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 20:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jason Singer \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAl \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--After more than a week of abstinence, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's sudden \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on Tuesday may have been a shout at traders that the yen's recent surge against the dollar had gone far enough. But many analysts say it's more likely Tokyo was politely easing the dollar down, so nobody gets bruised amid its fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 As the world struggles to decipher the shifting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of both Japan and the U.S. that came out of last month's meeting of finance officials from the world's leading economies, previously clear motives are now open questions: Will Japan return to doggedly suppressing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - as it had been doing all year - albeit based on a newly strengthened base level? Or is this a historic change in the way Tokyo plans to manage the price of its money?  \par \par \uc2 Since the Sept. 20 meeting in Dubai of the Group of Seven big industrialized nations, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gone from strength to strength, gaining in 10 of the past 12 days. On Wednesday, it gained a bit more muscle to about Y111.13 to the dollar from Y111.43 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com)  \par \par \uc2 Authorities confirmed they muscled in to douse the rally in New York trading on Tuesday. The move came just hours after data was released showing Tokyo sold a record Y4.46 trillion in September to keep the yen weak. So far this year, but not counting Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent a record Y13.48 trillion weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan has drawn a line in the sand, not a line in concrete," said Marshall Gittler, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank AG in Tokyo. Though the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "was quite an overt and outspoken action to the market, we think they want a managed appreciation against the dollar," he said. He expects the yen to power its way to Y102 against the dollar in the next 12 months, a 15% strengthening from a recent low of Y120.55 in April.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has been steadfastly using all the tools it has to ensure the yen stays down, which helps keep the country's economic growth humming by aiding powerful exporters. But U.S. officials have been urging the world to accept a weaker dollar, to benefit U.S. manufacturers and lead to added U.S. employment. A large part of that effort has been a campaign to also convince China to adjust upward its fixed-exchange rate - calls that China has rebuffed.  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be rising, because the economy has improved this year. Gross domestic product, or total economic output, grew at an annualized 3.9% between April and June, the fastest clip among the world's biggest economies. In the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's closely watched tankan survey of large manufacturers released Wednesday, optimists outnumbered pessimists for the first time since December 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Many economists say Japan's expansion is still fragile. Exporters such as Toyota Motor Corp. (J.TYM), Canon Inc. (J.CAN) and Sony Corp. (J.SNY) drive the bulk of the Japanese economy's growth, and these firms will see profits evaporate in lockstep with a stronger yen.  \par \par \uc2 The discord between the interests of the world's two largest economies in terms of where they want their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade isn't new. But since World War II, the way the nations' cope with it has adjusted several times.  \par \par \uc2 In the Plaza Accord of 1985, the world's five biggest economies agreed to drive down the dollar to help spark U.S. economic growth, hoping that would later reap gains in other countries. The dollar fell by 30% in the following two years. In the early 1990s, the U.S. adopted a "strong-dollar policy" which drove global growth by developing manufacturing centers outside the U.S. That policy lasted until early this year, when the U.S. started suggesting that a weaker dollar was now in the world's best interest.  \par \par \uc2 Japan didn't agree with this until last week. Now, people such as Gittler say this is another major turning point, where the U.S. is encouraging Europe and Japan to stop relying on a single U.S. engine of economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan, as a good corporate citizen, needs to go along with that thinking," Gittler says.  \par \par \uc2 Others maintain that Japan isn't about to sacrifice the health of its frail economy just yet. These people say any long-term decline of the dollar will only happen with Tokyo kicking and screaming to stop it.  \par \par \uc2 "The attitude hasn't changed," said John Vail, senior global strategist at Mizuho Securities USA. It's just that the level at which Japan will suppress the yen has moved up slightly, he said. When Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday in U.S. trading, it sold yen through the Federal Reserve for the first time this year, rather than through Japanese banks in New York. Many analysts have said that move was endorsed by Washington to halt the yen's advance.  \par \par \uc2 "The Fed's involvement sends a powerful signal to these people to just cool it," Vail said. "I don't think the Bush administration wants Japan to sink back into deflation," he said, referring to a general fall in prices that has hammered Japan's economy for nearly five years. "A weak Japanese economy would be bad for the global economy, and bad for the U.S. economy."  \par \par \uc2 -By Jason Singer, The Wall Street Journal  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031001dza1000uc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
