\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late; Upbeat Economic Outlook  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Cris Larano  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 11:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MANILA (Dow Jones)--Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly higher late Wednesday against the U.S. dollar, rising on the back of an upbeat outlook for the Japanese economy and for most of the rest of Asia.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers said the recent call by the Group of Seven industrialized countries for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility continued to hold sway in the market, highlighted by the efforts of some monetary authorities in the region to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "This is still a follow-through of the whole G7 call," said Jimmy Koh, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UOB in Singapore. "I think generally, post-G7 meeting, everybody is of the view that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to make adjustments after the line in the sand for the dollar-yen was washed away."  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan survey Wednesday showed business managers grew more upbeat about business conditions in the three months to September, but Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears pressured the yen lower.  \par \par \uc2 Koh said efforts by the BOJ to prop up the U.S. dollar has been ineffective and is "only subsidizing the long yen entry level."  \par \par \uc2 At 0915 GMT, the dollar stood around Y111.17, higher than Y110.93 late Tuesday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 With China and Hong Kong closed for a holiday, Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  took center stage. The Thai baht rose to a new 38-month high of 39.855 baht to the dollar during the day as the market focused on strong economic prospects even as they stayed wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said the baht's recent strength against the dollar was caused by exports growing faster than imports, while debt repayment has slowed due to the sharp fall in outstanding foreign debt since the 1997 financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the dollar was quoted around THB39.82, below Tuesday's close of THB39.99.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah continued to bask in the credit ratings upgrade the country received from Moody's Investors Service which cited Tuesday increased reserves, lower external debt, and an improving trend in government debt ratios for its latest action.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was quoted around 8,380 rupiah, down from IDR8,410 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Indonesia Deputy Governor Bunbunan Hutapea said that aside from Moody's upgrade, the rupiah is also getting a boost from improving macroeconomic conditions such as falling inflation and the global weakness of the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In Manila, the peso shadowed its neighboring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rose to a one-month high against the dollar, closing at PHP54.78 despite the Moody's revision of its outlook to negative for the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating. The dollar closed at PHP54.87 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 1136 4250  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031001dza1000eu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
