\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 1  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,104 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 11:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Big Japanese manufacturers optimistic about business conditions outnumbered pessimists for the first time in nearly three years, a key Bank of Japan survey showed Wednesday, highlighting continued improvement in the country's business environment. But worrisome signs remain for Japan's corporate sector, especially a recent sharp strengthening in the yen that forced Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily overnight in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and which wasn't reflected for the most part in the BOJ's latest tankan survey. ("=WRAP: Tankan Shows Bullish Cos In Majority; Worries Loom" ran at 0320 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SENDAI, Japan: The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday defended \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with two of its most senior officials saying a stable yen is crucial for maintaining the country's economic recovery. (WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore is almost sure to keep policy unchanged at its policy review this month, maintaining a neutral bias in the value of the local dollar - the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's primary tool for monetary policy in the small trade-dominated economy. Although tempted to weaken the Singapore dollar to boost the economy, the MAS won't want to change policy after an effective easing in July. It will also be reluctant to attract the ire of the U.S. after the G7 recently urged Asian governments to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Singapore MAS Likely To Stay Neutral" ran at 0703 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: The Australian government has acknowledged it is flush with cash, but how best to spend it, either through income tax cuts or on social services, may prove more difficult than raking the money in. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Tax Dilemma For Cash-Flush Government" ran at 0529 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Despite a devastating typhoon and a surging won, South Korean exports reached record levels in September, adding weight to the government's view that sales of semiconductors, automobiles and other high-technology products overseas will speed up the country's long-awaited economic recovery. ("=WRAP: S Korea Sept Export Data Rekindle Recovery Hope" ran at 0846 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Government bond yields and interest-rate swaps fell sharply from Singapore to Seoul after U.S. Treasury prices surged overnight amid worrying signs about the prospects for the U.S. economic recovery. But while weaker-than-expected data on U.S. consumer confidence and manufacturing in the Chicago region lifted sentiment among bond bulls in Asia, a better outlook for the global economy and various isolated domestic factors may soon reverse the downward trend for regional interest rates. (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's August trade surplus narrowed to $2.25 billion from $2.71 billion in July due to weaker-than-expected exports, the central bureau of statistics said Wednesday. Meanwhile, inflation was softer in September as lower basic food prices put a brake on overall price rises, paving the way for more rate cuts as the authorities try to deliver economic growth of 4% this year. ("DATA SNAP: Indonesia August Trade Surplus Narrows" first take ran at 0719 GMT; "DATA SNAP: Indonesia Sep CPI Paves Way For More Rate Cuts" ran at 0724 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: India's biggest offshore debt repayment in recent memory - a $5.5 billion dollar bond redemption - had no adverse effects on the market Wednesday due to several measures taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support system liquidity. ("=WRAP: India's Fincl Mkts Unruffled By Big Debt Repayment" ran at 0902 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's LG Card plans to issue a total of one trillion won of bonds by April next year, said the company in a disclosure to the Financial Supervisory Service Wednesday. ("S Korea/LG Card/Bonds" ran at 0166 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai inflation, as measured by the consumer price index, decelerated in September due chiefly to lower oil prices, the Ministry of Commerce said Wednesday. ("DATA SNAP: Lower Oil Prices Pressure Thai Sep CPI" first take ran at 0745 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The "rapid appreciation" of the New Zealand dollar against the U.S. dollar is hurting export revenue, but the country's economy still seems to be rebounding from a slow second quarter, Prime Minister Helen Clark said Wednesday. ("NZ's PM/Rapid Appreciation" ran at 0115 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Australian Prime Minister John Howard said Wednesday there is an understanding within his Liberal Party that Treasurer Peter Costello will be its next leader. ("Australia PM Says Treasurer Costello Is Clear Successor" ran at 0137 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Azim Premji, chairman of Indian software giant Wipro, was making a presentation to a big foreign client at Wipro's headquarters earlier this year when the electricity supply failed - not once, but four times. The incident illustrated how India's creaky infrastructure - inadequate power supplies, potholed roads, congested ports - is still hindering an economic take-off. ("Infrastructure Deficit Limiting India's Potential" ran at 0511 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: International agency Fitch Ratings said Wednesday that the recent upgrading of Thailand's sovereign long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit rating to BBB from BBB- was in recognition of the country's impressive debt repayment since the 1997 crisis and surprisingly good fiscal performance in the last two years. ("Fitch/Thai" first take ran at 0721 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Prices of private homes in Singapore weakened further in the third quarter, slipping 0.4% on quarter, according to flash estimates from the Urban Redevelopment Authority Wednesday. ("Singapore/3Q Pvt Ppty Prices" ran at 0500 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar remained steady against the yen in Asia Wednesday, as a much-anticipated survey showing an improvement in Japanese business sentiment was overshadowed by concerns of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0549 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Shares of KT Corp., South Korea's largest fixed- line carrier, rose Wednesday after the company said it would trim 12.6% of its work force to save 330 billion won a year. The reduction is KT's biggest since the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis and addresses concerns about its high labor costs. ("=WRAP: KT Shares Rise On News Of 12.6% Cut In Work Force" ran at 0552 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: While the hunt for a major acquisition continues, Australian beer and wine producer Foster's Group Wednesday favored a share buyback if no suitable targets are found. ("REPEAT:WRAP: Foster's Talks Up Buyback If No M&A Deals" ran at 0638 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Casino owner Burswood said late Tuesday it looks forward to working with Publishing and Broadcasting Ltd. as a major shareholder. ("Australia's Burswood Says To Work With New Major Hldr PBL" ran at 2324 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's All Nippon Airways said Wednesday it has proposed a uniform 5% cut in salaries of all ground staff and flight attendants in an effort to slash costs and weather the tough business climate. ("ANA Proposes Cut In Union Workers' Salary From Next April" ran at 0226 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Ripplewood Holdings says four companies will invest in Japan Telecom Holdings's fixed-line business, which the U.S. investment fund agreed in August to acquire, but didn't disclose the size of the stakes each will take. ("Ripplewood -2:Follows Agreement To Acquire Fixed-Line Ops" ran at 0213 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Midsize contractor Morimoto said Wednesday it has filed for court-led rehabilitation with the Osaka district court, leaving behind liabilities totaling Y215.3 billion. ("=WRAP:Japan Midsize Contractor Morimoto Fails;Debts Y215B" ran at 0707 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: News Corp.'s annual meeting should be a pleasure for Rupert Murdoch when the 72-year-old media mogul breezes into the company's sedate home town of Adelaide, South Australia, later this month. Profits are up, the global economy is brightening and Murdoch is only months away from attaining long sought control of U.S. satellite giant DirecTV. But that's not to say shareholders won't raise now familiar gripes concerning corporate governance, low dividends and generous option packages to executives. ("=News Corp Meeting To Be Smooth Sailing For Murdoch" ran at 0556 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan memory chipmaker Powerchip Semiconductor Wednesday said its full-year 2004 revenue will more than double its 2003 forecast of NT$22.69 billion with increased production at its newest chip plant. But its shares fell 4% on the announcement on fears that increased production and oversupply might threaten the fledgling upswing in the dynamic random access memory (DRAM) chip sector. ("Taiwan Powerchip/Rev" ran at 0311 GMT; WRAP will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: LG Group, South Korea's second-largest conglomerate, will boost research and development spending for its electronic units by 17% on year to 2.1 trillion won in 2004. ("S Korea LG/R&D" first take ran at 0051 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Kookmin Bank, South Korea's largest lender, has set aside 500 billion won in extra provisions for its credit card \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the merger of Kookmin Credit Card with the bank's in-house card \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Chief Executive Kim Jung-Tae Wednesday. ("Kookmin Bk Sets Aside Extra KRW500B Reserves For Card Ops" ran at 0124 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Malaysian budget airline AirAsia is still in talks with Changi airport's management on flights to Singapore's main airport, and AirAsia's CEO Tony Fernandes says he's hopeful of a favorable outcome. (story will run around 1015 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Is the Stock Exchange of Thailand overheating? Thai shares have been on a tear, with the benchmark index up 63% this year and recently hitting a six-year high. But the party can only continue if authorities find a way to rein in speculation by a growing number of short-term investors without harming the confidence of long-term market participants. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Thai Bourse Braced For Speculation" ran at 0241 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: U.S. entertainment group Liberty Media has exercised its right under a March 2003 agreement to buy US$500 million of News Corp. preferred limited-voting ordinary American Depositary Receipts at US$21.50 apiece. ("Liberty Media To Acquire US$500M News Corp Preferred ADRs" ran at 0157 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan compact disc maker CMC Magnetics Corp. has sold US$140.9 million worth of global depositary receipts, the deal's sole bookrunner JPMorgan Chase said Wednesday. The GDRS were priced Tuesday at US$14.09 per unit, a discount of 6.7% to CMC's Tuesday closing price of NT$25.70 a share. Each GDR is equal to 20 common shares. ("Taiwan CMC Magnetics Sells US$140.9M GDRs; Discount 6.7%" first take ran at 0051 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: The Taiwan government's securities regulators late Tuesday announced five changes to rules governing foreign investment in Taiwan's equities markets, making investments by foreigners easier. The new rules come into effect Thursday. ("Taiwan Govt/Investors" first take ran at 0044 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 COLOMBO: Several foreign investment banks and fund managers say they would be keen to buy shares in Sri Lanka Telecom if the government sold more of its holding, a company official said Wednesday. ("=Foreigners Keen On Buying Sri Lanka Telecom Govt Stake" ran at 0602 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Handheld computer maker Palm Inc. is "fairly optimistic" that shareholders will vote for its bold plans to remake the company later this month, a top official who is in Singapore to launch new Palm products said Wednesday. ("Palm "Fairly Optimistic" Shareholders Will OK Plans" ran at 0618 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co. hasn't made any comments on its proposed capital expenditure for next year, a company spokesman said Wednesday. A local news report had said TSMC would spend more on new plants and equipment in 2004 than the US$1.25 billion it has targeted for this year. ("Taiwan Semicon Denies It Has Commented On 2004 Capex Plan 0427  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's Mines and Energy Minister said Wednesday he isn't optimistic that non-OPEC countries will follow the organization's move to cut crude oil output next month. ("Indonesia Min/Non-Opec" ran at 0155 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Strong gold and nickel prices are helping underpin a boom in shares of Australian mining and exploration companies. ("Australian Mining Share Boom Could Continue" ran at 0545 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031001dza1000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
