\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Dollar Firm Around 5.5-Year High; Bonds Surge  \b0\par\par\uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 09:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--After rallying to a fresh five-and-a-half-year high US$0.6870 in overnight trade, the Australian dollar consolidated in Asia Wednesday with ongoing uncertainty about the direction of the U.S. dollar the overriding concern for traders.  \par \par \uc2 Australian government bonds were also strong after U.S. Treasurys surged overnight on the back of weak U.S. consumer confidence and manufacturing data. Three-year bond futures achieved a six week high with late stop-loss buying fueling the move.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at US$0.6838, up from US$0.6802 late Tuesday in Sydney. Against the yen the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at Y75.97, compared with Y75.34 late Tuesday.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. dollar by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York trading overnight was the central focus of the market, with traders saying the Japanese authorities were seeking to clear up uncertainty about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the wake of the G7 meeting in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that they have decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in U.S. hours, through U.S. monetary authorities is also a relevant point in term of their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics. It sends a signal that they have not agreed to stand aside," said Alison Montgomery, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac.  \par \par \uc2 However, Montgomery said the market will wait a few days to see if the MOF's resolve remains strong. Tuesday was the halfway point for fiscal 2004 in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been partly aimed at short-term support for the market.  \par \par \uc2 "People will be quite keen to see what the MOF does if we look to be threatening the downside again. There are skeptics out there," Montgomery added.  \par \par \uc2 While the outlook for the U.S. dollar remains uncertain the Australian dollar will be a side issue for the market, with most traders expecting it to continue tracking moves by the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Sydney dealers also absorbed news of a strong Japanese Tankan report which showed the first positive reading for business sentiment in more than two and a half years. Dealers said it highlighted the looming challenge for the MOF in trying to keep the yen weak.  \par \par \uc2 "It highlights why the MOF has got their work cut out for them...Foreigners have been buying the Nikkei, if that continues it is a real flow that will continue to keep the yen supported," Montgomery said.  \par \par \uc2 Glenn Maguire, chief economist at SG Australia, said the Tankan report is confirmation that the Japanese business cycle is broadening.  \par \par \uc2 "Our suspicion remains that the strength and momentum of the cyclical upswing in Japan is being underestimated," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Local data has also supported the Australian dollar with strong retail sales and credit growth data for August this week fanning the view that official interest rates will be raised in early 2004.  \par \par \uc2 More positive data was added to the pile on Wednesday with the Australian Industry Group's performance of manufacturing index showing strength. The PMI rose 2 points to 53 in September.  \par \par \uc2 "The 2 point rise in the Australian PMI suggests growth in the non-farm economy should strengthen from here," said Stephen Walters, economist at JP Morgan.  \par \par \uc2 The policy-making board of the Reserve Bank meets on Tuesday and is expected to keep rates steady awaiting further confirmation that the U.S. economy is recovering.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of the weekend the focus of traders will remain offshore with critical U.S. non-farm payrolls data for September due on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The market is looking for a fall of 25,000 over the month with some estimates suggesting the fall could be as great as 60,000. The always volatile report is critical as the market looks for signs that recovery in the U.S. economy is starting to generate jobs growth.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. data flow is particularly important. If you were to get further disappointment of the payrolls data on Friday, you will see the U.S. dollar continue to weaken across the board," said Westpac's Montgomery.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    6.75% Nov. bond '06:    4.94%  vs. 5.08% \par    6.50% Jun. bond '13:    5.24%  vs. 5.39% \par    3-10 Yr Yield Spread    0.30   vs. 0.31 \par    10-Yr Spread To U.S.:   1.28   vs. 1.33 \par    SFE Dec 3Y Futures:    95.00   up  0.13 \par    SFE Dec 10Y Futures:   94.745  up  0.145 \par    3-10 Yr Futures Spread  0.26   vs. 0.27 \par    (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices supplied by Reuters.) \par    -By James Glynn; Dow Jones Newswires; 612-8235-2955; \par  james.glynn@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1054 22766 4109  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031001dza1000ae\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
