\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Markets Want To See Jobs  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 07:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Data Sink Shares; Carmakers Slash Jobs \par  \par DJIA          9275.06    loss 105.18    dn 1.1% \par NASDAQ        1786.94    loss 37.62     dn 2.1% \par S&P 500       995.97     loss 10.62     dn 1.1% \par Dow Future    9270.00    gain 12.00     up 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1308.50    gain 2.50      up 0.2% \par S&P Future    996.50     gain 2.50      up 0.3% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.96% down 0.13 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Disappointing reports on consumer confidence and business activity in the Midwest sent stocks into a downward slide Tuesday. Treasury prices rose.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Tuesday was the fourth down day for the market out of the last five.  \par \par \uc2 "Any negative news corporate or economic is just another excuse to take profits....We have had a dramatic move up in almost every sector of the market," said Brian Bush, director of equity research at Stephens Inc. in Little Rock, Ark.  \par \par \uc2 Investors have been increasingly uneasy about stock prices, fearing they've climbed too high too, too fast given the still fragile economic recovery. Wall Street interpreted Tuesday's reports as confirming its fears about the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't say there's skepticism, but concern about (stock) prices today....I think investors at the margin are saying, 'Maybe I should take some of those profits,'" said Jack Caffrey, equities strategist at J.P. Morgan Private Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Tuesday's economic news also contributed to the market's losses.  \par \par \uc2 The Conference Board reported that consumer confidence, which had rebounded in August, took a bigger than expected dive in September due to a sluggish job market. The group's Consumer Confidence Index fell to 76.8, a five-point decrease from the revised 81.7 registered in August. The reading was also well below the 80.5 economists had predicted.  \par \par \uc2 Soon after, the Purchasing Management Association of Chicago said its index of area business activity fell to 51.2 in September on a seasonally adjusted basis from 58.9 in August. Economists were expecting a much smaller decrease to 57.0. The Chicago index is considered a harbinger of the national Institute for Supply Management's index, to be released Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Ford plans to cut about 12,000 jobs world-wide, while Chrysler is readying plans to eliminate several thousand positions. Fierce competition from foreign rivals and the costs of the U.S. auto market's price war are likely to force all of Detroit's Big Three to shed thousands of jobs in coming years.  \par \par \uc2 The Securities and Exchange Commission is investigating accounting at Freddie Mac (FRE), but SEC Chairman William Donaldson declined to say whether it has found evidence of fraud at the mortgage giant.  \par \par \uc2 In political news, the FBI has begun a full-scale criminal investigation into whether White House officials illegally leaked the identity of an undercover CIA officer, and President George W. Bush ordered his staff to cooperate with the first major probe of his administration.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Prices of U.S. Treasurys rose sharply Tuesday, with the benchmark 10-year yield closing below the key psychological level of 4.00% for the first time since mid-July amid poor economic data.  \par \par \uc2 The economic reports confirmed the jobless nature of the present recovery in the economy and "suggest that economic growth in the third quarter will not be perpetuated into the fourth quarter," said Bill Chepolis, managing director at Deutsche Asset Management in New York whose firm has $151 billion of fixed-income assets in North America under management.  \par \par \uc2 The Chicago Purchasing Managers survey, a gauge of the region's manufacturing activity, resulted in a decline in the employment index to 45.3 from 51.2 in August.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tankan Survey Brightens Japan Econ Hopes \par  \par USD-Yen             111.20       loss  0.30    dn 0.3% \par AUD-USD             0.6830       gain  0.0015  up 0.2% \par Nikkei 225          10310.39     gain  91.34   up 0.9% \par JGB Yield           1.3950% up 0.0150 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks were gaining toward the close Wednesday after a key survey indicated Japanese business conditions have improved to their strongest levels in nearly three years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan survey, called the tankan, showed Japanese managers grew more upbeat about business conditions in the three months to September.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The survey's sentiment index for big manufacturers stood at plus one in September, the BOJ said, showing optimists outnumbered pessimists among large manufacturers for the first time in nearly three years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading, the dollar stayed above 111 yen, after the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets through its account at the New York Federal Reserve.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Finance Ministry official who declined to be named confirmed Wednesday that the ministry bought dollars for yen Tuesday during morning trading in New York. He declined to specify how much Japan spent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of Japanese government bonds lost steam as cautious participants focused on the 10-year auction Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan has traced the country's mad cow disease outbreak of two years ago to either Italian cattle feed or British cows, but is still unsure exactly how the disease spread to domestic herds, according to a government panel report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stock Losses May Be Limited \par  \par Euro-USD            1.1675      gain  0.0015  up 0.1% \par Stlg-USD            1.6630      gain  0.0015  up 0.1% \par USD-Franc           1.3170      loss  0.0018  dn 0.2% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European share prices are seen opening flat to lower and prices of government bonds slightly higher, while the euro looks to have stalled.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares are likely to open flat to lower but the stalled euro and higher Tokyo stocks should brake the decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London spreadbettor IG Index sees the FTSE up 4 points at 4095 and the CAC up 2 to 3137, but the DAX down 6 to 3250.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were lower Tuesday, as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility and concern over the global growth outlook provoked widespread profit-taking.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alan Castles, economist at Lehman Brothers, said the U.S. data represents a warning signal. "It throws the strength of U.S. recovery beyond the third quarter into doubt," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices should open little changed to slightly higher as stocks find direction and the euro struggles to continue its upturn.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone government bonds rose Tuesday as surprisingly weak U.S. economic data sent bond prices and the euro soaring, while stocks tumbled.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A German trader said he expects bond prices to continue rising over the coming days.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Apart from supportive technicals, weak stocks should be giving impulses to bonds," he said. "Plus, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a huge help right now."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European manufacturing figures from purchasing managers could determine the market's direction until the U.S. ISM figures at 1400 GMT.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro inched higher overnight, stopping just under $1.1700.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve System has other tools at its disposal beyond interest rates to stimulate the economy, said J. Alfred Broaddus, president of the Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The consensus forecast for a modest firming and acceleration of the U.S. economic recovery is quite plausible, and the biggest downside risk is the current weak job market, said Broaddus.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Wednesday, Oct 1: Manufacturing PMIs; Fed Speakers \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0200/0600 Germany August Retail Sales (seen -0.4% MM, 2.8% YY) \par 0245/0645 France  Sept Consumer Sentiment Survey (seen unch) \par 0330/0730 E.U.    European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Chief Economist Issing \par 0350/0750 France  September Purchasing Managers' Manufacturing \par 0355/0755 Germany September Purchasing Managers' Manufacturing \par 0430/0830 U.K.    September Purchasing Managers' Manufacturing \par 0600/1000 E.U.    August Unemployment Rate (seen 8.9%) \par 0600/1000 U.K.    British Index Distributive Trends \par 0700/1100 U.S.    MBA Refinancing Index (last -0.4%) \par 0800/1200 U.S.    Federal Reserve Governor Gramlich addresses \par 1000/1400 U.S.    August Construction Spending (seen +0.4%) \par 1000/1400 U.S.    September Challenger Layoffs (last -6%) \par 1000/1400 U.S.    Sept ISM Manufacturing Index (seen 54.5) \par 1800/2200 U.S.    Dallas Fed McTeer speaks on the economy \par    N/A    U.S.    ABC/Money Consumer Confidence (last -20)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Arla Foods (K.AFA), Nordzucker (G.NZ): E.U. antitrust decision \par  \par France : September New Vehicle Registrations \par  \par Dassault Aviation (F.DAV): 1H Earnings \par  \par DSM (N.DSM): News conference \par  \par Arcelor (L.ARC): Analyst Presentation \par  \par Banco Popular (E.BPE): To Pay 2003 EUR0.395 Dividend \par  \par De Vere (U.DVR): Trading update \par  \par Dickinson Legg Group (U.DKL): 1H Earnings \par  \par Fairplace Consulting (U.FPC): FY Earnings \par  \par Paramount (U.PMN): FY Earnings \par  \par Peel Holdings (U.PEE): AGM \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1011  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031001dza10005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
