\par\par \b \uc2 DOLLAR OUTLOOK:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Weighs In Vs Flexibility  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 20:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar will likely continue to be subjected to a tug of war between the forces of investment flows and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists are mixed over which side will win.  \par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven finance ministers' call last month for countries to adopt greater flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies opened the floodgates to dollar selling, as some investors interpreted the communique as an agreement to let the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decline to help correct global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 But Japanese monetary authorities proved Tuesday that they wouldn't tolerate the 6% jump in the value of the yen against the dollar over the past two weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the same day to protect the key Y110 level.  \par \par \uc2 That \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the New York session via its account at the New York Federal Reserve for the first time in over a year convinced even some yen bulls that additional downside moves in the dollar will probably be kept in check.  \par \par \uc2 "We have underestimated the resolve of the MOF to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Neil Parker, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Royal Bank of Scotland in London, who doesn't expect the dollar to break below Y110. "They're not putting their eggs in the same basket, and we expect repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb strength in the yen."  \par \par \uc2 Other strategists, however, aren't so sure that the MOF is drawing a new "line in the sand," and believe instead that it would try to smooth the yen's appreciation in the face of economic and political realities.  \par \par \uc2 Umberto Alvisi, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in London, said it's hard to say whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the MOF, would be successful in defending Y110, because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility and effectiveness were hurt when it didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during much of the past month as the dollar was being sold off.  \par \par \uc2 Still, he thinks the BOJ will ultimately be able to reverse downward pressure on the dollar so that a strong yen doesn't hurt Japan's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "We may see an overshooting in the near term, but medium term, we don't see Japanese authorities letting the yen appreciate too much because it could derail the tentative recovery in Japan," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Of course, the G7 communique was at least as much a function of U.S. domestic politics as it was an attempt to address global imbalances. The Bush administration is expected to keep up its rhetoric about flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to show it is doing something to stem U.S. job losses, and while that is aimed mostly at China, Japan may feel the pressure, as well.  \par \par \uc2 "There is going to be even more noise from the U.S. about Asia," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan in New York. She said to expect a "full-court press," noting that U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow testifies before Congress on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in mid-October, right around when President George W. Bush is visiting Japan.  \par \par \uc2 With China unlikely to yield anytime soon to pressure to loosen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg with the dollar, "other Asian countries will bear the brunt of this pressure," said Patterson, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely have slow - though not halt altogether - its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 However, Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York, said she believes the market overreacted to the G7 communique.  \par \par \uc2 She thinks the "rhetoric will be toned down," unless U.S. election politics dictate an implicit move to advocate a weaker dollar by the administration.  \par \par \uc2 Just how much pressure the dollar will have to bear will depend largely on the market's perception of the U.S. recovery, especially the weak job market. Soft data releases earlier this week triggered bouts of dollar selling, and economists are expecting Friday's payroll data for September to show a loss of 25,000 more jobs.  \par \par \uc2 Germain said she expects "positive economic releases to start to give the dollar a bid in the market," both against the euro and yen. She expects the dollar to recover to Y115 and $1.10 against the euro in a month, and to Y118 and $1.08 against the euro by year end.  \par \par \uc2 However, the acknowledged the risk that if the payroll numbers are bad, the euro could go "significantly higher" against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In general, other strategists expect the euro to remain on the sidelines of the dollar/yen show and to be rangebound around $1.15, though it could appreciate further if weak U.S. data adds to concern about the pace of the recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday afternoon, the dollar was trading around Y110.78, down from Y111.45 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $1.1686, up from $1.1664 Tuesday. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also at Y129.44 from Y130.03 late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in Tokyo, said that while the Japanese economy is recovering, recent weaker-than-expected U.S. economic data is implying that the U.S. economic rebound may be slowing down.  \par \par \uc2 "The trend for dollar weakness and yen strength will continue," he said, predicting that the dollar would slide to Y106 by the end of the month before recovering to Y110 by year end.  \par \par \uc2 However, some strategists believe it's the Japanese economy that could falter, which would relieve some pressure on the dollar by itself if foreigners curtail their rampant buying of Japanese equities.  \par \par \uc2 Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities in Tokyo, said that the dollar would probably continue to decline at first, but he expects it to rebound toward Y113 in late October as foreign investment in Japanese stocks peak.  \par \par \uc2 "I expect further yen weakness in three months," he said, pointing to such potential factors as weakness in Japan's economy and a worsening in Japan's huge fiscal deficit.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Table of One-Month and Three-month Forecasts \par  \par                          End Oct '03   End Dec '03 \par  \par    Neil Parker, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Royal Bank of Scotland, London: \par  \par    euro/dollar            $1.15         $1.14 \par    dollar/yen             Y112          Y110 \par    euro/yen               Y129          Y125.5 \par  \par    Umberto Alvisi, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Credit Suisse First Boston, London: \par  \par    euro/dollar            $1.164        $1.15 \par    dollar/yen             Y110.8        Y112 \par    euro/yen               Y129          Y128.8 \par  \par    Rebecca Patterson, global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, JPMorgan, New York \par  \par    euro/dollar            $1.15         $1.17 \par    dollar/yen             Y110          Y107 \par    euro/yen               Y126.5        Y125.2 \par  \par     Lauren Germain, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Banc of America Securities, New York: \par  \par    euro/dollar             $1.10        $1.08 \par    dollar/yen              Y115         Y118 \par    euro/yen                Y126.5       Y127.4 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Koji Fukaya, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst, Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi, Tokyo  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    euro/dollar             $1.19        $1.22 \par    dollar/yen              Y106         Y110 \par    euro/yen                Y126         Y134 \par  \par    Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist, Mizuho Securities, Tokyo \par  \par    euro/dollar             $1.13-$1.17  $1.13-$1.17 \par    dollar/yen              Y108-Y113    Y115 \par    euro/yen                Y122-Y132    Y130-Y135 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Adriana Arai in London and Ron Harui in Tokyo contributed to this story.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1070  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031001dza10005d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
