\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Lifted off Lows on Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par 22:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- After a brief hiatus, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government returned to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with a force Tuesday, helping to drag the dollar back from fresh three-year lows against the yen and stopping the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too rapidly.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  - apparently buying dollars for yen - during the New York morning via its account at the New York Federal Reserve Bank, in the process sending the dollar soaring from Y110.08, its lowest level since November 2000, toward Y111.80.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the finance ministry, is also widely believed to have bought as much as $2 billion for yen earlier Tuesday in Tokyo to defend the key 110 yen level.  \par \par \uc2 Taken together with a surprise report from the finance ministry, showing it had spent a record 4.457 trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in September, the actions effectively confirmed market suspicions that Japan would continue to seek to counter yen strength despite the recent call by Group of Seven finance ministers for more flexible and marked-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.  \par \par \uc2 Jason Bonanca, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Credit Suisse First Boston, said Japan's return to the markets "communicates a pretty clear intention" to defend the 110 yen level, by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in a day and choosing to act via the Federal Reserve. It was the first time \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via its account at the New York Fed since June 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday, the dollar was quoted at 111.45 yen, off its session high of 112.06 yen just after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo but much stronger than 110.85 yen late Monday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover from three-month lows against its other major counterparts, including the euro, pound and Swiss franc, although it still ended New York trading somewhat weaker from a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Late in the day, the euro was trading at $1.1664, up from $1.1598 late Monday in New York. Against the yen, the euro was quoted at 130.02 yen, higher than 128.56 yen Monday. The dollar was at 1.3219 franc, down from 1.3267 franc, while sterling was lower at $1.6625, from $1.6666 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 While Japan's government hadn't been seen acting overtly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the G7 communique on Sept. 20, there was speculation it would try to protect the 110 yen `line in the sand' ahead of the end of the Japanese fiscal first half Tuesday. A strong yen hurts the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  value of revenues booked by Japanese companies in overseas markets.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had stabilized going into the New York trading session, but it came under pressure again after the Conference Board's consumer confidence index unexpectedly fell to 76.8 in September, its lowest level since March.  \par \par \uc2 That ensuing sell-off prompted Japan's finance ministry to respond for the second time in the global trading day.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far hadn't caused the dollar bears enough pain," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York. He said that's likely why Japan has now decided to up the ante.  \par \par \uc2 Several market participants said Japanese authorities could step in again in overnight trading Wednesday ahead of its quarterly tankan business sentiment survey, which is expected to show improvement. But the impact of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be short-lived.  \par \par \uc2 While short-term, or speculative investors, probably got burned by the latest action, medium-term investors will see current levels as a "good place to sell" dollars, said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at a Japanese bank in London, adding "Remember, there's only one buyer."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, with Japanese assets in strong demand and less public tolerance for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the G7 meeting, market participants disagree as to whether Japan is drawing a new threshold to defend at 110 yen, or even whether such a strategy could succeed.  \par \par \uc2 Several \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts think Japan's dramatic return to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market might be aimed more at slowing the rapid acceleration of the yen, than turning it around or halting it altogether.  \par \par \uc2 Marcel Kasumovich, G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in New York, said Japan's policy has changed from earlier in the year, when the finance ministry was trying to be proactive in creating economic conditions conducive to an export-led recovery. Now, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems "much more defensive."  \par \par \uc2 Yet Kasumovich said he agrees that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Fed is a tactical shift that adds some uncertainty into a market that has been intent on pushing the yen stronger.  \par \par \uc2 "If their objective is to create confusion, they've done a wonderful job," he said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this story.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrected September 30, 2003 19:13 ET (23:13 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1865 20714 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020030930dz9u00011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
