\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Slightly Dn Late; Mkt Focus On Yen Moves  \b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par 10:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was marginally weaker late Tuesday after market participants bought U.S. dollars amid suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against the yen early in the session.  \par \par \uc2 At 0835 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7290, up from S$1.7275 late Monday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7280 and S$1.7300 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar inched up to S$1.7300 early in the session but retreated around midmorning to around S$1.7280-90 after some market participants took profit.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in that range the rest of the day amid exceptionally thin interest in the local market, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Market attention was focused on the yen, which tends to set the direction for most other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Singapore dollar. Speculation that the BOJ had sold the yen to support the U.S. dollar also put pressure on the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But the effect of the BOJ's early morning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was short-lived, as Japanese exporters and other market players rushed to sell on the dollar's spike, erasing most of its gains by midday.  \par \par \uc2 Late in Asia, the U.S. dollar was trading at Y110.45, well off its session high of Y112.06.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers in Singapore said there were hardly any Singapore dollar trades being done, partly because many players didn't want to hold large positions ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's policy review due around Oct. 10.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore dealers say they expect the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue trading on a slightly weaker note over the next few sessions in the lead up to the MAS policy statement, though this would still depend on whether the yen makes any further gains.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar is expected to stay between S$1.7280 and S$1.7350 in overnight trading.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds ended lower Tuesday tracking an overnight retreat in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Stronger local stocks for most of the day on U.S. equity gains overnight also did little to relieve the pressure amid a quiet Treasurys session in Asia, dealers added.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the session, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds was up two basis points at 1.14%. Longer bond yields also ended higher, with the 10-year yield rising four basis points to 3.54% and the 15-year yield gaining three basis points to 3.98%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps moved generally in tandem with bond yields. The two-year offer inched up a point to 1.38% while the five-year was up two basis points to 2.54%. The 10-year offer was four basis points higher at 3.68%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps widened to 230 basis points from 227 basis points Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In the local money market, rates were steady with the overnight offer at 0.5000%, unchanged from late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Izham Ahmad,