\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Wall Street Roars Back  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Just Window-Dressing Or Real Buying? \par  \par DJIA          9380.24    gain 67.16     up 0.7% \par NASDAQ        1824.56    gain 32.49     up 1.8% \par S&P 500       1006.58    gain 9.73      up 1.0% \par Dow Future    9345.00    gain 3.00         0.0% \par NASDAQ Future 1334.50    loss 1.40         0.0% \par S&P Future    1004.00    loss 0.25         0.0% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.09% up 0.08 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Wall Street rebounded Monday after three straight losing sessions as institutional investors did some end-of-the-quarter buying to dress up their portfolios. Technology stocks, hardest hit in last week's big drop, showed the biggest gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Upbeat news about consumer spending and an encouraging report on semiconductor sales sent some consumer cyclical stocks higher, but otherwise had little effect on trading, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "It is certainly nothing to knock the market off its heels," said Chris Johnson, manager of quantitative analysis at Schaeffer's Investment Research in Cincinnati.  \par \par \uc2 Wall Street had encouraging economic news from the Commerce Department, which said consumers increased their spending by a strong 0.8% in August. The rise, attributable to consumers having extra cash in their wallets from tax cuts, met economists' expectations.  \par \par \uc2 Chip makers Intel Corp. advanced $1.10 to $28.37 and Advanced Micro Devices Inc. rose 33 cents to $11.20. The catalyst was a report from the Semiconductor Industry Association, which said worldwide chip sales grew by 4% in August.  \par \par \uc2 Sun Microsystems Inc. signaled Monday that it is facing another troubled quarter and projected a large loss for its fiscal first period. The computer maker said that for the three months ending last Sunday it would lose between 7 cents and 10 cents a share. Analysts had been anticipating a loss of 2 cents a share on revenue of $2.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Microsoft has told European regulators that it wants a hearing to defend itself against charges of monopoly abuse of power.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Prices of Treasurys fell Monday as a recovery in stocks caused some market participants to rethink their appetite for government securities.  \par \par \uc2 Heavy-duty data later in the week, including the September employment report due out Friday, and month-end and quarter-end considerations, also kept many investors on the sidelines, making for subdued trading, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's mostly back-and-forth trading, correcting the recent bull move we've had," said Jim Caron, bond market strategist at Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Stocks Up; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rescues Dollar \par  \par USD-Yen             110.98       gain  0.20    up 0.2% \par AUD-USD             0.6804       gain  0.0021  up 0.3% \par Nikkei 225          10354.89     gain  126.32  up 1.2% \par Hang Seng           11308.87     gain  167.59  up 1.5% \par JGB Yield           1.4100% down 0.0150 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)-- Tokyo stocks were rising toward Tuesday's close as investors bought on Wall Street's comeback. Investors were also reassured by signs the Japanese government is ready to step in to stem the yen's rise. In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading, the dollar rose briefly above the psychologically key 112-yen level, which dealers attributed to moderate, yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market sentiment was also boosted by the government's announcement Tuesday morning that Japan's unemployment rate had eased to 5.1% in August from 5.3%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Shares Set To Gain, Even With Higher Euro \par  \par Euro-USD            1.1629      gain  0.0035  up 0.3% \par Stlg-USD            1.6692      gain  0.0024  up 0.1% \par USD-Franc           1.3231      loss  0.0027  dn 0.2% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares are set to open higher, with prices of government bonds slightly lower. The euro starts higher against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European share prices are expected to open higher after solid gains on Wall Street and in Tokyo. The lower dollar may weigh on some multinational shares, so plays on companies that primarily deal in the euro-zone might be more appealing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London spreadbettor IG Index is calling the FTSE 100 up 18 at the open to 4160, the DAX up 2 at 3325, and the CAC up 4 at 3192.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the news, Air France's board approved plans to acquire Dutch airline KLM. The union would save the carriers roughly half a billion dollars and would create one of the world's largest aviation partnerships.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German software maker SAP AG (SAP) said that price competition was a bigger factor in its losing deals than ever before. Chief Executive Henning Kagermann said that discounting by rival Oracle Corp. (ORCL) was largely responsible for the price pressure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CSFB's ex-general counsel will be the government's star witness in its case against Frank Quattrone.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Roche subsidiary Genentech filed an application with the FDA for Avastin as a treatment for colorectal cancer in combination with chemotherapy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are set to lose some ground with stocks again on the rise. But the higher euro should cushion the impact. Murky prospects for lasting economic recovery also should support the market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government bond prices fell Monday, but recovered from intraday lows as stock markets slipped into the red and the euro rallied.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro continued to add to gains and is set to open European trading at around $1.1625.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This follows its dramatic rise on Monday against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's weakness derived mainly from heavy selling against the euro and Swiss franc, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury Secretary John Snow's chief spokesman came out later in the day to reiterate that U.S. policy on the dollar remains unchanged. "We are not going to respond to every goofy market rumor on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Treasury spokesman Rob Nichols told reporters. "Of course, there is no change in policy."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, even pure speculation about this was pounced on by a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market that is in any case eager to sell the dollar. "I think this is a technical, order-driven move looking for a reason," said Patrick Brodie, chief dealer at Sumitomo Bank in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Around the same time, a Swiss bank sold a large amount of dollars - over $1 billion, said dealers - against the Swiss franc, which broke trendline support at CHF1.3260-70.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Tuesday, Sep 30: EU Business, Consumer Sentiment \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0240/0640 France       September Business Sentiment Survey \par 0245/0645 France       August New Construction \par 0245/0645 France       August Unemployment Rate (seen 9.7%) \par 0250/0650 France       Second-Quarter Gross Domestic Product \par 0300/0700 Italy        Trade Data \par 0415/0815 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allocates bids \par 0430/0830 U.K.         Revised 2Q GDP; 2Q Balance of Payments \par 0515/0915 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allocates bids \par 0600/1000 E.U.         August-September Business Confidence \par 0600/1000 E.U.         August-September Consumer Confidence \par 0600/1000 E.U.         Sept Flash Inflation Rate (seen 2.1%) \par 0745/1145 U.S.         BTM-UBS Retail Sales Index \par 0855/1255 U.S.         Redbook Retail Sales Index \par 1000/1400 U.S.         Sept Chicago Purchasing Managers' Index \par 1000/1400 U.S.         Sept Consumer Confidence Index   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrected September 30, 2003 01:45 ET (05:45 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Areva (F.ARV): 1H Earnings \par Note: The company said in Aug 1H revenues rose 3.9% to EUR1.14B from EUR3.98B a year earlier. \par  \par Mean Fiddler Music Grp (U.MEF): 1H Earnings \par Note: The company said it reported pretax loss of GBP3.3M against GBP4.4M loss last year. Sales rose to GBP14.6M from GBP13.4M a year ago. \par  \par Envesta (U.EVS): FY Earnings \par Note: The company said in Jul it sees FY revenue in excess of GBP10.8M \par  \par Civilian Content (U.CVI): 1H Earnings \par Note: The company said in Sep it sees 1H losses reduced compared to a year ago. \par  \par Mr Bricolage (F.MRB): 1H Earnings \par  \par Benchmark (U.BMK): FY Earnings \par  \par Canterbury Foods Group (U.CFO): 1H Earnings \par  \par Clinton Cards (U.CCA): 1H Earnings \par  \par James Halstead (U.JHL): FY Earnings \par  \par MacLellan Group (U.MLG): 1H Earnings \par  \par Manchester United (U.MUN): FY Earnings \par Note: First results without departed CEO Peter Kenyon seen tak