\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japan Draws The Yen Line Then Draws It Again  \b0\par\par\uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par 20:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    By Tom Barkley and Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par  \par NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Japan sent a strong message to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets \par Tuesday that it won't tolerate the recent sharp rise in the yen. \par  \par In an apparent effort to draw a new "line in the sand," the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during Tokyo trading hours to \par keep the dollar from falling towards the key Y110 level, sending it briefly \par back up above Y112. \par  \par When that action failed to stem the selling, Japan redrew that line, buying \par dollars for yen in North American trading via the New York Federal Reserve \par Bank as the dollar fell to Y110.08, its lowest level since November 2000. The \par move, the first time \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  via its account at the New York Fed \par since June 2002, sent the dollar back up to around Y111.80, and it recently \par traded at Y111.51. \par  \par Jason Bonanca, vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Credit Suisse First \par Boston, said Japan's action "communicates a pretty clear intention" to defend \par the Y110 level "by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  twice in one day" and by going through the Fed. \par Steven Englander, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York, \par said he believes there is a new line in the sand, possibly at Y110. "I'm \par pretty sure Japan doesn't want to see the dollar down at Y105," he added. \par  \par Japan's motives are clear: Since the G7 meeting in Dubai on Sept. 20, the yen \par has appreciated by around 3.5% against the dollar. Take into account the heavy \par dollar selling in the week leading into the meeting, and the yen's gains stand \par at around 6%. \par  \par A strong yen makes Japanese exporters' goods more expensive on international \par markets, which hampers the country's nascent economic recovery. It helps keep \par Japan mired in the deflationary spiral it's been struggling to free itself \par from for five years. And just when domestic demand needs to grow, it \par encourages further saving. \par  \par However, the winds of change are blowing in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, with \par Japanese assets in strong demand and the Group of Seven calling for more \par flexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies. This leaves uncertainty as to whether the Y110 \par line is really the pain threshold for Japan, or even if such a defense of that \par level could hold. \par  \par     Smoothing Things Out \par  \par Against this backdrop, several \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts think Japan's dramatic return \par to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Tuesday might be aimed more at slowing the \par rapid acceleration of the yen, than turning it around or halting it \par altogether. \par  \par That view is also reflected in comments by Japanese authorities, with Finance \par Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki calling recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements "sharp," and \par saying that if exchange rates don't "stably reflect (economic) fundamentals" \par then "we will act appropriately." \par  \par Marcel Kasumovich, G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in New \par York, said he reckons the "message they're trying to send is mostly one of \par speed rather than direction." He said Japan's policy has changed from earlier \par in the year, when the finance ministry was trying to be proactive in creating \par economic conditions conducive to an export-led recovery. Now, the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seem "much more defensive." \par  \par A similar shift occurred in 1999, when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily to keep \par the yen from appreciating and choking off a fragile recovery. The Bank of \par Japan, the government's agent in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, sold yen at least 18 \par times by the spring of 2000, but its strategy turned into more of a smoothing \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the dollar eventually weakened to Y102 by April. \par  \par Yet Kasumovich said he agrees that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through the Fed is a tactical \par shift that adds some uncertainty, adding, "If their objective is to create \par confusion, they've done a wonderful job." \par  \par       Old Habits Die Hard \par  \par The one thing that is clear is that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't abandoned its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par policy, not after buying a record Y4.457 trillion last month, despite the lead \par up to the G7 meeting. \par  \par Several market participants said the BOJ could step in Wednesday ahead of its \par quarterly tankan business sentiment survey. But the impact of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par is likely to be short-lived. \par  \par While short-term, or speculative investors probably got burned by the latest \par action, medium-term investors will see current levels as a "good place to \par sell" dollars, said the head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales at a Japanese bank in \par London, adding "Remember, there's only one buyer." \par  \par Reinforcing this view, another strategist at a large U.S. bank in New York \par immediately shouted "buy yen" (and sell dollars) in the trading room when it \par became clear \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market, the person said. \par (Tom Barkley covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York. Jamie McGeever \par covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, and has also reported on \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro, London and Madrid). \par  \par  -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par  -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; \par jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par Copyright 2003 OsterDowJones Commodity News (ODJ). All rights reserved. \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020030930dz9u000p0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
