\par\par \b \uc2 AWSJ: Reports Show Japanese Econ At Highest Level In Yrs  \b0\par\par\uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par 16:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Sebastian Moffett \par    Of THE ASIAN WALL STREET JOURNAL \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Despite a strengthening yen and the damping effects of poor weather, recent indicators show Japan's economy is at its healthiest level in three years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Unemployment fell to 5.1% in August, from 5.3% in July, to reach the lowest level since mid-2001, the government said Tuesday. Industrial production fell 0.5% as a cool summer reduced the usual seasonal demand for such things as cold drinks and air conditioners. But manufacturers were expecting to increase production by 2.7% in September and 1.5% in October.  \par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com.)  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday in Japan, the Bank of Japan's much-watched tankan survey of business confidence might show that more large businesses say conditions are good than say they are bad, for the first time since December 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Japan, long derided among the world's advanced economies for its inability to pull out of its funk, is on something of a roll.  \par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product, or the total value of its economic output, rose by a full 1% in April-June from the previous quarter - an annualized rate of 3.9% - putting Japanese growth ahead of both the U.S. and Europe. To be sure, some of that growth came from exceptional factors; for example, Japanese traveled abroad less than usual because of fears of SARS and the Iraq war, meaning they spent more money at home.  \par \par \uc2 What's more, many economists say the economy likely contracted in the July-September quarter just ended. Still, the underlying trend is for healthy growth of an annualized rate of about 2%, says economist Shuji Shirota of Dresdner Kleinwort Benson. "You have to think of the two quarters together."  \par \par \uc2 Taking this into account, the government last Friday revised its estimate for GDP growth in the fiscal year ending in March 2004 to 2.1% in price-adjusted terms, from a forecast made in January of a 0.6% expansion. It also predicted that growth in nominal terms, before adjustment for deflation, would be 0.1% - the first gain since 2000.  \par \par \uc2 In an economy that has for the past decade or more consistently disappointed expectations of a revival, the question is, can the recovery last? Japan's growth spurt could be derailed by a number of things. Most pressing is the soaring yen. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to about 110.64 on Tuesday, sparking apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 A higher yen erodes the value of exporters' overseas revenue when calculated back into yen. That's a problem, because exports have long been Japan's most robust source of growth. The Japanese government has been aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to hold the yen below a level of 115 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Between Jan. 1 and Sept. 26 - updated Tuesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  but not including that day's apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 13.5 trillion yen ($120 billion) in an effort to curb the yen's appreciation. The selling by July had already exceeded the previous full-year high for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 7.64 trillion yen set in 1999.  \par \par \uc2 Before Tuesday's apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the call on Sept. 20 by the Group of Seven industrialized nations for more-flexible exchange rates, and the yen climbed to near three-year highs. On Tuesday, the Bank of Japan was thought to have resumed selling yen, pushing its level down to a trough of 112.06 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen is certainly a potential problem," says HSBC Securities economist Peter Morgan. Economists say the government can no longer hope to keep the yen below, or stronger than, 115 yen to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Longer term, a plethora of problems still act like a sea anchor on Japan's economy.  \par \par \uc2 The nation's formerly sky-high land prices are still falling: the latest survey by the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport showed nationwide residential land prices fell an average 4.8% this year through July 1 from a year earlier, while commercial land prices slipped 7.4%.  \par \par \uc2 Deflation has probably eased a little, but is still entrenched. Core consumer prices, which have been declining for nearly four years, dipped 0.1% in August from a year earlier, a smaller fall than the 0.2% drop in July, and the 0.4% tumbles in the previous three months. But the GDP deflator, considered the broadest measure of price changes in an economy because it includes producer prices too, plunged 2.5% from the previous year in April-June, the latest quarter for which it is available.  \par \par \uc2 However, the American economy will likely continue to improve as the Bush administration prepares for the 2004 presidential election, and this will help boost Japanese exports to the U.S., economists say. Morgan of HSBC forecasts real growth of 2.9% in Japanese GDP for the current calendar year, slipping to 2.2% in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 -By Sebastian Moffett, The Asian Wall Street Journal; 81-3-3241-2676  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020030930dz9u0006g\par }\page {\par