\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-U.S. stocks sink on Sun view, data - bonds rally.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jan Paschal  \par \uc2 971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 00:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 30 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks dropped on Tuesday after Wall Street got a double whammy - a warning from technology bellwether Sun Microsystems about a bigger-than-expected loss and weak economic data - while those same numbers fed a bond rally.  \par \par \uc2 The three major stock indexes slid in September, breaking a string of monthly gains. But they ended the third quarter higher.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed against the yen after the Bank of Japan sold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bought dollars, in hopes of keeping the cost of Japan's exports from rising.  \par \par \uc2 In New York, gold futures prices rose as investors sought a safe haven from worries about the U.S. economy's recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Oil futures jumped almost 3 percent, still getting a lift from OPEC's output-cut announcement last week.  \par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the day's economic numbers added up to bad news: The number of consumers saying jobs were still hard to come by hit a 10-year high of 35.3 percent, according to The Conference Board's September consumer sentiment survey. The consumer sentiment index showed a surprising slide to 76.8 in September from a revised 81.7 in August, and confounding forecasts for an increase to 81.8.  \par \par \uc2 A separate report on business activity from the Chicago Purchasing Managers showed a sharp drop in September to 51.2 from 58.9 in August. Economists had expected a dip to 57.0.  \par \par \uc2 "The data did spook people," said Henry Herrmann, chief investment officer at Waddell & Reed.  \par \par \uc2 Sun Microsystems Inc., a network computer maker, warned its fiscal first-quarter loss would be bigger than expected - seen as a bad omen for the earnings season just ahead. Sun's stock fell more than 14 percent, dropping 55 cents to $3.31, and ranked among Nasdaq's most actives.  \par \par \uc2 The technology-laced Nasdaq Composite Index dropped 37.62 points, or 2.06 percent, to 1,786.94, based on the latest available data.  \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average sank 105.18 points, or 1.12 percent, to 9,275.06. The broader Standard & Poor's 500 Index shed 10.61 points, or 1.05 percent, to 995.97.  \par \par \uc2 For the month of September, the Nasdaq fell roughly 1.3 percent, snapping a 7-month streak of gains. The S&P 500 dropped 1.2 percent and the Dow fell about 1.5 percent for the month. Both broke a 6-month string of gains.  \par \par \uc2 For the quarter, the S&P 500 rose 2.2 percent, the Dow gained 3.2 percent and the Nasdaq Composite climbed 10.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 BOND BUYING SPREE  \par \par \uc2 Buyers snatched up Treasury bonds, driving the price of the 30-year bond up almost 2 full points on the belief that the country's worsening job picture meant the Federal Reserve could afford to take its time before raising interest rates again.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year note, which moves in the opposite direction of its price, fell to 3.94 percent, its lowest level since mid-July.  \par \par \uc2 "If you get a dramatic weakening in payrolls on Friday and a rise in the unemployment rate, you're going to be hearing a lot more talk about a Fed cut," said Bill Chepolis, head of the rates desk at Deutsche Asset Management. The Labor Department is set to release September's unemployment data on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year Treasury note, the most sensitive to the perception of the direction of official interest rates, rose 9/32 to a price of 100-10/32, while its yield fell to 1.46 percent from 1.61 percent on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year note leaped 1-5/32 to a price of 102-17/32, while its yield skidded to 3.94 percent from 4.08 percent late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond surged 1-30/32 to a price of 107-11/32, while its yield dropped to 4.89 percent from 5.01 percent on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR GETS A BOJ LIFT  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended the day higher against the yen, thanks to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell yen and buy dollars after the dollar hit a 3-year low against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's slump in response to the weak U.S. economic data may have forced the Bank of Japan's hand, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 In late U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the dollar was quoted at 111.45 yen, up 0.58 percent from 110.81 yen at Monday's close in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to $1.1659, up 0.59 percent from $1.1591 late Monday, as the disappointing U.S. data took its toll on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In the commodity markets, COMEX gold rallied up to near last week's 7-year high after the economic data prompted investors to dump stocks and the dollar in favor of gold.  \par \par \uc2 COMEX December gold jumped $2.90 to settle at $386.10 an ounce.  \par \par \uc2 On the New York Mercantile Exchange, U.S. light crude for November delivery rose 80 cents to settle at $29.20 a barrel, off its session high of $29.38, its highest price since Sept. 11. OPEC's decision last week to cut output, effective Nov. 1, continued to drive oil futures prices higher, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Overseas, the FTSE Eurotop 300 index fell 1.75 percent to 861.73 points after the weak dollar hurt DaimlerChrysler and Volkswagen.  \par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the benchmark Nikkei average slid to a 6-week closing low at 10,219.05, down 0.10 percent, after investors sold shares of Toyota and other auto makers in the face of yen resilience.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030930dz9u0033p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
