\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar slide boosts euro bonds, gold - stocks slip.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nigel Stephenson  \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par 12:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 30 (Reuters) - A big fall in the dollar on Tuesday boosted euro zone government debt and gold and hit Asian stocks as investors awaited key consumer confidence data for clues to the strength of recovery in the world-leading U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slid to a three-year low against the yen, prompting talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength 10 days after Group of Seven ministers called for more flexibility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Some Tokyo traders suspected the Bank of Japan had been selling yen in both the Asian and European sessions but Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined to comment on whether Japanese authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in the Asian session.  \par \par \uc2 Against the euro, the greenback fell to its lowest since June, boosting the value of euro-denominated bonds as investors reassessed the chances of a cut in European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates. The strong euro made gold a more attractive investment for European investors.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was last worth 110.27 yen and the euro was at $1.1687.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's strength helped push Japan's Nikkei stock index to a six-week low while European shares edged lower.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts struggled to explain the dollar's fall, which began on Monday, citing hedge fund activity and the breaking of key chart levels.  \par \par \uc2 "The exact trigger yesterday is difficult to pin down but the market is now very short dollars going into important key U.S. data so more volatility is likely," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. consumer confidence data is due at 1400 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The G7's call for greater flexibility in exchange rates to iron out global imbalances, which was interpreted as criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also hit the dollar and dollar assets last week.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average slid to a six-week low as yen strength led investors to pull back from Toyota and other automakers. However, the broader TOPIX index rose on a better outlook for banks and optimism ahead of Wednesday's release of the "tankan" business sentiment survey.  \par \par \uc2 "With the yen having returned to 110 yen...it's now not such a great atmosphere for buying high techs and the like," said Ken Masuda, a senior dealer at Masudo Securities.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei closed down 0.1 percent but is still up 28 percent in the six months since the financial year began. The TOPIX index ended up 0.79 percent.  \par \par \uc2 European stocks slipped, having initially bounced off a seven-week low, as Air France and KLM revealed a long-awaited merger deal.  \par \par \uc2 But sentiment remained cautious on the final day of the third quarter, ahead of U.S. reports on consumer confidence and business activity.  \par \par \uc2 The FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips was down 0.09 percent while the narrower DJ Euro STOXX 50 index was off 0.21 percent.  \par \par \uc2 December Euribor futures were last at 97.955, off a high of 97.965, and pricing in three-month rates of 2.045 percent by year-end.  \par \par \uc2 "The strong euro means the market is pricing in some probability of a rate cut next year," said a London money market trader. "But we have to be cautious. A strong euro does not mean the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut rates and it has said a cut is unlikely.  \par \par \uc2 The interest rate-sensitive two-year Schatz note yield, which moves inversely to price, was down 3.2 basis points at 2.31 percent and the benchmark 10-year German Bund was yielding 4.03 percent, down 1.8 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 Gold ticked higher in Europe as the stronger euro made bullion a cheaper investment for European investors. Spot gold was last at $384.30/385 an ounce, compared with $381.90/382.60 at Monday's New York close.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks had risen on Monday, led by technology companies, as investors scooped up stocks that suffered in a selloff last week.  \par \par \uc2 The tech-laced Nasdaq index closed up 1.81 percent and the blue-chip Dow Jones Industrial average ended 0.72 percent higher.  \par \par \uc2 However, U.S. stock index futures were lower in Europe, indicating Wall Street would open lower.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030930dz9u0017v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
