\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar briefly jumps vs yen, dealers cite BOJ.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The dollar bounced after hitting three-year lows against the yen on Tuesday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, but it quickly shed gains amid worries about the strength of a U.S. economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar suddenly jumped to around 112 yen after hitting a trough of 110.65 yen early in the day, its lowest since December 2000. But it soon gravitated back below 111 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined to comment on whether the Japanese authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the dollar's fall but traders were convinced Tokyo had acted after warning for weeks that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary.  \par \par \uc2 Yet, traders said a single round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not enough to reverse the yen's uptrend, particularly as the dollar weakened broadly, hitting three-month lows against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation was growing that the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" corporate survey on Wednesday would show improving business conditions and reignite global interest in Japanese stocks, keeping the yen buoyant.  \par \par \uc2 "It looks as if it (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) was just a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which means these actions by the authorities may not have the same sort of impact they once did," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley Japan, agreed.  \par \par \uc2 "The market theme now is yen strength and dollar weakness," he said. "So, I don't think Japan will be able to change this tide no matter how much they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 With most Japanese companies closing their books for the first half of the business year on Tuesday, various position adjustments and hedging activities for the second half added to the weight on the dollar, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0545 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 110.92/97 yen a touch above Monday's late New York levels and only a quarter yen above the three-year lows set earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also hovered near three-month highs around $1.1640 rebounding swiftly after briefly pulling back in sympathy with the yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  JITTERS  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities worry that an over-strong yen could hurt Japan's export-led economic recovery and drag down stock prices, which are key to the health of the banking system.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the authorities had reasons to seek a higher dollar/yen exchange rate on Tuesday due to the half-year book-closings.  \par \par \uc2 "It is likely there was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push down the yen for the half-year book closing, as it affects companies' earnings reports," said a trader at a foreign bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei stock average rose sharply in the morning, taking heart from the apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but shed its gains to end 0.10 percent lower on the day. Still, it rose 28 percent during the fiscal first half.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent 9 trillion yen ($81 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first seven months of the year, keeping the dollar above 115 yen until early September.  \par \par \uc2 But a Group of Seven statement on September 20 calling for flexibility in exchange rates and the apparent absence of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then had emboldened yen bulls to test the resolve of the Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to unveil the amount of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the period between August 28 and September 26 at 1000 GMT on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that Japan likely spent around 2 to 3 trillion yen ($18-27 billion) during that period, when the dollar fell to around 110.90 yen from 117.80 yen. ($1=110.79 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030930dz9u000z2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
