\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Stronger Late Amid General Dlr Weakness  \b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2003\par 10:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was up sharply against the U.S. dollar late Monday in line with the U.S. unit's weakness versus the euro, yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 At 0759 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7275, down from the opening level of S$1.7287 and late Friday's S$1.7315. The dollar traded a range of S$1.7268 to S$1.7293 during the Asian session.  \par \par \uc2 A dealer at a foreign bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore didn't appear to be in the market Monday but added that the local market is wary of MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the U.S. dollar fall to around S$1.7240/45. "But that would merely be to smooth USD/SGD," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's all yen play and selling is across the board. Funds are selling dollars and there's not much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do if everyone is selling dollars," a dealer at another foreign bank said, commenting on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's across-the-board retreat against most East Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 U.S. dollar selling was exacerbated Monday as Japanese exporters and investors bought yen ahead of the fiscal half-year closing in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and in the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At 0800 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y111.35, below Y111.86 late Friday in New York and Y112.00 late Friday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, the first foreign bank dealer said any moves in the Singapore dollar overnight will depend on how the U.S. dollar trades against the yen. "Sing is not moving on its own."  \par \par \uc2 Dealers, however, predict further downside for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen, and hence the Singapore dollar, as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are now focused on the widening U.S. budget deficit and growing pressures on Asian governments to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds ended higher Monday following gains in U.S. Treasurys Friday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Bond prices were, however, below opening levels amid caution ahead of a key U.S. September jobs report due this coming Friday, dealers added. U.S. Treasurys were flat in Asian trade on the same uncertainty.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the session, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds was down three basis points at 1.11% from Friday. Longer yields also ended lower, with 10-year paper at 3.48% from 3.51% and the 15-year note at 3.94% from 3.96%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps moved generally in tandem with bond yields, with the two-year offer slipping a point to 1.37%, while the five-year was at 2.53% from 2.55%. The 10-year offer was three basis points lower at 3.64%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 227 basis points from 229 basis points Friday.  \par \par \uc2 In the local money market, rates were steady with the overnight offer at 0.5000% from 0.5625% Friday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Prices quoted in this report are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par  \par   \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\uc2 \f2  \par  \par   -By Sai Man, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4155; sai.man@dowjones.com \par   -Edited by Kevin Lim \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030929dz9t000bz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
