\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW:Positive BOJ Tankan Index Expected, 1st In 3 Yrs  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Soble  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,062 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2003\par 08:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan's closely-watched tankan survey is likely to show Wednesday that optimists outnumbered pessimists among large manufacturers for the first time in nearly three years, highlighting the continued improvement in the country's business environment.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect on average that the survey's headline sentiment index for big manufacturers will be better for a second straight time with a reading of plus one, a six point improvement from the previous survey in June and the first positive result since December 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Companies are also expected to report that they plan to boost capital spending by 6% this fiscal year started April, higher than their previous plans for a 4.9% increase, the analysts surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires predicted on average.  \par \par \uc2 A positive tankan large manufacturers' index would be another bullish sign for Japan's economy, coming on the heels of data showing a 3.9% annualized economic growth rate for the April-June quarter and a slew of other positive indicators. Major obstacles still lie in the way of a sustainable recovery, however, particularly in light of the yen's recent sharp appreciation, which could drag down exports.  \par \par \uc2 "The index for large manufacturers is likely to climb above zero and indicate boom times," said Ryutaro Kono, chief economist at BNP Paribas in Tokyo. "But most Japanese don't yet have a sense that things are picking up."  \par \par \uc2 Three months ago, large manufacturers predicted that September's index, which subtracts the percentage of respondents saying business conditions are bad from those saying they are good, would stand unchanged from June at minus 5.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ is scheduled to release the September tankan survey at 2350 GMT Tuesday, which is 8:50 a.m Wednesday local time.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             Strong Yen A Cause For Worry \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tankan is expected to show once again that large manufacturers are doing better than the rest of Japan's corporate sector.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sentiment index for large non-manufacturers, for instance, is expected to stand at minus 10, three points better than in the previous survey. But that reading would underscore the fact that pessimists remain firmly in the majority in service-related industries, which must rely mainly on the sluggish domestic market for business.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Big manufacturers, which generate more of their profits from business in overseas markets, also have a cause for worry in the form of a strengthening Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen hit a three-year high of Y110.91 against the dollar last week after Group of Seven industrial countries urged more flexibility in exchange rates - a stance likely to hamper \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's campaign to weaken the yen through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Based on reporting patterns in previous tankan surveys, most managers likely completed the September poll before the recent surge. Thus any concerns that the rise could undercut Japan's recovery by weighing on exports won't be factored into Wednesday's sentiment index.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But if the yen's appreciation persists, "the momentum of the (sentiment) recovery will inevitably slow down" in the next survey in December, said Tetsufumi Yamakawa, Goldman Sachs' chief Japan economist.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In June, managers reported that their business plans assumed an average exchange rate for the dollar of Y117.79 for the fiscal second half from Oct. 1. At 0300 GMT Monday, the dollar was changing hands at Y111.21.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Positive Tankan Good For Shares \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business sentiment has also been lifted by a dramatic rebound in Japanese stock prices. But some analysts worry a strong tankan could push the yen even higher, erasing potential share price gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The stock market can accept a long-term yen rise based on the economic recovery, but a yen rise is negative for corporate earnings in the near-term," said Masatsugu Okeya, general manager at Chuo Securities Co.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, he said a positive tankan would "likely be favorable for stocks" on the whole.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei 225 Stock Average hit a 15-month closing high of 11033.32 on Sept. 18, before paring some of those gains last week. The rise marked a 45% rebound from the benchmark index's 20-year closing low of 7607.86, set on April 28th, and a 20% gain from its level at the end of the June survey period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say a positive tankan result could help re-ignite the market, after a soaring yen drove investors to take profits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The September tankan is also likely to show a slight upward adjustment in large companies' business investment plans for this fiscal year, though the upgrade won't match the dramatic improvement that surprised investors in June, economists said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the last survey, companies said they planned to boost capital spending by 4.9%, compared with their previous plans to cut outlays 0.8%, the first time in ten quarters that the firms said they would expand investment in plant and equipment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists said the expected upgrade in September would be in line with typical rates of increase for the quarter. Corporate capital investment plans are normally adjusted upward as the fiscal year progresses, regardless of sentiment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Large Co.Sentiment   FY03 Capex Plans \par                              Mfg Non-Mfg    Large Cos \par    Barclays Capital           5    -8         6.7% \par    Nomura Research            4    -7         5.6% \par    HSBC Securities            3    -8         5.5% \par    Okasan Econ Research       3   -10         5.5% \par    Goldman Sachs              2    -9         7.0% \par    UBS                        2    -9         7.0% \par    MMS International          2   -10          - \par    UFJ Tsubasa Sec            2   -11         5.0% \par    BNP Paribas                2   -12         5.7% \par    Morgan Stanley Japan       2   -12         5.4% \par    S'tomo Mitsui Asset Mgmt   2   -13         6.9% \par    Nikko Citigroup            1   -10         5.5% \par    J.P. Morgan Securities     0    -7         7.0% \par    JCER                       0    -7         6.1% \par    Merrill Lynch Japan        0    -9          - \par    Mizuho Investors Sec       0   -11         6.5% \par    Sumitomo-Life Research     0   -11         6.0% \par    MCM Asia Pacific           0   -12         6.5% \par    Shinkin \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2        0   -12         5.8% \par    NLI Research Institute    -1   -10         6.1% \par    Shinko Research           -1   -11         6.0% \par    UFJ Institute             -1   -11         6.0% \par    Daiwa Inst of Research    -1   -11         5.3% \par    Mizuho Financial          -1   -12         5.7% \par    Dai-Ichi Life Research    -1   -14         5.9% \par  \par    AVERAGE                    1   -10         6.0% \par  \par    PREVIOUS                  -5   -13         4.9% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Ed