\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Yen Up Vs Dlr, Euro Ahead Of Japan FY 1H End  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Soble  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 923 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2003\par 07:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen advanced against the dollar and the euro in Asia Monday, as Japanese exporters continued buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up their books ahead of their fiscal half-year end Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rise, which accelerated around midday with the triggering of stop-loss buy orders against the euro, was spread across a range of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Canadian and Australian dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "The cross-yen pairs are moving quite a bit today," said Masamichi Koike, head dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui bank. "The dollar/yen is still weak, and pressure from the crosses is pushing it lower."  \par \par \uc2 The yen's recent surge, particularly against the U.S. dollar, has caught many Japanese exporters by surprise, forcing them to accumulate more yen to beef up hedging positions before the Sept. 30 end of the fiscal half year, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 The yen hit a three-year high of Y110.91 against the dollar last week after Group of Seven industrial countries urged more flexibility in exchange rates - a stance likely to hamper \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's campaign to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Officials at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  have continued to insist they will sell yen to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise as they see fit. But traders have seen little evidence that the MOF has been in the market since the G7 met in Dubai on Sept. 20.  \par \par \uc2 Lingering concerns that the MOF could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the dollar from falling too far before Tuesday's half-year bookings kept a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, traders said. That floor, however, could give way after firms' books are closed Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Once that's been put behind them, people are likely to try to push (the dollar) lower," Koike said. "We could see a test of the downside starting in London tomorrow."  \par \par \uc2 He said he didn't think the MOF had abandoned its policy of capping the yen, but simply realized that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -sparked rise in the dollar now would quickly be reversed by the market  \par \par \uc2 "They could push it back above Y113, but with the dollar looking as weak as it is, lots of people would be waiting to sell at that level," he said. "They're thinking carefully about their timing."  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y111.24, below Y111.86 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y112.00 late Friday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1440, lower than $1.1475 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.1474 in Tokyo Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y127.29, down from Y128.36 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y128.50 in Tokyo late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.6560, down from $1.6595 in New York and lower than $1.6571 in Tokyo Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar faces another test Wednesday, when the Bank of Japan is scheduled to release its closely-watched tankan survey of business sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect on average that the headline index for large manufacturers in the quarterly poll will stand at plus 1, a six point improvement from the previous survey in June. That would be the first time optimists outnumber pessimists since the December 2000 poll.  \par \par \uc2 Investors hope a strong tankan will re-ignite Japan's cooling stock market, which has slipped some 8% from its mid-September peak due to the yen's sharp rise. But many also worry the result could push the yen even higher, erasing potential share price gains.  \par \par \uc2 "The stock market can accept a long-term yen rise based on the economic recovery, but a yen rise is negative for corporate earnings in the near-term," said Masatsugu Okeya, general manager at Chuo Securities Co.  \par \par \uc2 Such concerns go beyond the yen's immediate impact on share prices. Investors, like Japan's finance ministry, worry a significant rise in the yen could snuff out Japan's recovery by eroding all-important exports.  \par \par \uc2 Based on reporting patterns in previous tankan surveys, most managers likely completed the September tankan poll before the yen's recent surge. Thus any concerns that the rise could undercut Japan's recovery by weighing on exports won't be factored into Wednesday's sentiment index.  \par \par \uc2 But if the yen's appreciation persists, "the momentum of the (sentiment) recovery will inevitably slow down," said Tetsufumi Yamakawa, Goldman Sachs' chief Japan economist.  \par \par \uc2 The concern was underscored by Fujio Cho, president of Toyota Motor Corp.'s (TM or 7203), who said earnings at the auto giant could be hit hard if the yen's rise strengthening trend continues.  \par \par \uc2 "We're worried about this," Cho said at a press conference Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In the June tankan, managers reported that their business plans assumed an average exchange rate for the dollar of Y117.79 for the fiscal second half from Oct. 1 - more than six yen above the U.S. unit's current level.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030929dz9t0005j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
