\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Slow Week Until US Jobs Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,291 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2003\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Treasurys Rally On Stocks Fall \par  \par DJIA          9313.08    loss 30.88     dn 0.3% \par NASDAQ        1792.07    loss 25.17     dn 1.4% \par S&P 500       996.85     loss 6.42      dn 0.6% \par Dow Future    9267.00    loss 13.00     dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1308.00    loss 2.50      dn 0.2% \par S&P Future    993.50     loss 1.00      dn 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.99% down 0.11 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Stocks extended their slump Friday amid growing doubts about the economy, delivering the worst weekly loss on the Nasdaq composite index in well over a year. Prices of Treasurys rallied.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Better-than-expected economic growth in the second quarter was overshadowed by a poor showing in consumer confidence and unease ahead of the third-quarter earnings season. Stocks were also vulnerable to selling after the runup in prices since March.  \par \par \uc2 "What we've seen here is the market starting to distrust the durability of the economic recovery," said Charles H. Blood Jr., senior financial markets analyst at Brown Brothers Harriman & Co.  \par \par \uc2 "The most cyclical and volatile stocks, which had been leading the way up, also declined," Blood said. "What's more, the bond market rallied and expectations for an interest rate hike were pushed more into the future."  \par \par \uc2 Canada's Manulife Financial Corp., long on the prowl for a big acquisition, agreed to buy John Hancock Financial Services Inc. - one of the best-known names in the U.S. insurance industry - for $10.3 billion in stock.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys rose sharply Friday thanks to investors scrambling to realign their positions as yields continued to defy expectations by falling into critical territory that could spark yet more buying.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said a wide variety of factors contributed to the rally, including short covering, sluggish stocks, month-end and quarter-end portfolio adjustments and the emergence of some buying by mortgage investors.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Stocks Lower Toward Close \par  \par USD-Yen             111.22       loss  0.53    dn 0.5% \par AUD-USD             0.6715       loss  0.0031  dn 0.5 \par Nikkei 225          10204.22     loss  114.22  dn 1.1% \par Hang Seng           11191.61     loss  98.54   dn 0.9% \par JGB Yield           1.3950% up 0.0050 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks fell toward the close Monday as Wall Street's decline last week and a weaker U.S. dollar hit exporter issues.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of Japanese government bonds drifted ahead of the Bank of Japan's release Wednesday of the "tankan," a roundup of the economic outlook. Some bond traders worried the tankan outlook would tip sentiment toward stocks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of U.S. Treasurys drifted slightly higher in Asia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose to $364.73 billion at the end of August, from $356.49 billion at the end of July, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  figures published Monday show.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks Seen Lower; Euro Off; Japan Warns \par  \par Euro-USD            1.1448      loss  0.0010  dn 0.1% \par Stlg-USD            1.6572      loss  0.0002  dn 0.01% \par USD-Franc           1.3570      gain  0.0016  up 0.2% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares should open slightly lower, while prices of government bonds start little changed and the euro opens a shade weaker against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares should start slightly lower as investors open the new week with a cautious view toward upcoming U.S. third-quarter earnings.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London spreadbettor City Index is calling the FTSE 100 down 15 at the open to 4142, and the DAX down 20 to 3304.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks ended last week lower amid broad-based profit-taking as investors remained nervous about the pace of global economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philip Shaw, economist at Investec, said the macroeconomic calendar swings into top gear this week, with manufacturing updates released on both sides of the Atlantic. The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also scheduled to decide on interest rates Thursday. "Many investors are fretting over weak U.S. labor markets, so September's employment report on Friday will take center stage," Shaw said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the news, a falling tree that hit electricity lines in Switzerland and the outage of two French transmission lines may have caused a domino effect that blacked out most of Italy on Sunday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices should open steady to slightly higher, extending gains from Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is typical for September that everybody trades in the same direction, trying to make money ahead of the year end," said a trader in Paris, "So now, most of the players buy even more bonds, as they are on an upward trend."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trader said the market could rise as high as 115.50 for the December bund.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session at slightly lower levels of around $1.1450 as investors turn their attention back to a more familiar question this week: when will the U.S. recovery start producing jobs?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. payroll numbers for September, due Friday, are expected to remain the focus of the market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will also watch for Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and lesser data such as Tuesday's Conference Board Consumer Confidence Index and Wednesday's ISM Manufacturing Index, both for September.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think we're at a threshold," said Greg Anderson, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at ABN-Amro Bank in Chicago. "It will probably be a very quiet week until we get to Friday because the number one issue on everybody's minds is whether the U.S. economy is strong enough to create jobs."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tim Mazanec, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust, also expects the weak tone to continue for the dollar, though its range against the yen could be limited to between Y111 and Y113 because of fears that the BOJ could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If you go long yen, you have to understand that you're up against the BOJ," said Mazanec, adding that he wouldn't be surprised if they come into the market to some extent on a move below Y110.75.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ministry of finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again warned of an "excessive" rise in the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold fell more than $3 to around $381 overnight, continuing last week's profit taking-led selloff.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Monday, Sep 29: Italy CPI; UK Confidence \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0300/0700 Italy        Sept CPI (seen +2.8%-2.9% YY) \par 0400/0800 Italy        August Producer Price Index \par 0430/0830 U.K.         Provisional M0, Full M4 \par 0500/0900 U.K.         September GfK Consumer Confidence \par 0500/0900 Italy        Treasury auctions BTPs, CCTs \par 0830/1230 U.S.         August Personal Income (last +0.2%) \par 0830/1230 U.S.         August Personal Spending (last +0.8%) \par 0930/1330 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  calls for bids   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Close Brothers (U.CBG): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit before items (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP79.5M (GBP78.5M) \par Note: Improving markets should have helped across businesses and especially Winterflood market-making, because of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gearing. \par  \par Severfield-Rowen (U.SFR): 1H Earnings \par Note: The company said 1H interim pretax profit was GBP3.7M compared to GBP5.1M last year. \par  \par Bede (U.BED): 1H Earnings \par  \par Dataflex Medical (U.DFX): 1H Earnings \par  \par Hardy Underwriting Group (U.HDU): 1H Earnings \par  \par Madisons Coffee (U.MCF): FY Earnings \par  \par Netcall (): FY Earnings \par  \par Personal Group Hldgs (U.PGH): 1H Earnings \par  \par SABMiller (O.SAB): Trading update \par  \par United Utilities (U.UUT): Trading update \par  \par Watermark Group (U.WMK): 1H Earnings \par  \par White Young Green (U.WHY): FY Earnings \par  \par Compass (U.CPG): FY trading update \par  \par Mmo2 (U.OOM): Trading update \par  \par Nordanglia Education (U.NAE): AGM \par  \par Systems Integrated (U.SYI): AGM \par  \par Banco Zaragozano (E.BZA): EGM \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1011  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030929dz9t0004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
