\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Sep 26  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2003\par 11:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi Friday declared his structural reforms are just starting to bear fruit and pledged his new administration will press ahead with his policies to restore the economy to health.Addressing an extraordinary parliamentary session that unofficially marks the start of a campaign to win a Lower House election expected this autumn, Koizumi is riding high after a strong victory in a ruling party leadership race last weekend, and amid new polls showing his popularity rising and the economy improving. "Japan PM Koizumi" first take ran at 0506 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Fukui says the G7 agrees that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sometime necessary to calm \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and he pledges to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ultra-easy policy in place for now, saying: "We will absolutely maintain our current monetary easing in order to assure a sustainable economic recovery." ("Fukui -2: Underlines \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Readiness To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0522 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The latest economic data show Japan's recovery remains on a weak footing, with consumer prices still sliding and retail sales continuing to slump. Nationwide CPI fell 0.1% on year while retail sales dropped 2% in August. (WRAP will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: War in Iraq, SARS in Asia, a domestic consumption hangover from a credit-card binge, nationwide strikes and a devastating typhoon have battered the South Korean economy this year. Now the government is in no mood to let a surging won further delay an export-led recovery from Korea's first recession since the 1997-98 regional financial crisis. The G7 may have come out last weekend in favor of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising against the dollar, but Seoul has fought this week to keep the U.S. unit above 1,150 won and signaled it won't let up. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Seoul Tries To Lasso Won As It Tracks Yen Rally" ran at 0750 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Industrial Revitalization Corp. said Friday it will help rescue a pair of midsize firms from western Japan - home appliance and electronics retailer Matsuya Denki and chemical trading firm Meisei Shokai - bringing to six the number of companies for which it has announced aid. ("Japan IRC" first take ran at 0842 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's economy grew at its slowest pace in 11 quarters during the three months to June 30, with activity hit by a power crunch and weak exports, but economists think this represents the nadir of the current slowdown. ("=DATA VIEW: NZ Growth Expected To Snap Out Of 2Q Funk" ran at 0133 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Solid domestic demand and a simmering housing market will keep New Zealand's economy ticking over despite the drag from weak exports, according to an upgraded consensus growth forecast released by the NZ Institute of Economic Research.("NZ Year To March '04 GDP Forecast Revised Up - NZIER" ran at 0301 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Australia's Assistant Treasurer Helen Coonan said Friday the government is satisfied with how the domestic economy is performing, signaling it may not need to tinker with its 2003-04 growth forecast of 3.25%. ("=INTERVIEW: Australia Assistant Treasurer Upbeat On Econ" ran at 0834 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Goldman Sachs Group is teeing-off in a new direction. The Wall Street investment bank is set to become Japan's biggest golf-course operator, making a big bet that the sport whose opulent image epitomized Japan's "bubble" economy of the late 1980's will appeal to a broader range of the population. ("=Goldman Bets On Bringing Golf To The Masses In Japan" ran at 0813 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =RBI WATCH: In a bid to revive India's fledgling bond futures market, which has seen no trades since mid-August, regulators plan to launch new derivative instruments and pricing models that are more in tune with the local market. ("=RBI WATCH: India Tries To Revive Ailing Bond Futures Mkt" ran at 0418 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Now that the dust has settled from the storm unleashed by the G7 in Dubai, the consensus is that the Australian dollar is headed higher, but possibly not as high as initially thought. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Australian Dlr Heading Higher, But Not Too High" ran at 0510 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's producer price index rose 1.4% year-on-year in August as cost increases for oil, steel and other raw materials boosted prices for finished goods, official figures released Friday show. ("China's August Producer Price Index Up 1.4% On Year" ran at 0253 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's export growth slowed in August as trade with some East Asian markets moderated or fell, the Census and Statistics Department said Friday. ("DATA SNAP: HK Aug Exports Slow As E Asia Demand Moderates" ran at 0857 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's manufacturing expanded for the first time in five months - rising 10.3% on year in August - thanks to strong growth in the pharmaceutical sector, raising hopes the economy may have turned a corner.("=DATA VIEW: Singapore Aug Output Expands Surprise 10.3%" ran at 0728 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =NZ WATCH: Moves by a New Zealand watchdog group to raise awareness about cyber-patents show businesses here are starting to wake up to the risk of infringing on intellectual property rights and the need to reform the patent system, according to industry insiders. ("=NZ WATCH: Retailers Get Wake-Up Call On Software Patents" ran at 0302 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand manufacturing activity continued to expand in August, albeit only slightly, with new orders rising strongly, according to figures issued Friday. But activity is still well down from levels a year earlier. ("NZ Manufacturing Activity Expanded Slightly In August" ran at 1200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar held its ground against the yen in Asia Friday as a lingering view that the Japanese authorities might sell yen to prop up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  offset dollar selling by Japanese exporters.("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0536 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency, or IBRA, plans to announce bidders for a 51% stake in PT Bank Internasional Indonesia around 1000 GMT. Proceeds from selling the BII stake will be used to narrow the 2003 budget deficit. IBRA plans to announce the winning bidder in mid-November. (story will run around 1000 GMT or later)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Asia Pulp & Paper said Friday it has reached a compromise agreement with creditors over a major sticking point in talks aimed at resolving its $13.9 billion debt problem. After a late night meeting Thursday, APP and leading creditors signed a memorandum of understanding which lays down a timetable for the company to repay $6.7 billion in debt owed by its Indonesian units, Gandhi Sulistiyanto, an APP negotiator in the talks, told Dow Jones Friday. ("APP Claims Near Compromise With Creditors On Debt Workout" ran at 0447 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Seiko Epson lifted its group net profit outlook for this fiscal year, citing robust demand for electronic devices used in mobile phone handsets, while Advantest raised its outlook for group sales and profits for the same period, crediting a recovery in capital outlays by chip-making companies. (WRAP will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Honda Motor plans to start production of its Accord sedan in Taiwan in October and aims to start marketing the model in November. The Japanese auto maker previously provided technological assistance to Taiwan's San Yang Industry to manufacture and sell Honda cars, but didn't renew the contract with San Yang when it expired in June 2002. ("Honda Motor To Produce, Market Accord Sedan In Taiwan" ran at 0207 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: The sole pay-television operator in Malaysia, Astro All Asia Networks PLC, Friday lifted the lift a little on its widely anticipated initial public offering, saying around 26.6% of the company will be floated on the Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange. ("Malaysia's Astro/IPO" ran at 0638 GMT; "Malaysia Astro/IPO Proceeds" ran at 0823 GMT; WRAP will run around 1130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Convenience store operator Lawson and Nikko Cordial Securities will tie up in a deal that will enable individuals to trade stocks and bonds at Lawson's 7,700 convenience stores nationwide, the companies said Friday. ("Lawson Ties Up With Nikko To Allow Stk Trading At Stores" ran at 0322 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Railway operator Tokyu Corp. said Friday it will make Tokyu Tourist a wholly owned subsidiary via a stock swap, in order to provide full support to the struggling travel agent. ("Tokyu Corp" first take ran at 0252 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The Australian government said Friday it will hold talks with oil and airline companies about the unexpected jet fuel shortage at the nation's busiest airport, which led to international flight disruptions. Industry Minister Ian Macfarlane will meet with oil and airline representatives Tuesday in Canberra to discuss the cause of the fuel shortage and review reporting systems within the fuel industry. ("=WRAP: Australian Govt To Review Sydney Jet Fuel Shortage" ran at 0815 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: BOC Hong Kong said Friday it will introduce reforms recommended by a special committee set up following default on a HK$2.1 billion loan to Shanghai businessman Chau Ching-ngai, currently under investigation for corruption. ("BOC HK To Implement Recommended Reform After Loan Scandal" first take ran at 0106 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CHIBA, Japan: When it comes to games, simple is best - that's what Nintendo is counting on, according to the software game maker's president, who says game consoles equipped with super-large chips and ever more functions may not be the answer for the future of the struggling games industry. ("=Nintendo Pinning Hopes On Simpler Games To Attract Users" ran at 0559 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The Federal Court's Justice Ronald Sackville said Friday he will deliver judgment Tuesday on Australian Agricultural Co.'s call for an injunction stopping the sale of Stanbroke Pastoral Co. AACo has alleged the tender process for the sale was conducted unfairly and should be run again. ("Judgment On Australia Stanbroke Pastoral Co Due Tuesday" ran at 0741 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: U.S. chip design firm Atheros Communications, expects shipments of its wireless fidelity, or Wi-fi chips to grow five-fold this year, one more reason for its manufacturing partner, Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co., to celebrate. Wi-fi is just one of the hot technology segments driving sales at TSMC this year. (INTERVIEW with Atheros Communications President and CEO will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: As China's economy continues to boom and roads expand at a rapid pace, Honeywell International is betting on bigger growth in the heavy duty truck market and with it a jump in its own sales of turbochargers. ("=INTERVIEW: Honeywell Banks On Growth In China Truck Mkt" ran at 0459 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Neptune Orient Lines, which operates the world's seventh biggest container fleet, should see higher 2004 profits than previously thought thanks to further hikes in freight rates on trans-Pacific sea routes, shipping analysts said Friday. ("Analysts Up Singapore NOL 04 Pft Outlook On Freight Rates" ran at 0909 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Tom.com, the media company backed by Hong Kong tycoon Li Ka-shing, said it will buy a technology firm from one of its substantial shareholders for a total consideration of no more than US$150 million. ("HK Tom.com/Tech Co" first take ran at 0135 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: As more global auto makers look to India to source car parts, the prospect of increased export orders is revving up shares of the country's auto-component industry. ("=Asian Stock Focus: India Auto-Part Cos In High Gear" ran at 0335 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's second-largest container-board manufacturer Lee & Man Paper Manufacturing debuts on Hong Kong's main board today. ("HK Lee & Man Paper" first take ran at 0208 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's largest cotton textile producer Weiqiao will announce first-half interim results later today. In the first quarter of 2003, the company recorded a net profit of CNY108.2 million. (story will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwanese display maker Quanta Display has priced 26.1 million global depositary receipts, raising US$269.9 million, one of the underwriters of the deal said Friday. ("Taiwan Quanta Display Sells US$269.9M In GDRs - Source" first take ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's INI Steel said it will list 16.91 million global depositary receipts on the Luxembourg Exchange Oct. 15. ("S Korea INI Steel/GDRs" first take ran at 0038 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Fubon Financial Holding has priced US$225 million worth of overseas convertibles at a premium of 24.5%, according to a banker familiar with the transaction. ("Taiwan Fubon Fincl Sells Convs At 24.5% Premium - Banker" ran at 0503 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: The stock of EverFocus Electronics may well be down from its June high on the eve of its traditional peak sales season, but the count is still out on whether that makes it a good buy. Despite a sharp fall of nearly 40% from its early-June year peak, most analysts are advising investors to shun the Taiwan surveillance-system maker altogether because, they say, the plunge still doesn't fully reflect the firm's poor first-half financial results. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Taiwan's EverFocus Still Lacks Luster" ran at 0248 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Troubled orchid grower Euro-Asia Agricultural said the High Court has further adjourned a hearing on a petition to wind up the troubled orchid grower to Dec. 29. ("HK Euro-Asia Winding-Up Hearing Adjourns To Dec. 29" ran at 0324 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Foreign analysts are attracted to Island & Peninsular, following the recent announcement of an asset swap that will make it Malaysia's biggest property developer in landholding terms. But any buying interest in I&P shares is likely to be short-lived, analysts say. ("Foreign Analysts Eye Malaysia's I&P But Mainly Short Term" ran at 0709 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: India pipeline manufacturer Welspun Gujarat expects a sharp rise in earnings for the current fiscal year, boosted by large orders from Iranian oil firms, said a senior company executive. ("=INTERVIEW: Welspun Gujarat Sees FY Pft Up On Iran Orders" ran at 0440 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Changsheng Fund Management said Friday that a qualified foreign institutional investor has bought into its latest fund. Changsheng's second open-ended fund will mainly invest in bond indexes, said Li Yanmin, a compliance official at Changsheng Fund Management. Li declined to identift the QFII firm or the size of its investment. ("China Changsheng Says QFII Invests In Open-End Bond Fund" ran at 0801 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's Finance Ministry has given the mandate for the initial public offering of government-owned Felda Holdings to JP Morgan and local firm Avenue Assets Bhd., a source close to the ministry said Friday. ("JP Morgan, Malaysia's Avenue Assets Get Felda IPO Mandate" ran at 0420 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: A bullish tide will sweep through the Mideast and Asian crude markets in coming days, buoying prices, following the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries' surprise pledge to curb output by 900,000 barrels a day. But only time will tell if OPEC follows through with the full cutback - it has a poor record in sticking to its ceiling - although some trimming of output can be expected in the near term.("=OUTLOOK: Asia, Gulf Crudes Bullish, Term Awards Awaited" ran at 0745 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Disruptions to Japan's oil and power supplies due to a major earthquake that closed a refinery in Hokkaido, northern Japan, and disrupted oil shipping are expected to be short-lived, officials said Friday. ("=WRAP: Japan Quake - Oil, Power Disruptions Short-lived" ran at 0912 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030926dz9q000ds\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
