\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Fukui says no change in G7 view on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  policies.  \b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2003\par 09:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 26 (Reuters) - A recent statement by the Group of Seven (G7) calling for flexible exchange rates does not rule out individual countries acting to protect their economies from volatility, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The yen has surged in recent days against the dollar after traders interpreted a G7 statement calling for flexible exchange rates as putting a brake on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the yen from rising.  \par \par \uc2 "The G7 position on allowing authorities to act in times of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility has not changed," Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said in a speech in Tokyo, adding that the statement was not aimed at Japan and China.  \par \par \uc2 In the first seven months of this year Japan spent nine trillion yen ($80 billion) to keep the yen down.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities are concerned the yen's surge will cut profits at exporters by making their goods expensive overseas, just as demand for Japanese products shows signs of picking up.  \par \par \uc2 Ministers and senior officials have issued a series of warnings that authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to calm the yen, with the latest coming earlier Friday from Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady around 112 yen on Friday afternoon in Tokyo, about one percent above three-year lows around 110.90 yen set on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 China meanwhile has been under pressure from trading partners over its policy of keeping the yuan pegged to the dollar, which U.S. manufacturers see as giving Chinese products an unfair price advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Fukui said it was desirable for China to move to a flexible exchange rate system in the long term, but added that the country should strengthen its financial system first to avoid any instability.  \par \par \uc2 Turning to the domestic economy, Fukui repeated the view of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government that there are signs of brightness, with corporate profits rising and leading indicators such as machinery orders picking up.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that the recovery was not yet self-sustaining.  \par \par \uc2 "The BOJ still has a lot of work to do to make the recovery a strong and self-sustaining one," Fukui said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's recovery was being led by large manufacturing companies, while non-manufacturers and smaller companies were still finding conditions difficult.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters would likely see business pick up later this year in line with an improvement in the U.S. economy, while there were signs domestically that employment conditions and wages were starting to improve, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Fukui said the BOJ would concentrate its resources on beating deflation, and reaffirmed the BOJ's commitment to keeping its current ultra-loose monetary policy until prices stabilise above zero. ($1=112.10 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030926dz9q0016o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
