\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-MOF Tanigaki says Japan to act as needed on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 342 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2003\par 03:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister repeated an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday, saying Japanese authorities would act as and when needed to curb the export-crimping rise in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Recent moves in the yen have been sudden," Sadakazu Tanigaki told reporters after a cabinet meeting. "We are watching closely and will take appropriate measures when necessary. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 The yen was steady on Friday, holding about one percent off three-year highs against the dollar set earlier in the week, as wariness about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a retreat in Tokyo share prices tempered bullishness.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading just above 112 yen in mid-morning in Tokyo, little changed from Wednesday's late New York levels and about one percent above three-year lows around 110.90 yen set on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities are concerned the rise in the yen will cut profits at exporters by making their goods expensive overseas, just as demand for Japanese goods shows signs of picking up.  \par \par \uc2 With domestic demand slow and data on Friday showing consumer prices fell for the 47th straight month in August on a year-on-year basis, exporters are the main support for the nascent recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have stepped up warnings over the yen's rise in recent days, though some ministers' views differ.  \par \par \uc2 Economics and Financial Services Minister Heizo Takenaka said on Friday that the yen's rise this week was not affecting Japan's economy for the time being.  \par \par \uc2 "So far I don't think it is having a great impact on the macroeconomy," he told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 "The issue is the medium-term trend," he said, adding that it was not possible to judge the impact of moves in exchange rates over just one week.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki added that he would work closely with the Bank of Japan to beat Japan's falling prices, calling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take effective policies.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030926dz9q000md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
