\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Wary of  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , dollar cuts losses vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2003\par 19:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Sept 25 (Reuters) - The dollar cut its losses versus the yen on Thursday as wary traders remained on guard for potential yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo after an eight-session rally for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the dollar traded in relatively snug ranges against its other major rivals.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which fell to three-year lows below 111 yen this week, was nearly flat against the euro, as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's three-week gain was checked by a disappointing German business survey.  \par \par \uc2 In midday U.S. trade, the dollar rose to 111.93 yen , up 0.13 percent on the day. The euro fell 0.03 percent to $1.1488 . The euro rose to 128.79 yen .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 0.41 percent to 1.3438 Swiss francs . Sterling was up 0.10 percent at $1.6604 .  \par \par \uc2 A string of U.S. economic data helped refocus market attention on the likelihood of a U.S. economic recovery. The dollar weakened a touch after the government reported a surprise drop in orders for durable goods, a volatile survey, and a decline in weekly jobless claims.  \par \par \uc2 "Generally the durable goods numbers are a bit softer than expected," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York. "We have had some soft economic data, higher oil prices, got a general sell-off in equity markets. These things tend to be associated with a weak dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Orders for durable goods fell 0.9 percent in August, the first drop since April. A 0.6 percent rise had been expected.  \par \par \uc2 Another report showed the number of first-time jobless claims fell in the week ended Sept. 20 to 381,000 from 400,000 a week earlier. But the drop was aided by Hurricane Isabel, which closed government offices to would-be claimers on the East Coast.  \par \par \uc2 "We just had a tiny dip lower of the euro on jobless claims, but now it looks as though the attention is on the durable goods orders," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IDEAglobal in New York. "It is another reason to kick the dollar lower."  \par \par \uc2 Data on home sales - both new and existing - beat expectations. Housing remains one of the pillars holding up the U.S. economy. Sales of new homes in August rose to an annual rate of 1.15 million units, up from 1.11 million in July. Sales of existing homes rose to a record high annual rate of 6.47 million units in August from 6.13 million in July.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. recovery is in focus as improvement in consumer demand and manufacturing is being held back by soft labor market conditions.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar suffered losses Wednesday after oil cartel OPEC issued a shocking announcement that it would cut oil production. The news hit stocks in the United States, Japan and Europe as it clouded the global economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 The euro recovered from losses incurred after a slide in the influential German Ifo survey's business conditions index to 79.2 from 79.9, even though the main business climate index rose for a fifth month in a row in September.  \par \par \uc2 YEN RALLY PAUSES  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rally against the dollar paused with the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  near a three-year high notched on Tuesday. Markets had perceived a Group of Seven call for flexible exchange rates as a demand for Japan and China to stop intentionally weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defense of their export sectors.  \par \par \uc2 But concerns that Japan might again sell its yen have intensified amid a string of statements from officials who insist Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies are unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's new trade minister, Shoichi Nakagawa, warned against sudden moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said authorities were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a rising yen if necessary.  \par \par \uc2 "We had verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which drew market attention. The yen is seen as a leading indicator for other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a sharp appreciation from here would prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mary Davies, global \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston.  \par \par \uc2 Recent upward pressure on the yen stemmed from a rebound of more than 40 percent in the Nikkei average from 20-year lows set in April, but it fell 1.83 percent on Thursday after OPEC's decision increased worries over Japan's export sector. (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases and John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030925dz9p002pi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
