\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen rally pauses on stocks,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  caution.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2003\par 12:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 25 (Reuters) - The yen paused on Thursday after rallying this week to three-year highs against the dollar, on wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Tokyo stock losses, while the euro tripped on a disappointing German business survey.  \par \par \uc2 Japan tried to talk down the yen earlier in the day, with new trade minister Shoichi Nakagawa warning against sudden moves after it rose as far as 110.85 per dollar on Tuesday on a Group of Seven nations' appeal for flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's five-yen rally over the last week was also called into question by financial markets, which pushed Tokyo stocks to four-week lows after an OPEC oil output cut increased worries about the viability of Japan's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "Clearly overnight we had sizeable declines in Japanese equities, with exporters leading the way, and that led the market to worry about Japan's capacity to live with a stronger yen," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro.  \par \par \uc2 "Given the kind of speculative positions that exist now in dollar/yen and the scale of the moves we have seen, the implication is that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point without necessarily attracting anger from the U.S."  \par \par \uc2 By 0740 GMT the yen was unchanged on the day at 111.78. Against the euro it was also unmoved at 128.43 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady at $1.1491, off this week's eight-week high of $1.1529.  \par \par \uc2 The euro climbed back from a dip caused by a slide in the German Ifo survey's business conditions to 79.2 from 79.9, even though the main business climate index rose for a fifth month in a row in September.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. durable goods orders are due at 1230 GMT and are expected to rise 0.6 percent after a 1.0 gain in the previous month. U.S. jobless claims are expected to come in at 400,000 compared with 399,000 in the previous week.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. recovery is in focus as improvement in consumer demand and manufacturing is being held back by soft labour market conditions.  \par \par \uc2 YEN COOLS DOWN  \par \par \uc2 Recent upward pressure on the yen stemmed from a rebound of more than 40 percent in the Nikkei average from 20-year lows set in April, but it fell 1.8 percent on Thursday after OPEC's decision increased worries over Japan's key export sector.  \par \par \uc2 A series of official comments warning about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise also helped to cool the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said authorities were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a rising yen if necessary. His predecessor Haruhiko Kuroda, now an adviser to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, said late on Wednesday the yen's rise has gone too far and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary.  \par \par \uc2 "We had verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  which drew market attention. The yen is seen as a leading indicator for other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a sharp appreciation from here would prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mary Davies, global \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston.  \par \par \uc2 OPEC's decision was also seen as a negative for the dollar, after it sent Wall Street stocks lower on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Anything that dampens global growth prospects is going to damage the dollar," said Jalinoos.  \par \par \uc2 Futures indices showed signs of a possible recovery at New York's open on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, France pledged to cut its public sector deficit next year to 3.6 percent of GDP from four percent this year, and get it back below European limits in 2005, seeking to head off an EU row and the risk of fines.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030925dz9p001gl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
