\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Got Its G7 Wish Too; Beware  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2003\par 10:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Miyako Takebe \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DUBAI (Dow Jones)--Poor Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Browbeaten by U.S. free-market purists, Tokyo was forced to accept a Group of Seven call for freer exchange rates and then endure the resulting painful rise in the yen. The mercantilist export machine was bullied into forswearing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation to keep America and Europe happy.  \par \par \uc2 That at least is the popular spin on Saturday's meeting here of G7 finance ministers and central bankers, which adopted a U.S. call for Asian governments to stop restraining their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to let the dollar fall.  \par \par \uc2 But consider another possibility: maybe Japan got just what it wanted, too.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo no longer has to defend an untenable and economically unnecessary Y115 floor for the dollar. It wins favor with the U.S. for joining the "flexible exchange rate" bandwagon, while remaining free to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at weaker-dollar levels to smooth out the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, Japan no longer has to mute its criticism of China for keeping its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak. Elsewhere in Asia, the yen rise pressures other export powers like Taiwan and South Korea to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate too.  \par \par \uc2 And, in time-honored fashion, if domestic exporters yelp at a higher yen, the Japanese government can blame it all on foreign pressure.  \par \par \uc2 Make no mistake, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't kicked the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  habit. Look for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen aggressively again, perhaps if the dollar looks in danger of falling below Y110.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan still seems committed to the idea that yen strength will be fiercely resisted if it threatens economic crisis," said ING Securities economist Richard Jerram in a note to clients. "Target levels might have changed, but we think basic principles have not."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials are already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally.  \par \par \uc2 The government's point man of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and top delegate in Dubai, Zembei Mizoguchi, said the market had overreacted by bidding up the yen, suggesting the dollar will rebound: "The market has a self-adjusting mechanism."  \par \par \uc2 But the vice finance minister for international affairs made it clear to reporters he'll be there to help if the market fails to get the idea: "If those mechanism aren't fully \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we will take appropriate action. We can act if necessary at any given time."  \par \par \uc2 In fact, Japanese officials are pleased with the outcome of the meeting here. They won't admit to relief that the yen has broken higher - although one official joked to some Japanese reporters that their souvenir-shopping had been aided by the yen's new purchasing power.  \par \par \uc2 As the dollar has weakened generally this year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign had diverted most of greenback's weakness to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar was down 8% against the euro before the G7 fireworks but less than 3% against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 But while Tokyo was buying an estimated $100 billion for yen so far this year - far more than in any previous full year - its economy posted its sixth straight quarter of growth, its main stock market index jumped 40% and a half-decade of deflation began to show signs it might ebb.  \par \par \uc2 And yet even if the economy didn't need such \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  heroics, the Finance Ministry was locked into defending a Y115 line in the sand - partly because the finance minister had publicly declared it a key level on several occasions and partly because the ministry's obvious attention to Y115 made it a huge target for speculators.  \par \par \uc2 In that sense, the ministry likely didn't object to signing on to the U.S.-led move for freer \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which takes some pressure off Japan as lone guardian of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 That was done with a brief G7 statement.  \par \par \uc2 The officials from the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and Canada called for "more flexibility in exchange rates" to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system."  \par \par \uc2 Translation: Asian central banks shouldn't sell their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars so much because this keeps their exports artificially cheap and widens the alarmingly widening U.S. current-account deficit. The idea is to help rebalance a global economy that relies too much for its growth on U.S. shoppers buying Asian goods.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders got the message loud and clear, dumping the dollar and snapping up yen, Chinese yuan, South Korean won, Taiwan dollars and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In fact, Tokyo's defense of the dollar at Y115, which had withstood numerous assaults since February 2001, gave way Friday, just as G7 officials gathered in Dubai, after Dow Jones Newswires reported the contents of a draft G7 statement. On Monday the dollar buckled, crashing some Y3. It touched a 33-month low of Y110.92 Tuesday before reaching a jittery equilibrium as the market tried to guess when or whether Tokyo would heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Around 0600 GMT Wednesday, the dollar was at Y111.99.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit has also been dragged to 34-month lows against the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a 13-month low against the Taiwanese unit.  \par \par \uc2 Against the pegged Chinese and Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders used the nondeliverable forwards markets to bet Beijing will eventually unhitch the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the U.S. dollar. The greenback sank to a record discount in the yuan-forwards market, implying expectations the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise 3% or more in a year. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to a discount against the Hong Kong unit for the first time since 2001.  \par \par \uc2 China has strongly reaffirmed this week that it's in no hurry to fulfill long-term promises to liberalize yuan trade. The Bank of Korea has warned against a won rise, but both Seoul and Taipei find a stronger domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  easier to handle as long as the yen is rising too.  \par \par \uc2 The focus now is where Japan will make its next all-out stand to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "adjustment" from becoming a dollar collapse.  \par \par \uc2 "Lowering their supporting level to 110 could be a reasonable strategy for the time being," said CSFB strategist Satoru Ogasawara. "Free fall is a risk. If it goes below 100 again, they would have to come up with some kind of reflationary policy."  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, the yen is already stronger than corporate Japan was expecting until very recently. Large manufacturers told the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June they expected the dollar to average Y117.79 in the six months to March, while carmakers Toyota, Nissan and Honda were banking on the U.S. unit between Y115 and Y120.  \par \par \uc2 But Morgan Stanley equity strategist Naoki Kamiyama reckons Japanese companies could withstand a dollar fall to Y108-Y112.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, the recuperating Japanese economy is growing a tad less dependent on its world-beating exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs strategist Kathy Matsui said Japanese exporters are contributing just 33% of profit growth among Tokyo Stock Exchange First Section companies this year, half the contribution of last fiscal year and that this portion will likely fall to 27% in the year starting next April.  \par \par \uc2 That doesn't mean the Finance Ministry has walked away from the market.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though we are carefully watching day-to-day and minute-to-minute market developments, we must also always look at the big picture," said one ministry official.  \par \par \uc2 But when asked about the authorities' quieter presence, this week, he added, "Of course, we haven't said we haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 777 620 0923; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Miyako Takebe, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and government policy for Dow Jones Newswires in Tokyo, has been a financial journalist in Japan for seven years.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (Lisa Twaronite in Tokyo, Sue Chang in Seoul and Chris Taylor in Taipei contributed to this item.) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc