\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Sep 24  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2003\par 11:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Hurt by cool, wet summer weather, Japanese economic activity fell sharply in July, government data released Wednesday showed. But the declines in key measures of business activity are likely temporary, and don't mean the end of Japan's recent economic resurgence, economists said. ("=WRAP: Chilly Weather Cools Japan Econ Activity In July" ran at 0403 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Key ministers of Japanese Prime Minister Koizumi's new Cabinet hold group interviews with media organizations, including reappointed bank/economy minister Takenaka and Finance Minister Tanigaki. (stories will run around 0930 GMT or later)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Poor Japan. Browbeaten by U.S. free-market purists, Tokyo was forced to accept a G7 call for freer \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then endure the resulting painful rise in the yen. That's the popular spin, anyway, but consider another possibility: maybe Japan got just what it wanted, too. Tokyo no longer has to defend an untenable and economically unnecessary Y115 floor for the dollar, it wins favor with the U.S. for joining the "flexible exchange rate" bandwagon, while remaining free to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at weaker-dollar levels to smooth out the yen's rise. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Got Its G7 Wish Too; Beware \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0802 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese government bond prices lost ground Wednesday on profit-taking after Monday's surge, but strategists expect big gains in the medium term if the yen continues to appreciate against the dollar. ("=WRAP: Japan Govt Bonds Slip But Further Gains Forecast" ran at 0855 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The People's Bank of China on Wednesday offered to buy back 20 billion yuan in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills, a move meant to ease extraordinary tightening in the yuan money markets in recent days. ("China PBOC Bills" ran at 0713 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Hong Kong dollar's unexpected rise against the U.S. dollar - the biggest since the 1997-1998 Asian financial crisis - sent a shiver of panic through traders and the HKMA, but many economists are arguing that the rise is a sign of returning confidence and incipient recovery for the economy. (story may run after 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 DUBAI: Singapore's economy is showing signs of recovery after a sharp contraction in the second quarter, but Second Finance Minister Lim Hng Kiang declined to comment on whether the improvement will prompt a monetary policy shift. Ben's interview on wire. ("=IMF/INTERVIEW:Singapore Econ Rebound Likely Underway-Min" ran at 0215 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: India's only asset reconstruction company, Arcil, is set to complete the acquisition of its first batch of bad banking loans this month in what regulators hope will be the first step in creating a market for distressed assets here. ("=INTERVIEW:Arcil Paves Way To India's First Bad Loans Mkt" ran at 0337 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: Australian fixed-income markets may have read too much into the G7's weekend statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with hopes that it will somehow prevent the Reserve Bank of Australia from hiking rates likely wide of the mark. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH:Markets' RBA Expectations Probably Wrong" ran at 0350 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy, the Bank of Korea, and other key financial agencies Wednesday agreed to raise the limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization bond issuance as part of the government's effort to secure funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. ("S Korea/\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market" first take ran at 0037 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Debt levels of South Korean companies fell on quarter in April-June, although government and personal debt both rose during the period, the Bank of Korea said Wednesday. At the end of the second quarter, South Korean non-financial companies' outstanding, interest-bearing domestic and foreign debt was down 0.2% on quarter to 697.6 trillion won. ("S Korea Non-Fincl Cos' 2Q Debt KRW697.6T,Down 0.2% On Qtr" ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The baht set a 38-month high against the dollar after the G7 markets over the weekend with a statement encouraging greater flexibility in exchange rates, and forecasters see only further gains ahead for the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA:Thai Baht Sure To Rise;How Much Depends On Tokyo" ran at 0348 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: As the U.S. dollar sinks against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market is debating how the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness will reshape interest rate swap and bond yield curves across the region. ("=ASIA DEBT: Weak Dlr Triggers IRS Curve Debate In Region" ran at 0418 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: House isn't a four-letter word but for Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Alan Bollard it may soon be. After just a year in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top job, Bollard is finding monetary policy a much more exacting art. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: RBNZ Growth Experiment Unravels" ran at 0232 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Fears of a voter backlash may prompt nervous New Zealand lawmakers to water down a proposed ban on smoking in bars and other licensed premises, Hospitality Association Chief Executive Bruce Robertson said Wednesday. ("=INTERVIEW: Backlash May Force NZ Smoking Ban Compromise" ran at 0148 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The International Bank for Reconstruction & Development, known as the World Bank, has issued a NZ$344 million three-year uridashi bond in the Japanese retail market, said Nikko Cordial, the Japanese retail arm of Citigroup, Wednesday. ("=World Bank/3Y NZ Uridashi" ran at 0141 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand gas and electricity networks company Powerco said investor demand was sufficiently strong for it to increase the amount of long-term debt the company has just raised through a U.S. private placement market to US$175 million from an initial US$125 million, company chairman Barry Upson told Dow Jones Wednesday. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Powerco Taps US Market, Plans Local Issue" ran at 0449 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar lost a little ground against the yen in Asia Wednesday but stayed well off its dramatic overnight lows, as market participants pondered where the pair's near-term bottom might lie. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0545 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's largest cotton textile producer Weiqiao Textile made a strong debut on Hong Kong's main board Wednesday to close at HK$9.45, up 11% from its initial public offering price of HK$8.50. ("China Weiqiao/Debut" first take ran at 0213 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: U.K. drinks giant Allied Domecq is reviewing its options after directors of takeover target Peter Lehmann Wines earlier Wednesday threw their support behind a rival offer by Switzerland-based Hess Group. ("Allied Domecq/ Lehmann" ran at 0140 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Resona Holdings, Japan's fifth-largest banking group, may post a huge group net loss for the fiscal half ending Sept. 30 as it is likely to accelerate its bad loan write-offs based on its ongoing asset reevaluations, according to a Nihon Keizai Shimbun report Wednesday. But analysts at credit rating agency Standard & Poor's didn't appear to be bothered by the report, saying the recent public fund injection appears sufficient for Resona to set aside needed reserves and maintain a sufficiently high capital adequacy. ("=WRAP:Resona May Post Huge Loss After Reevaluating Assets" ran at 0844 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The yen's rapid ascent has spooked some investors in Japan's stock market, but concerns about the impact on corporate Japan may be overblown, making the recent pullback a good chance to pick up stocks on the cheap, strategists say. ("=Yen's Surge Doesn't Faze Tokyo Stock Market Bulls" ran at 0837 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: The head of the world's largest contract chipmaker, Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co., said Wednesday that China will become the next giant in the semiconductor industry - but that could lead to the industry's next downturn in coming years, as too many new chip plants lead to overproduction and falling prices. ("Taiwan Semicon Mfg Chmn: China To Become Next Chip Giant" first take ran at 0733 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: SMBC Friend Securities, a midsize securities affiliate of Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp., says it will merge with Sumitomo Life Insurance affiliate Izumi Securities on April 1. ("SMBC Friend" first take ran at 0130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia moved a step closer Wednesday to sorting out a messy commercial dispute over a Central Java-based telecoms operator that has been running since the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis. Foreign and Indonesian partners in the company, PT Mitra Global Telekmounikasi Indonesia, or MGTI, have agreed to sell their stakes to a subsidiary of Saratoga Investama Sedaya, a local investment firm.("Telstra/ MGTI" first take ran at 2255 GMT; "Indosat/ Indonesia's MGTI" ran at 0211 GMT; WRAP will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Bridgestone says the recent fire at its Tochigi tire plant will knock Y2 billion off its group net profit forecast for the fiscal year to Dec. 31. ("Bridgestone" first take ran at 0631 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The timing for China to issue 3G licences remains unclear as a lengthy trial process keeps the global communications industry guessing about the network standard to be endorsed by the world's biggest mobile phone market. (story will run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's LG Electronics said Wednesday it expects to ship more than $500 million worth of plasma display panels overseas this year and expects shipments to double that in 2004. ("LG Elec/Plasma Display" first take ran at 0018 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Mainland Chinese steel companies have seen a meteoric rise in their Hong Kong-listed H-shares to six-year highs, but the party seems to be over with a bout of profit-taking stretching into the next couple of months. ("=Asian Stock Focus: China Steel Cos A Sell After Rally" ran at 0249 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Woori Finance Holdings said Wednesday it expects to list American Depositary Receipts on the New York Stock Exchange at the end of the month to improve corporate governance and accounting transparency. ("S Korea Woori Finance Expects NYSE ADR Listing End Sept" ran at 0336 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Telecommunications took a step closer to resuming its regional ambitions after its chief executive said it had "digested somewhat" its A$14 billion acquisition of Optus, and now has appetite for more. But analysts say any new investments aren't likely to be imminent. ("REPEAT:SingTel/Regional Expansion" ran at 0438 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Duke Energy may be the next U.S. utility to quit Australia after approaching investment banks about the possible sale of its gas pipelines and power stations. ("=WRAP: Duke Reviews A$1.5B Australian Assets,IPO Possible" ran at 0617 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Malaysia's mobile phone carrier Maxis Communications doesn't intend to match the price cuts offered by rivals like DiGi.Com and Celcom, said chief executive Jamaludin Ibrahim, but Maxis plans to launch more value-added plans that don't necessarily focus on price issues from the fourth quarter. ("=INTERVIEW: Malaysia Maxis" first take ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: StarHub will remain the sole pay-TV provider in Singapore after a government tender for another license failed to attract bidders at the close of Wednesday. ("Singapore/Pay-TV License" ran at 0534 GMT; WRAP will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Peter Ziegler's A$148 million-plus lawsuit against Australian entertainment group Village Roadshow revolves around a bonus payment upon the expiry of a consultancy contract. ("=Suit Against Village Roadshow Centers On Bonus Payment" ran at 0558 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Telecom Corp., New Zealand's biggest fixed- line telephone company, estimates that providing services to uneconomic customers cost it NZ$215.5 million in the financial year ending June 30, 2003 - a figure the Commerce Commission is likely to calculate as substantially lower before apportioning the cost among all carriers. ("Telecom NZ Estimates Uneconomic Customers Cost NZ$212.5M" ran at 0007 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Profitability at industrial firms in China continued to improve in the first eight months of 2003, with profits rising 52.3% on year to 501.5 billion yuan, official figures issued Wednesday show. ("China Jan-Aug Industrial Profits Rise 52.3% To CNY501.5B" ran at 0417 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Lee & Man Paper Manufacturing said Wednesday its 187.5 million share offer has been priced at HK$4.17 apiece, close to the upper end of a range set earlier between HK$3.33 to HK$4.50. The IPO will raise HK$751 million in net proceeds. ("HK Lee & Man Paper's IPO Priced At HK$4.17 Per Share" ran at 0242 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's third-largest mobile telephone provider SmarTone said Wednesday it is planning to cancel the entire amount in credit in its share premium account and transfer that credit to a contributed surplus account. SmarTone said yesterday it was giving out a special dividend of HK$3.50 a share, on top of plans for a 27-HK cent final dividend. ("HK SmarTone Plans To Cancel Share Premium Account" ran at 0405 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: India's largest engineering and construction group Larsen & Toubro expects revenue to grow 20% in the current financial year, boosted mainly by increased international orders, its chief financial officer said. ("=INTERVIEW India Larsen & Toubro Rev Up 20% This FY -CFO" ran at 0858 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: HCL Infosystems, India's biggest computer and telecommunications equipment maker, expects sales from rural areas to make up a fifth of revenues in the current financial year after slashing prices in this growing market. ("=INTERVIEW: HCL Banking On Rural India To Boost PC Sales" ran at 0601 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand based dairy multinational Fonterra Cooperative Group won't be selling its 19.9% stake in rural services company Wrightson to Craig Norgate - Fonterra's recently replaced chief executive. ("NZ's Fonterra Says Won't Sell Wrightson Stake To Norgate" ran at 2011 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Foreign companies are in no doubt about China's oil market potential, but they are reluctant to make hasty decisions to invest in China's oil retail business before they are sure about the rules of the games and their profit potential. (=China Energy Watch will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Western Australia state remains on track to harvest massive winter crops, including wheat, Colin Tutt, general manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at logistics concern Cooperative Bulk Handling Ltd., said Wednesday. ("=INTERVIEW: W Australia On Track For Massive Wheat Crop" ran at 0712 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030924dz9o000eg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
