\par\par \b \uc2 Jumpy yen traders plot new BoJ line of demarcation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 919 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2003\par 16:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Japan's 'line in the sand' for the dollar versus the yen may have moved lower, but most traders still believe the BoJ (Bank of Japan) has not gone away.  \par \par \uc2 As traders returned to their desks in Japan on Wednesday, after Tuesday's holiday there, the risks that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will return are rising, New York-based analysts and traders said. Some expect imminent action.  \par \par \uc2 "We think the BoJ will definitely be back. If they need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they need to do it this week," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Citibank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Most U.S.-based analysts agree that Japan needs to reestablish its desire for a weaker yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets soon, or traders could punish the dollar severely.  \par \par \uc2 The question is where Japan's tolerance level now sits.  \par \par \uc2 While traders are flying by the seat of their pants in a highly volatile market and still grasping for guidance on key levels, some think the long vaunted 115.0 yen line in the sand is now resistance rather than support.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan acts on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MoF) in the event of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 KURODA FIRES WARNING SHOT  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Haruhiko Kuroda, formerly \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat and now a special adviser to the cabinet, sent a warning signal to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. He said the yen's rise has been too rapid and too big, and added that there is no reason for the yen to rise further over the near term.  \par \par \uc2 Warning \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary, Kuroda added he would not be surprised if the dollar rises between 2 and 3 yen on the unwinding of long-yen positions.  \par \par \uc2 For roughly two years, the Bank of Japan (BoJ) acted to stop the dollar slipping much below the critical 115.0 yen mark in order to protect Japanese exporters' competitiveness. But the G7's call over the weekend for flexible exchange rates has altered that landscape.  \par \par \uc2 Widely interpreted as pressuring first Japan and, over the longer term, China, to desist from artificially weakening their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the G7 assertion that "more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable," dragged the dollar down to three-year lows around 110.93 yen on Tuesday. On Wednesday in New York, the dollar was trading at 111.64.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have pushed the greenback lower with no apparent resistance from the BoJ, which has seemingly stood back from the market for the past several sessions.  \par \par \uc2 But with every passing day that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is conspicuously absent, traders grow more nervous that a return could be imminent, which would whiplash the dollar higher against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "I think if we see dollar/yen below 110.30 yen, we should probably see some (BoJ) action and should see a break up to the 115.0 yen area," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital in Warren, New Jersey. "That's really where the bottom fell out," he said.  \par \par \uc2 After acting as a long-established support for the dollar, the now definitively broken 115.0 yen floor is becoming a near term resistance area.  \par \par \uc2 It's as good a guess as any, with so little guidance to go on. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  options positions are still in the process of shifting as investors get used to the dollar's new, weaker trading range. For the moment, thin clusters of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  options positions offer little guidance to spot traders.  \par \par \uc2 So although not flying completely without maps, the market is trading more intuitively than usual.  \par \par \uc2 Traders "are beginning to feel there is a line in the sand, that the MoF (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) hasn't walked away, but they are wondering where the line is," said Steven Englander chief North American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Barclays Capital in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "They are pretty sure it's not 105 yen or 107 yen either; that it's somewhere around 110 yen," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The lull before the return of many market participants on Wednesday did give "Japanese authorities time to regroup," after being caught somewhat by surprise with the sharpness of the dollar's descent against the yen, Englander added.  \par \par \uc2 "Now they have had a chance to think about it and try to lay out a plan. With Japan opening up for business again the odds that they will resist another push lower (of the dollar) are higher," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Fundamental reasons why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not shirk from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain. The world's second biggest economy can't easily shake off the disadvantage a stronger yen places on its exporters, analysts continue to insist. Some argue that the G7 communique's supposed signals that the yen should be allowed to firm were exaggerated by many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, who were already keen to sell dollars.  \par \par \uc2 That yen surge may have created a classic market overshoot, in which the dollar has been overly drubbed so far this week, with traders temporarily turning a blind eye to the very real threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The G7 official press release should be viewed as a "no change" document with strong overtones of face-saving statements, but instead it has become the holy grail of the dollar bears," wrote strategists with Banc of America Securities in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 (Gertrude Chavez in New York contributed to this report).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030924dz9o001wx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
