\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Japan move could pull a prop from under the dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2003\par 09:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 24 (Reuters) - An apparent shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance has analysts recalling 1999, when it stood aside to let the yen rise to 101 yen to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But some see chances of a slide in the dollar as even greater than they were then.  \par \par \uc2 A weekend statement by the Group of Seven industrial nations has done little to discourage this view.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets took the G7 statement as targeting Japan and China, but at the same time it cast a spotlight on the U.S. problem," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "If the United States seriously wants to adjust its current account deficit, the dollar would need to be around 80 yen," Saito said.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. economic fundamentals have worsened significantly since 1999, with its current account deficit swelling to record levels and a weak job market still posing a risk to recovery.  \par \par \uc2 This suggests that despite Washington's insistence that it still has a "strong dollar" policy, it may be worried that dollar strength hurts U.S. exporters, especially ahead of a presidential election late next year and with public support for President Geroge W. Bush at the lowest level since he took office.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the dollar fell to a three-year low of 110.91 yen compared with the 120 yen level in early August.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback extended its broad decline in the wake of the G7 statement, which said more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries or economic areas to promote adjustments in the financial system, based on market mechanisms.  \par \par \uc2 The statement was seen as a call to Japan and China to stop weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Now the market is wondering if Japanese authorities will change their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the appointment of a new finance minister, Sadakazu Tanigaki.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki is due to give interviews to news organisations on Wednesday. So far, he has said policy will not change and other officials have stressed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as it sees fit.  \par \par \uc2 Memories are is still fresh of when Haruhiko Kuroda took over as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main spokesman on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from Eisuke Sakakibara - dubbed "Mr Yen" because of his influence over the market - in July 1999, and caused a stir with a large volume of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his first week in the job.  \par \par \uc2 Kuroda was replaced in January by Zembei Mizoguchi.  \par \par \uc2 1999 DEJAVU?  \par \par \uc2 In the Japanese financial year that began in April 1999, when the Tokyo stock market was rising, foreign investors bought a net 11 trillion yen of Japanese shares (worth about $98 billion at today's exchange rate), pushing up the yen. The market's key Nikkei average reach a high of over 19,000 in that year.  \par \par \uc2 At that time, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curb the yen's rise so as to help Japanese exporters - the main engine of the nation's economic recovery - spending what was then a record 7.6 trillion yen. Officials worry that a strong yen hurts exporters and could derail the recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But it suddenly stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the dollar fell to the 101 yen level by the year-end. The reason Japan pulled back is thought to be U.S. criticism of its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when the rate was around 122 yen to the dollar, and a rise in Tokyo share prices which was attracting funds into Japan and easing concerns about the economy and exports.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors are again pouring in funds into Japanese assets, with net buying of around 6.4 trillion yen of stocks by mid-September, and last week the Nikkei hit 15-month highs.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  suddenly changed its game in 1999 and slowed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , letting the yen go as high as 101 yen, and the situation is very similar now," said Junya Tanase, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be unlikely to offset massive fund flows into Japanese assets and a current account surplus of roughly 1.0 to 1.5 trillion yen every month.  \par \par \uc2 Japan spent a record nine trillion yen in keeping the yen down in the first seven months of this year, although it stayed out of the market in August.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have changed its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to one of tolerating a gradual dollar fall while making sure it doesn't fall too sharply," UFJ Tsubasa Securities' Saito said.  \par \par \uc2 "But this is hard to do, as history has shown."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030924dz9o000rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
