\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Slumps More Than Two Yen, Ends Lower Against Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley and Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,238 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 23:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar dropped across the board Monday, plunging to its lowest level against the yen in nearly three years after the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations meeting over the weekend called for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had already been sold heavily going into the G-7 meeting in Dubai, United Arab Emirates, as investors were betting that Japanese monetary authorities wouldn't risk \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  before such a big political event, especially with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Liberal Democratic Party holding elections over the same weekend.  \par \par \uc2 That wager paid off when the G-7 communique urged "major countries or economic areas" to adopt "more flexibility" in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 This was viewed as a clear signal to Asian countries, in particular, to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate more against the dollar and ease back on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  competitively weak, observers say.  \par \par \uc2 When the market opened in Tokyo, the dollar fell to a 33-month low of 111.37 yen, a loss of about 6% from its intraday high Monday, Sept. 15. The dollar later reached multimonth lows against the major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well.  \par \par \uc2 The weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit U.S. equities and Treasurys, as well, over concerns that foreign investors would pull out of U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 Late Monday, the dollar was at 112.02 yen, significantly lower than 114.27 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.1480 after hitting a two-month high of $1.1506, up from $1.1371 late Friday. However, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also at 128.63 yen, down from 129.72 yen late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3546 francs, recovering from a six-week low of 1.3502 francs, but well below Friday's level of 1.3666 francs. Sterling was at $1.6505, up sharply from $1.6368 Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Another reflection of the sour sentiment toward the dollar Monday was in the surge in gold to within a whisker of fresh seven-year highs. Gold, priced in dollars, traditionally rises as the dollar weakens because it becomes cheaper to foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 That relationship had shown signs of breaking down in recent weeks, but it firmly reasserted itself Monday, with spot gold climbing to $387.70 a troy ounce before settling at the end of New York trading up $5.35 at $386.85 an ounce.  \par \par \uc2 Gold hit $388.50 an ounce in February. The last time it traded as high as that was seven years before.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which carries out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is expected to remain cautious about stepping in unless the dollar falls toward 110 yen. Further weakness is considered likely, according to analysts.  \par \par \uc2 That is because despite the relative strength of the U.S. economy, the U.S. budget deficit and current account deficits are huge, with the current account gap requiring around $2 billion of foreign capital every working day just to keep the dollar stable. Perhaps as importantly, there is a perception in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that the Bush administration is happy for the dollar to weaken.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said Monday, however, that there "has been no change in the U.S. strong dollar policy."  \par \par \uc2 But David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Bank PLC in London, said the G-7's implicit call for less dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and others, and more flexibility in exchange-rate regimes, aimed at China, represents a case of "strong-dollar policy rest-in-peace."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's sharp movement has prompted some analysts to lower their forecasts for the dollar. UBS Investment Bank views the G-7 communique effectively as a call for countries across the globe to take their fair share of a broad decline in the value of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 UBS said in a research piece that the "statement is strong enough" to move up its three-month forecast of 115 yen and six-month forecast of 110 yen, making them the one-month and three-month forecasts, respectively.  \par \par \uc2 "The other clear message from the G-7 is that the dollar's long-term trend remains downward," said UBS.  \par \par \uc2 Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist at Royal Bank of Scotland in New York, thinks the dollar will move into a lower range of between 105 yen and 110 yen.  \par \par \uc2 As the burden of the dollar's decline on global markets shifts toward Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and away from European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro should begin to ease back from the $1.15 area to around $1.10-$1.12, Bhagavatula said.  \par \par \uc2 However, several banks that have been bullish on the dollar still expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rebound, though even some of those lowered their target for the dollar's rise.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar bulls based their view, in part, on the expectation that Japanese authorities will step back in, especially once this week's World Bank and International Monetary Fund meetings in Dubai have ended.  \par \par \uc2 "We still believe the basic policy stance in Japan is the same and that they will stabilize the yen," said Hongyi Chen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York. "If [the] yen goes to 110 yen [to the dollar], it's going to hurt companies like Toyota and Canon, so I don't think the Japanese government will be able to tolerate it."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Sadakazu Tanigaki, Japan's incoming finance minister, said Monday he wouldn't rule out large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if exchange rates get drastically out of sync with economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of America expects the dollar to move back up into a range 113 yen to 117 yen after this week, with a forecast of 120 yen in a year.  \par \par \uc2 Yet even without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Chen believes the dollar will reach that level over the 12 months unless the structural and banking reform "takes hold" in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Even though the Chinese yuan and Hong Kong dollar are tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar's plunge Monday filtered through to both.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yuan, the dollar sank to a record one-year discount in the nondeliverable forward market. Market participants also noted that positions built up in nondeliverable forward markets hit record levels.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded down to a 2,650-point discount to the yuan's spot price, which is kept in a tight band, moving around 0.3% either side of a 8.2770-yuan midpoint.  \par \par \uc2 This reflects expectations China might tweak its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to allow the yuan to rise within the next 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 At 2,650 points the nondeliverable forward market implies a 3.3% strengthening in the yuan against the dollar - much greater than the leeway now permitted - to 8.012 yuan during the next year.  \par \par \uc2 And the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  uncharacteristically took no action Monday to prevent the dollar sliding sharply to around 7.7450 Hong Kong dollars from HKD7.7950. At the close of local trading, the dollar settled around HKD7.7468.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-2096;  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020030922dz9m0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
