\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Sep 22  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 11:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of East Asia's big export economies surged and stock prices tumbled Monday after the Group of Seven industrial nations urged the region's governments to accept \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. G7 finance ministers and central bankers, meeting in Dubai at the weekend, issued a statement calling for "more flexibility in exchange rates", in order to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system". By indicating it would accept a substantially weaker U.S. dollar, the G7 appeared to confirm the end of Washington's "strong dollar" policy of the late 1990s, which helped to underpin East Asia's economic recovery after the 1997 financial crisis as the U.S. sucked in Asian goods. ("=WRAP: G7 Policy Shift Boosts Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Hits Stks" ran at 0717 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Asia will continue to be the world's fastest- growing region, even with slightly stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but a sharp drop in the U.S. dollar could seriously dent the region's prospects, economists say. (story will run around 1015 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: The sharp fall in the U.S. dollar Monday stunned investors throughout Asia, triggering a sell-off of shares in Japan and other export-sensitive markets, but most analysts and fund managers don't expect a sustained outflow of funds from the region following the initial knee-jerk reaction. (=ASIA FUND VIEW will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Japanese monetary officials came to Dubai last week seeking to defend their aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  before their G7 counterparts. The meeting is over, and now they're heading home with their arguments intact, prepared to jump right back into the market if the dollar resumes its dive - as if there was little out of the ordinary.("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Makes Clear It Plans To Keep \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 2359 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The one-year US dollar/yuan non-deliverable forward discount widened to as much as 2400 early Monday, following the weekend G7 statement calling for increased flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes to underpin a balanced global economic recovery. Current level implies in one year's time one USD to fetch CNY8.0374 vs spot rate of 8.2774 currently. The latest bid-offer on the NDF was at 2450/2250, according to Prebon Yamane. ("USD/CNY NDF" first take ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator Monday reaffirmed their support for China's stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the face of mounting pressure ahead of the G7 meeting in Dubai for a loosening of the yuan exchange rate. ("China/\b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy" first take ran at 0217 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi unveiled a new Cabinet lineup Monday, pleasing financial markets by keeping reform-minded Heizo Takenaka as both banking and economy minister, and appointing well-respected Sadakazu Tanigaki as the country's new finance minister. (first WRAP will run around 0915 GMT; second WRAP will run after 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa indicated Monday Japan will watch \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets closely amid a sharp rise in the yen, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said. Emerging from a meeting with Shiokawa, Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's International Bureau, told reporters the minister had told him to "keep a close eye on the markets." ("Japan MOF" ran at 0205 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Moody's Investors Service Monday placed China on review for possible upgrade on its sovereign ratings, a move that further boosts already strong prospects for the country's long-awaited return to the international debt market in less than a month. (WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: An executive at Citigroup's Citibank expressed support Monday for the People's Bank of China's long-term plans to widen the yuan trading band and liberalize the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange market. (INTERVIEW will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa ended a summer of political turmoil by naming Liberal Party deputy leader Selina Chow to the Executive Council and hinting he won't change the makeup of his Cabinet or introduce new security legislation until after legislative elections 12 months from now. ("HK Tung/Liberal Party's Chow" first take ran at 0430 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Dollar-yen options prices have exploded as the Tokyo authorities let the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crash through their presumed line of defense, but this may offer a rare selling opportunity. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views: USD/JPY Vol Explosion A Chance To Sell?" ran at 0117 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's merchandise trade surplus expanded much faster than expected in August, rising 23.1% from a year earlier to Y787.2 billion, the Finance Ministry said Monday, as growth in exports outstripped growth in imports. ("Japan Aug Trade Surplus" first take ran at 0003 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The Australian economy should grow 3.2% in 2003-04, aided by a rebound in Australia's farm sector, ongoing growth in consumer spending, and a rise in business investment, the government's chief commodities forecaster said Monday. ("Australia Abare Keeps FY04 Econ Growth Forecast At 3.2%" ran at 0044 GMT; and "=Australian Commodity Exports To Continue Sliding" ran at 0824 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WUJIANG, JIANGSU province: Trade in this eastern Chinese city is set to double this year and its economic growth rate will be twice the national average thanks to a heavy presence of Taiwanese technology firms, a senior local government official said. ("=INTERVIEW: Taiwan Cos Underpin Growth In Wujiang, China" ran at 0558 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: Lured by low-cost manufacturing, many Taiwanese technology giants have taken up residence in the rapidly industrializing triangle of countryside surrounding Shanghai. Yet a little more than a decade since Taiwan and China began developing an economic relationship, Taiwanese expatriates in China have yet to penetrate deeply into the social fabric of a community that - at least on the surface - shares the same culture and speaks the same language. ("CHINA WATCH:Yangtze Not Home And Hearth For Taiwan Techs," ran at 0845 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Australian Treasurer Peter Costello said Sunday the global economic outlook appears more promising, however signs of a recovery remain tentative. ("Australia's Costello: Global Economic Outlook Brighter" ran at 0202 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted back to neutral gear Monday after last month's effective monetary policy tightening appeared to have found its target. ("=WRAP: Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Keeps Policy Rates Steady" ran at 0836 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's jobless rate rose to 5.21% in August from 5.16% in July, boosted by college graduates entering the job market seeking employment, the government said Monday. ("Taiwan Aug Jobless" first take ran at 0816 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand house price inflation continued to accelerate in August with the median sale price up 16.2% from a year earlier, according to Real Estate Institute Inc. figures published Monday. ("NZ's Aug House Prices" ran at 0230 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand interest rates will have to rise if inflationary pressures from the housing market don't abate, Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Alan Bollard said Saturday as he continued a campaign to quell property speculation. ("NZ Bollard: Rates To Rise If House Pressures Don't Abate" ran at 2025 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Inflation in India is expected to inch higher in the fiscal third quarter, spanning October to December, due to higher prices of manufactured goods, according to economists polled by Dow Jones. Despite a slight kindling in inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to maintain its soft monetary bias, say analysts. ("=India Inflation Expected To Inch Higher In FY 3Q - Poll" ran at 0843 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KARACHI: Pakistan's proposed record PKR50 billion sale of government bonds over the next three months will help soak up excess money in the banking system and keep inflation under control, a senior government official said Monday. ("=Pakistan's Record Bond Sale To Refinance Debt,Deepen Mkt" ran at 0846 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar plunged to a 33-month low against the yen in Asia Monday after G7 finance ministers urged more flexibility in exchange rates - a call that seemed aimed at beating back \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0550 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Blue-chip port operator and container leasing company COSCO Pacific reported Monday a lower-than-expected first-half net profit of US$73.6 million, up 5.4% from the year-earlier period. More details at a press conference starting at 0930 GMT. ("REPEAT&CORRECT: HK Cosco Pacific 1H Net +5.4% To US$73.6M" ran at 0735 GMT; WRAP will run around 1200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: U.K. beverage giant Allied Domecq on Monday finally entered the race for Peter Lehmann Wines, lodging a A$143.6 million cash bid for the Australian winemaker, eclipsing an existing offer by Switzerland-based Hess Group. ("Peter Lehamnn/Allied" first take ran at 0641 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Workers at Taiwan's largest phone company, Chunghwa Telecom, plan to surround the Legislature Tuesday to protest the company's privatization plans, which Chunghwa's union says will benefit business interests at the employees' expense. The union claims that more 5,000 workers and their fmailies will show up at the event, but Chunghwa Monday estimated the number at no more than 2,000. (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Hino Motors has set up a joint venture in China to produce diesel engines with a local partner in Shanghai, aiming to meet growing demand for trucks in the rapidly growing auto market with its advanced technology for environmentally friendly engines. (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Electric on Monday more than doubled its group net profit forecast for the fiscal year through March, citing stronger-than-expected sales of telecom systems and industrial electronics in Asia and Japan. ("Mitsubishi Elec/Pft" ran at 0502 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DRAM REPORT: Samsung Electronics' ramp up of flash memory chip production should provide much-needed relief for second-tier dynamic random access memory chipmakers, and should be good news for the DRAM industry as a whole, analysts say ("Asia DRAM Report:Samsung's Flash Boost To Help DRAM Mkt" ran at 0735 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Samsung Electronics said Monday it is reviewing plans to set up a liquid crystal display joint venture with Sony Corp. of Japan, but said "nothing has been determined at this time." ("S Korea Samsung Elec: "Reviewing" LCD Tie-Up With Sony" ran at 0059 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Japan's second largest-steel maker, JFE Holdings and China's Guangzhou Iron & Steel Enterprises Group plan to set up a steel joint venture, according to an official at the Guangzhou government news office. The venture will produce steel sheets for automobiles and high-end household appliances, with annual capacity of 400,000 metric tons. ("Guangzhou Iron & Steel, Japan's JFE Plan China Steel JV" ran at 0543 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's stock market regulator Monday issued rules tightening requirements for companies looking to raise money through initial public offerings, with the aim of improving the quality of firms listing on the country's exchanges. ("China/IPO Rules" first take ran at 0151 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: If you missed the rbally, forget it. Shares of Scomi Group, a Malaysian oil and gas services company, are now so expensive that the only place the price is going is down, analysts say. ("=ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Malaysia's Scomi Gets A Thumbs Down" ran at 0037 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Orient Overseas' minority shareholders Monday approved the shipping company's buyback of a 9.08% stake from companies controlled by businessman Li Ka-shing for HK$460.2 million in cash. Orient Overseas is controlled by the family of Hong Kong Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa. ("HK Orient Overseas/Buyback" first take ran at 0400 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's largest minibus maker Brilliance China Monday said its first-half net profit almost doubled to a forecast-beating CNY574 million from CNY289.6 million a year ago, helped by a full six-month contribution from sales of its domestic-branded sedans and a rebound in the minibus market. ("HK Brilliance China/Earnings" first take ran at 0832 GMT; WRAP will run around 1130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Piped-gas distributor Xinao Gas said Monday its first-half net profit rose 62% on year to 65.5 million yuan as revenue doubled due to continued expansion of its gas-connection service in China. ("China Xinao Gas/Earnings" first take ran at 0657 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shareholders of Li & Fung Monday overwhelmingly approved the purchase of the remaining one-third interest in a garment importer the trading firm doesn't already own, for US$5.22 million. ("("HK Li & Fung/Garment Importer JV" first take ran at 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: International airport operator Macquarie Airports said Monday that traffic at Sydney Airport rose 3.6% in August from a year ago. ("Macquarie Airports/Sydney Airport" first take ran at 0022 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian clothing, footwear and music retailer said Monday it has sold its U.K. music business Sanity Entertainment (UK) Ltd. to Primemist for A$12 million. The move is in line with the group's strategy to exit the U.K. music business and refocus the company's resources on low-risk businesses. ("Australia's Brazin/Sanity UK" first take ran at 0010 GMT; "=INTERVIEW: Brazin To Expand Australian Ops After UK Exit" ran at 0408 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's Woosh Wireless, a high-speed Internet service provider, says the New Zealand Commerce Commission's decision on opening access to Telecom Corp.'s local telephone and data networks won't hurt its current expansion, but may set a bad precedent. ("=INTERVIEW: Woosh Says NZCC Ruling May Set Bad Precedent" ran at 0346 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand transport company Mainfreight has raised its takeover bid for competitor Owens Group to NZ$1.10 a share from NZ$1.03. Mainfreight said in a statement Monday that the Owens board has "unanimously recommended that shareholders accept the new Mainfreight offer." ("Mainfreight/Raises Offer" ran at 2229 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Samart Corp., a Thai telecommunications service provider, is seeking further growth within the currently expanding telecommunication sectors in Thailand as well as Southeast Asia region after reducing its debt burden, executive vice chairman Pairote Varophas told Dow Jones. (INTERVIEW will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Foreign fund managers are likely to increase weightings in Malaysia in the near term, adding as much as 70 billion ringgit to the Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange, said an ING Financial Markets senior official. ("=REPEAT:INTERVIEW:Foreign Funds In Malaysia To Rise -ING" ran at 0423 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's LG Electronics said Monday its total revenue in August rose 26% on year 1.55 trillion won due in part to robust sales of mobile handsets. ("S Korea LG Elec Aug Rev Up 26% At KRW1.55T On Handsets" ran at 0225 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China imported slightly less crude oil in August than July, down 0.6 % at 6.79 million metric tons, and import levels are expected to continue to be eroded for the rest of the year. ("=WRAP: China Aug Crude Imports -0.6% On Mo; +26% Jan-Aug" first take ran at 0318 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's major oil refiners plan over the next five years to reduce the number of their service stations by as much as 20% in an effort to combat a severe business environment in the domestic retail market. ("Japan Oil Cos To Close 20% Of Service Stations In 5 Yrs" ran at 0740 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: A recent wave of entrants to the fuel oil market in Huangpu, south China, will be an opportunity rather than a threat, according to a senior official from a major fuel oil importing company based in southern China. ("=INTERVIEW: Guanxi Is Key To China Fuel Oil Mkt - Twinace" ran at 0243 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030922dz9m000h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
