\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Ends Flat; NDF Discount Widens Sharply Post-G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 10:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan closed unchanged Monday in active trade, but the offshore market for dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards reacted sharply to a call by the Group of Seven leading industrial nations over the weekend for greater flexibility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 The one-year USD/CNY NDF rose to record levels during the session, signaling expectations of a stronger yuan in one year's time.  \par \par \uc2 In the spot market, the U.S. dollar closed unchanged at CNY8.2773 Monday, after trading between CNY8.2768 and CNY8.2774 during the session.  \par \par \uc2 Trading volume reached $498.3 million Monday, traders said, up from $350 million during Friday's session.  \par \par \uc2 The bid-offer quote for the one-year USD/CNY NDF touched a record discount of 2450-2350 to the yuan late Monday afternoon, compared with 1920-1870 late Friday afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 The NDF market doesn't officially close and the trades aren't tallied at a central exchange. During Monday's session, the bid on the one-year USD/CNY NDF discount was quoted as high as 2650, though actual trades were only done around 2250, 2350 and at one point 2400, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The trade at 2400 was made early in Monday's session, and implied the U.S. dollar would fetch 8.0373 yuan in one year, representing a 3% appreciation in the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 In a statement over the weekend, G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors urged "major countries or economic areas" to adopt "more flexibility" in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate regimes in order to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen also rose sharply Monday, dragging its regional peers with it.  \par \par \uc2 A trader at a Chinese bank in Shenzhen said the lack of major \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the strength in the yen fueled speculation China may loosen its control over the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Late in Asia Monday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at Y112.26, down sharply from Y114.27 late Friday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 While the spot rate of the U.S. dollar against the yuan remained steady, other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose in line with the overall dollar-selling sentiment in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar rose to CNY1.0611 from CNY1.0610. The Japanese yen strengthened to CNY7.3553 per 100 yen from CNY7.1748, and the euro rose to CNY9.4700 from CNY9.3220.  \par \par \uc2 Domestic traders said the one-year dollar-yuan NDF discount is expected to narrow in the next few days as China's top regulators repeatedly reaffirm their support of the current yuan policy.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the NDF trades Monday were likely due to punting by day traders, rather than a belief China will succumb to international pressure to let the yuan float.  \par \par \uc2 The NDFs don't have a direct influence on the spot dollar-yuan rate, but reflect market psychology about exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 China tightly controls the flow of capital across its borders, allowing only convertibility on the current account. Rarely does the spot market jolt on news, even if related to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218 3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 1153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030922dz9m000fx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic