\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: G7 Policy Shift Boosts Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , Hits Stks  \b0\par\par\uc2 By William Mallard  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,007 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 09:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of East Asia's big export economies surged and stock prices tumbled Monday after the Group of Seven industrial nations urged the region's governments to accept \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and central bankers, meeting in Dubai at the weekend, issued a statement calling for "more flexibility in exchange rates", in order to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system".  \par \par \uc2 Although it didn't mention specific countries by name, the statement took direct aim at the policies of Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, China and some other Asian economies, which have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets or structured their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  systems to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  relatively weak and thus boost exports.  \par \par \uc2 By indicating it would accept a substantially weaker U.S. dollar, the G7 appeared to confirm the end of Washington's "strong dollar" policy of the late 1990s, which helped to underpin East Asia's economic recovery after the 1997 financial crisis as the U.S. sucked in Asian goods.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts drew parallels between the G7's statement and the Plaza Accord of 1985, in which major nations reached an understanding that ushered in a major period of decline for the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 statement "supports our view the yen and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to participate more actively in broad dollar weakness in the next few months," said UBS Warburg.  \par \par \uc2 It said the G7 was essentially calling on Asia to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, which was "a key change for the G7", although the statement couldn't stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  completely.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts also noted the G7's policy switch risked turning the "smooth adjustment" which the group desired into an uncontrolled dollar collapse, which could damage the world economy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's fall Monday "was pretty much inevitable after the G7 statement - it was a very significant development for the dollar," said Masamichi Koike, head \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Sumitomo-Mitsui Bank in Tokyo. "It's going to be hard to change the dollar's downward momentum now."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which had already sagged Y3.5 last week, plunged in early Asian trade Monday to as low as Y111.37 - its lowest level since December 2000 - from Y114.27 late Friday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then rebounded slightly, to around 112.50 in the Asian afternoon on Monday, as Japanese officials warned they would act against excessive volatility and some traders said they detected covert Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't nearly strong enough to support a major dollar recovery, and many traders said they expected further dollar weakness in coming weeks and months, perhaps to around Y105 or lower.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won climbed to a 34-month high against the U.S. dollar Monday, while the Taiwan dollar hit a 13-month high. Although the central banks of both countries were detected overtly or covertly buying U.S. dollars to slow the moves, and the Korean authorities warned against "inappropriate" won appreciation, many traders doubt Seoul and Taipei will be able to restrain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen continues rising.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of sharply higher \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent shockwaves through East Asian stock markets; shares of exporters in particular suffered as investors worried their shipments to the U.S. and corporate profits would be damaged. The Nikkei 225 Stock Average closed 4.2% lower, while Seoul's Kospi lost 4.5% and Taiwan's main stock index slipped 1.4%.  \par \par \uc2 Bond yields in all three countries fell on the belief that the negative economic impact of a weaker U.S. dollar would force authorities to keep monetary policy loose for longer.  \par \par \uc2 China's markets were relatively quiet Monday because the yuan is effectively pegged to the dollar. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator insisted Beijing's stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy wouldn't change despite G7 pressure.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanisms and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy are a country's internal affairs in which other countries are not entitled to interfere," said the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the dollar sank in the market for forward delivery against the yuan, which can be used in place of the spot market to make bets on the yuan's long-term direction. The dollar's one-year discount steepened to a record 2400 points at one stage, implying expectations of the dollar falling to 8.0374 yuan over the next 12 months, a nearly 3% drop from the tight spot range that Beijing now allows around CNY8.277.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow spent much of a week-long Asian trip early this month trying to coax Beijing to uncouple the yuan from the dollar. Although he failed to win any public commitment to a specific time frame, traders were betting Monday that G7 pressure would hasten some degree of reform by China - perhaps a modest widening next year of the yuan's trading band, which would allow appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The G7's statement appeared to be a diplomatic victory for Snow, who has been pressing Asian countries to appreciate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to raise demand for U.S. exports. The administration of George W. Bush, struggling with high joblessness in the run-up to an election year, is fielding complaints from U.S. manufacturers and lawmakers about the U.S. trade deficit with Asia.  \par \par \uc2 A draft statement circulated among the G7 - which comprises Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, the U.K. and the U.S. - was relatively weak when seen by Dow Jones Newswires Friday; it didn't mention "more flexibility in exchange rates" and didn't call for "widespread" adjustments.  \par \par \uc2 By the time the final statement was issued on Saturday, the U.S. appeared to have pressed the G7 to toughen its call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By William Mallard, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4041; billy.mallard@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Andrew Torchia  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030922dz9m000df\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
