\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Bonds End Sharply Higher On Yen's Strength  \b0\par\par\uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 08:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bonds finished sharply higher Monday after the yen soared against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The bonds started higher in the morning and remained solid on buying by investors such as pension funds, as the yen soared over Y2 against the dollar since Friday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose sharply after financial officials from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations urged more flexibility in exchange rates, which was interpreted as a call for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Lead December 10-year JGB futures finished 1.45 higher at 138.50. The 10-year yield fell 16.5 basis points to 1.225% after falling as low as 1.22%, its lowest since Aug. 18. The 20-year yield also fell sharply, dropping 21 basis points to 1.62%, its lowest since Aug. 14.  \par \par \uc2 Reaction to the new Cabinet lineup was somewhat limited, as "it was already reported over the weekend that key ministers, such as (Heizo) Takenaka, were to stay," said a senior manager at a securities house.  \par \par \uc2 Takenaka kept both his banking minister and economy minister posts, although previous media reports said he would lose one of the posts.  \par \par \uc2 Sadakazu Tanigaki, former minister in charge of industrial rehabilitation became Finance Minister. The post had become a market focus as previous minister Masajuro Shiokawa had said he would resign.  \par \par \uc2 The new Japanese Cabinet lineup is "likely viewed positively by the JGB market," said UBS chief strategist Eiji Dohke. The Takenaka and Tanigaki appointments are good news for fiscal reforms, and they don't point to a policy shift, he said.  \par \par \uc2 But echoing views of many in the market Monday, "the main focus was on the yen's rise today," Dohke said, to which the bonds reacted in a conventional way.  \par \par \uc2 Stock weakness Monday also supported JGBs throughout the day, traders said. The Nikkei 225 Stock Average closed down 4.2% or 463.32 at 10475.10, marking the largest fall in two years in terms of points lost.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Prices as of 0600 GMT: \par  \par    Tiffe Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:   99.830  Up 0.010 \par    SGX Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:     99.825  Up 0.005 \par    TSE Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:  138.47  Up 1.41 \par    SGX Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:  138.50  Up 1.45 \par    10-Yr 1.6% JGB No. 253 Yield:    1.225%  Dn 0.165 \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lead futures' rise of 1.45 by the market close marked the largest single-day jump since Feb. 9, 1999.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although pension funds were buying Monday, city banks were limited buyers of JGBs ahead of the book-closing for the fiscal first half at the end of this month. The securities house senior manager said it is unlikely there will be much more buying before the start of the fiscal second half.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The general view in the JGB market is that it's still too early for the 10-year yield to move in a 1.5%-2.0% range. It's still 1%-1.5%, but I think the yield will stay near the upper end of that range," the senior trader said. "So the current level - in the low-1.20% range - may be good enough for now."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond players will be keeping a close eye on stocks and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels, as the new Cabinet lineup is also likely to be welcomed by stock investors, while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could well enter the market to prop up the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yen interest rate swaps fell further on the JGB gains. Swaps fell again near the end of the session, with the new finance minister making clear that he will continue to pursue fiscal reforms. The seven-year swaps stood at 1.0-1.025% vs 1.1325% Friday, while the three-to-four year spread was 15.0%-15.5%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Asako Tanabe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; asako.tanabe@dowjones.com (With contributions from Hiroshi Miwa)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by John Gibson and Hugh Lawson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020030922dz9m00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
