\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Strong dollar policy laid to rest by G7 language shift.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry and Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK/CHICAGO (Reuters) - The Group of Seven's weekend communique on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  put to rest any lingering thoughts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have had that Washington's strong dollar policy still had some life, U.S.-based traders say.  \par \par \uc2 The wording of the G7 statement on the desirability of "more flexibility in exchange rates" for major countries has increased the pressure on the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut back on its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As the BoJ has apparently stepped back from the market over the past few days, according to traders, that has allowed the dollar to slip sharply against the yen, causing a broader dollar relapse against other major rivals.  \par \par \uc2 "For a long time, the United States has been moving away from a strong dollar policy. This (the G7 statement) is definitely another nail in the coffin," said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The so-called "strong dollar policy," created by former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, has been gradually toned down by his successors, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts.  \par \par \uc2 The rationale for that policy, conceived during a period of robust U.S. economic growth, was that a strong dollar was compatible with strong economic performance. But as the pace of economic activity slowed in the United States, that logic began to look shaky.  \par \par \uc2 Under current U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, the policy has been further watered down, although Snow was at pains on Monday to deny that it has has been altered.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking from Dubai, Snow denied a shift in the policy.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no change in the strong-dollar policy," Snow told reporters when asked whether a G7 statement represented a shift in U.S. policy.  \par \par \uc2 Snow refused to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements since the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers are highly skeptical by this stage, especially in light of the extent of dollar weakness since the G7 statement over the weekend. On Monday, the dollar shed nearly 2 percent against the yen and 1 percent against the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 "Obviously most people are reading between the lines. That's something he has to say," said one New York-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader.  \par \par \uc2 "They probably feel that an orderly devaluation of the dollar would help not only the U.S., but would get the global (economy) going."  \par \par \uc2 Snow is paying lip service to the dollar in defense of the U.S. bond market, which would suffer if Washington formally abandoned the strong dollar policy, the trader added.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. administration is more than cognizant of the damage it would cause to the U.S. capital markets if it were to declare a change to the strong dollar policy," said Jeremy Fand, senior proprietary trader in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with WestLB in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But a weaker dollar is still a desirable goal for Washington, behind the scenes, at least.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think there is question in the market's mind that a weaker dollar is one of the tools the U.S. administration hopes to use to engineer very strong growth and better its election chances. I think that has been there all year. I don't think G7 has done anything but subtley reconfirm that," said Fand.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. manufacturers have criticized the strong dollar policy as an impediement to U.S. exporters' competitiveness. Some analysts interpret the G7 communique's reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an implicit acknowledgement by the Bush Administration of American industry's position.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the U.S. administration was able to engineer the headline it wanted and now has the ability to say they are trying to do something about manufacturing job losses," said Fand.  \par \par \uc2 Treasuries were in retreat on Monday as the market fretted that a sharp fall in the dollar could scare foreign investors, and perhaps even central banks, away from U.S. assets. Asian central banks have been massive buyers of Treasuries and agencies in recent years as they recycle trade surpluses.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we changed policy over the weekend," said Bob Priore, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk manager at Carr Futures in Chicago. "They probably didn't say that they're reversing the strong dollar policy. But it's pretty clear to me that they did."  \par \par \uc2 Others were even more definitive about the policy's demise, although they warned that this does not mean \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will completely desist from its hefty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has used around $70 billion so far this year to buoy the dollar against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The strong dollar policy is now officially declared dead and the dollar index will correct lower. But I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  - especially because if all of Asia cease to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it will certainly lead to the collapse of the dollar," said Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Morgan Stanley in London. (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases and Wayne Cole in New York and Natsuko Waki in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030922dz9m009xk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
