\par\par \b \uc2 COMEX gold surges on G7 flexible  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  call.  \b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 22:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 22 (Reuters) - COMEX gold ended Monday near the top of a new higher trading zone as dealers said they were responding to a Group of Seven communique calling for flexible exchange rates as a concerted effort to weaken the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Today, for the most part, it's the old story from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That was basically the whole thing," said James Pogoda, Mitsubishi International Corp. vice president of precious metals.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said funds jumped in to buy and drove gold up to their strong closing levels.  \par \par \uc2 December gold pushed up to $388.30 to close $5.40 an ounce higher, after setting its range in overnight trade at seven-month highs between $384 to $389.10 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Other gold futures settled with gains from $5.30 to $6.20. Several COMEX contracts finished with new lifetime highs.  \par \par \uc2 So-called G7 industrialized nations issued a weekend communique from their summit in Dubai that stressed exchange rate flexibility. Analysts read the statement as a greenlight to sell dollar/yen, and as criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak to benefit its exports.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar/yen tumbled to lows not seen since December 2000 and the dollar slipped to lows against the euro set in July.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar-denominated gold's value appreciates in foreign markets when the dollar deteriorates.  \par \par \uc2 COMEX gold volume was estimated at a healthy 47,000 contracts on Monday, compared with 57,980 trades on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they look for $389 to serve as first resistance for December COMEX gold, then $391, the high from March 1996, on its way to $400 an ounce.  \par \par \uc2 Although gold enjoyed its latest price gains on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, some participants worry that the hefty net long gold positions outstanding could undermine its ascent.  \par \par \uc2 The CFTC delayed its Commitment of Traders report until Monday afternoon because of the impact from the storm Isabel.  \par \par \uc2 As of September 16, it said non-commerical net long positions fell to 105,818 from 115,092 in the prior week.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's open interest in gold rose by 8,649 to 291,043.  \par \par \uc2 Also spurring gold prices, dealers said, was a Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official's comment from Dubai that the bank plans to raise gold and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $65 billion by year end, from $62 billion currently, according to Russian news service Interfax.  \par \par \uc2 "You've got someone who may be sucking up some of this gold that would normally come out onto the market," Pogoda said.  \par \par \uc2 Some players thought finance officials' preliminary meeting on a new gold pact at the International Monetary Fund meetings in Dubai had underpinned gold prices, others were skeptical and said it merely confirmed what was widely expected anyway.  \par \par \uc2 On approach to its seven-year high at $388.50 an ounce, spot gold bullion rallied to $387.00/7.50 by late Monday, up from $381.60/2.30 on Friday. London bullion dealers fixed the afternoon spot reference price at $385.50 an ounce.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Silver prices fell victim to profit. December COMEX silver  ended 1.70\par cents lower at $5.275 an ounce, in a $5.245 and $5.37 range. Spot\par silver        edged lower to $5.23/5.25 on Monday, down from $5.24/26\par on Friday. The fix was $5.31.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NYMEX October platinum rose $7.40 to $703.70 an ounce. Spot platinum was last at $703.00/708.00.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par December palladium  eased $2.75 to $220.00. Spot palladium        was\par unchanged at $215.50/221.50 an ounce.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030922dz9m009q1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
