\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-No change to U.S. strong-dollar policy, Snow says.  \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 19:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 DUBAI, Sept 22 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow on Monday brushed off questions about sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings after a weekend Group of Seven meeting and insisted no change had been made in U.S. dollar policy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar weakened to near three-year lows against Japan's yen on Monday in the wake of a statement by G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors calling for greater flexibility in how \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are determined.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also weakened against the euro, Swiss franc and pound sterling.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm not going to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels or day-to-day fluctuations in any prices or indices of economic value," Snow said in response to questions from a small group of reporters on the sidelines of the International Monetary Fund's annual meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers interpreted Saturday's G7 statement as the final abandonment of a previous Washington position that a strong dollar was in the U.S. interest.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about that view, Snow replied: "There's no change in the strong-dollar policy."  \par \par \uc2 The G7 brings together the United States, Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 At the Dubai meeting, the ministers said they would prefer to see more exchange-rate flexibility to help smooth global imbalances - a statement widely read as criticism of Asian economies including China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down and so gain an export advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Even before the Dubai communique, many dealers had concluded that Snow and the Bush administration were subtly modifying the mantra that a strong dollar was in the U.S. interest.  \par \par \uc2 In May at a G7 meeting in Deauville, France, Snow set a new definition of a strong dollar that emphasised qualities besides its value.  \par \par \uc2 "What you want to be strong is (that) you want people to have confidence in your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you want them to see a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a good medium of exchange. You want the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be a good store of value. You want it to be something people are willing to hold. You want it to be hard to counterfeit....those are the qualities," Snow said then.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's value, he said, "represents the fundamentals of the demand and supply for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".  \par \par \uc2 Since that time, the administration has said repeatedly that Snow was not implying a change in U.S. policy of supporting a strong dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030922dz9m009hm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
