\par\par \b \uc2 COMEX gold jumps on G7 call for flexible  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 16:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 22 (Reuters) - COMEX gold prices surged Monday morning to play catch-up with the higher levels established overnight, when traders interpreted a Group of Seven call for flexible exchange rates as a concerted effort to weaken the dollar, gold dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's all \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  related. There's nothing new for this latest spurt. We rallied on the news overnight. And even though we (gold) opened higher, it got sluggish right after that," said on COMEX floor broker.  \par \par \uc2 The so-called G7 industrialized nations issued a weekend communique from their summit in Dubai, that stressed exchange rate flexibility. Analysts read the statement as a green light to sell dollar/yen, and as criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak to benefit its exports.  \par \par \uc2 While December gold eased off the overnight highs to trade up $3.90 at $386.8 an ounce, it clearly established its range at a new plateau between $384 to $389.10 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they expect $389 to serve as first resistance level for gold, then $391, the high from March 1996.  \par \par \uc2 After COMEX gold's open, the dollar added to losses against the euro and yen. Weakness in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  improves dollar-denominated gold's value in overseas markets.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to the weekend \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  news, finance officials at the International Monetary Fund meeting in Dubai had said that while no new gold pact would be set at the weekend, preliminaries were discussed on the sidelines.  \par \par \uc2 Gold headed toward its 7-month high after initial brief IMF talks on renewal of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gold sale pact, that expires next year, were held at the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 While Dutch \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Nout Wellink told Reuters on Sunday that a new deal would be covered properly next year, expectations arose among gold dealers that Europe's Cental Banks would renew the pact to limit gold sales from their vaults for another five years.  \par \par \uc2 Central banks worldwide hold more than 32,000 tonnes of gold, enough to feed world demand for about 12 years. The expiring pact limits gold sales by 15 central banks to 400 tonnes a year until September 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Spot bullion was last quoted at $384.20/4.70 an ounce, up from $381.60/2.30 late Friday. London bullion dealers fixed the spot reference price at $385.50.  \par \par \uc2 Silver prices surged in response to the soaring gold prices, then a rapid round of profit taking knocked it lower.  \par \par \uc2 December silver was down 2.2 cents at $5.27 an ounce, trading $5.27 to $5.37. COMEX silver rose to a 3-year high at $5.38 on Sept 12.  \par \par \uc2 Spot silver slipped to $5.22/5.24 on Monday from $5.24/26 on Friday. The fix was $5.31.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NYMEX October platinum  rose $4.70 to $701.00 an ounce. Spot        was\par last indicated at $703.00/708.00.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December palladium eased $0.25 to $222.50 an ounce. Spot palladium was unchanged at $215.50/221.50.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030922dz9m0095s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
