\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Europe gold near Feb high, weak dollar spurs funds.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Veronica Brown  \par \uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 17:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Gold jumped in Europe on Monday and was poised to conquer a seven-year high after a G7 call for flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes and on expectations that central banks will maintain restraint in selling off the bullion stored in their vaults.  \par \par \uc2 In other precious metals, the breakout in gold ignited silver, with the market moving earlier towards fresh 3-1/2 year highs above $5.30 an ounce.  \par \par \uc2 In Asia, the gold market was just a whisker away from the 6-1/2 year high scored on February 5 at $388.50 an ounce as speculators, already enamoured of the metal's safe-haven appeal, piled into the market as a weaker dollar strengthened their buying power.  \par \par \uc2 The buying spree continued in Europe, punctuated by bouts of profit-taking, before fund buyers took up the baton again when New York opened. Another round of taking profits soon after left the market ranging between $385.00 and $387.00 in the European afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 "Gold's rally has been almost entirely on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  factors - we've seen lots of buying from funds who are long, they are not in any particular trouble with the positions as the price has been steadily moving higher," John Reade of UBS Investment Bank told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 "If the dollar now goes through a big weakening phase they (funds) will be quite happy to sit there and watch gold tick higher," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Investors saw the Group of Seven (G7) statement as being aimed at countries such as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop a yen rise that is seen as bad for exports, but the move had broad consequences for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund (IMF), meeting in Dubai at the weekend, held initial talks on renewing a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gold sales pact that expires next year amid expectations the amount of bullion up for sale will rise from 400 tonnes a year but not dramatically so.  \par \par \uc2 The G7, also meeting in Dubai, lent gold support by calling for more flexibility on exchange rates, which led the Japanese yen to rise to its highest since December 2000 against the dollar, pushing the dollar down against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Gold closed in Europe at $384.20/384.70 a troy ounce at 1515 GMT after hitting a high of $387.75 in Asia and compared with $381.60/382.30 at Friday's New York close. The euro was at $1.1477 after touching a high of $1.1495 earlier.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Silver  backed off to $5.20/5.22 after moving to a high of $5.32\par earlier and compared with $5.24/5.26 quoted late in New York on Friday.\par Platinum        moved up to $703.00/708.00 from $697.00/702.00, while\par palladium        was at $215.50/221.50, unchanged from Friday.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CENBANK TALKS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dutch \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Nout Wellink said on Sunday that a new deal covering the sale of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gold was discussed briefly during the IMF meetings in Dubai and would be tackled properly early next year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This prompted expectations that many of Europe's leading central banks would renew the pact limiting sales of gold from their vaults for another five years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market wanted to take the comments on the agreement positively. The comment that seems to have been taken on board is that there would be no fundamental break to the approach taken, which most people are assuming means largely status quo," said Kamal Naqvi, precious metals analyst with Macquarie Investment Bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks worldwide hold more than 32,000 tonnes of gold, enough to feed world demand for about 12 years. The current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deal limits gold sales by 15 central banks to 400 tonnes a year until September 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 News from the IMF meeting that Russia would like to increase the percentage of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves it holds in gold, even though it is not in a position to buy at the moment, was also viewed as supportive of higher gold prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUYING SPREE  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculators continued digging into their wallets for gold, encouraged by the dollar/euro climate, shaky bond markets and reticence over stock market rallies on the belief that talk of a rapid U.S. economic recovery may have been overdone.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the latest Commitment of Traders Report, which outlines the extent of long positions built on the U.S. COMEX futures market, has been delayed until late Monday due to Hurricane Isabel.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With the COTR report delayed due to the hurricane we have no way of seeing the exact extent of COMEX speculative positions; we do know that the they are very long but apparently unconcerned by the extent of their buying," said John Reade of UBS Investment Bank in a daily report.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030922dz9m006sn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
