\par\par \b \uc2 Ex- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF Aide: G7 Didn't Say  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Can't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 315 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2003\par 17:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 DUBAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as it feels the need to, as the Group of Seven countries didn't necessarily say Tokyo should stop stepping into the markets, a former top Japanese finance ministry official said here Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 "The G-7 isn't saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it's also not saying it shouldn't," said former vice finance minister for international affairs Kosuke Nakahira, now vice chairman of the Institute of International Economic Research in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Nakahira, speaking to Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank meetings, said that if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry decide that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to curb volatility, they should go ahead.  \par \par \uc2 On Saturday, G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors released a statement calling for "major countries or economic areas" to adopt "more flexibility" in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 But Japanese officials stressed that the G-7 also maintained its view that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals and that they would cooperate as needed in the event of extreme short-term volatility.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, who holds Nakahira's former post, said there was no change to the ministry's stance that it will closely monitor markets and act as needed.  \par \par \uc2 Nakahira said a too-excessive yen surge may harm Japan's economy at a time when it's just starting to show signs of recovery - a fact which other G-7 nations likely acknowledge.  \par \par \uc2 Nakahira served as vice finance minister at a time when the finance ministry fought aggressively to battle the dollar back up from a low of Y79.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 777 620 0923; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020030921dz9l0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
