\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Makes Clear It Plans To Keep  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 827 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2003\par 13:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Miyako Takebe \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DUBAI (Dow Jones)--Japanese monetary officials came to Dubai last week seeking to defend their aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before their Group of Seven counterparts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The G-7 has come and gone and now they're heading home with their arguments intact, prepared to jump right back into the market if the dollar resumes its dive - as if there was little out of the ordinary.  \par \par \uc2 But it's not that Japanese officials are slighting the G-7 statement - which they took part in writing - that "major countries or economic areas" should adopt "more flexibility" in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 It's just that Japan has decided to put the emphasis on "smooth" - a word which they feel will justify continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen surges in a way that endangers \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui told reporters Saturday there has been "absolutely no change" to the G-7's basic stance that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals, and that in cases of extreme short-term volatility, the G-7 would cooperate as needed.  \par \par \uc2 In this context, Tokyo has sold up to $90 billion worth of yen so far this year to prevent generic dollar weakness lifting the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too far. While the dollar has dropped almost 10% so far this year against European units, it is down just 2% against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's point man on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , chimed in, saying there was no change to Tokyo's policy of monitoring markets, and acting - code for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  - in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as needed.  \par \par \uc2 In a clear warning to yen bulls, Fukui said: "We hope the markets will take that into consideration," while also observing that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements leading up to Saturday's Dubai meeting had been "speculative."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar crashed through key support at Y115 - a level that capped yen gains on a number of occasions since March 2001 - late last week, sinking briefly to a 30-month low of Y113.57 on Friday. It ended New York trade at Y113.98.  \par \par \uc2 The comments by Fukui and Mizoguchi mean that if the dollar falls further, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will snap the market back to attention by selling yen through the BOJ.  \par \par \uc2 Some in the market appear to have received that message. "For the time being, we're expecting some more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - although we also expect different tactics" said Kaneo Ogino, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at HSBC in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "As the MOF won't be able to push the dollar up - they'll likely go for a smoothing it down," he said, making the Y115-line in the sand - on which the government spent more than Y9 trillion defending this year - a thing of the past. With that gone, the next area the MOF may protect will probably be Y110, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Adjustments Inevitable, But Japan Economy Comes First \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over the longer-term, however, Japanese officials are aware that they will have to let the yen rise in order to help bring about a smooth and gradual adjustment in the huge U.S. current account deficit and other global imbalances.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And they will - at some point - let go of the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Everyone knows they can't keep this up indefinitely. The most important thing is just when and how the ministry will exit the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  scene," HSBC's Ogino said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the spin coming out Japan at Dubai is any indication, that won't be any time soon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A prerequisite for MOF to disengage itself from the market will likely be a Japanese economy strong enough to weather the debilitating impact a strong yen will have on exports - the nation's key economic driver.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But despite the fact the GDP grew at a blistering annualized rate of 3.9% in the second quarter, the economy is still not out of the woods, Japanese officials say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While some may view that 3.9% growth shows improving Japanese fundamentals, fundamentals involve a wide variety of other factors," Mizoguchi said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He was likely referring to the fact the economy is still mired in persistent deflation and also still in the process of purging bad loans from the nation's banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And there's also the issue of relativity, said Mizoguchi, who pointed out that it appears certain the U.S. economy will grow more strongly than Japan's in coming quarters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So if the G-7 means what it says about getting global economies into a sustainable recovery, then it shouldn't mind if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with that aim. Or at least that's what MOF is keen to have us believe.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 777 620 0923; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020030921dz9l00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
