\par\par \b \uc2 G-7 Urges  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Flexibility, Sets Growth Plan  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Hannon  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2003\par 19:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 DUBAI (Dow Jones)--The world's top monetary officials on Saturday issued an unusual call for increased flexibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes to underpin a balanced global economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 And the finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven backed up that talk with an action plan to stimulate growth and ensure that market imbalances adjust smoothly.  \par \par \uc2 In a communique, the G-7 urged "major countries or economic areas" to adopt "more flexibility" in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes in order to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 The main problem facing the world economy as it begins to emerge from its slump is that it relies too heavily on the U.S. as a driver of growth.  \par \par \uc2 The result is that the U.S. is sucking in imports, but finding it hard to attract buyers for its exports. That can't continue indefinitely, and the challenge for policy makers is to make the process of reducing America's large current account deficit gradual and smooth rather than sudden and chaotic.  \par \par \uc2 It would be a big help if Asian countries that are running large trade surpluses allowed their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float, and appreciate against the dollar. That in turn would make it easier for the euro zone economy to withstand a rise in the value of the euro, since its exports wouldn't be priced out of the U.S. and Asian markets.  \par \par \uc2 China is one example of a major country that doesn't have much flexibility in its exchange rate, which has been pegged at 8.28 yuan to the dollar since 1994. And G-7 member \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this year in an effort to limit the appreciation of the yen against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par U.S.'s Snow Pleased With \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Language \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said he was pleased the statement reflected his view on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The need for action extends beyond the G-7," Snow said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is a risk that the process of realigning major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be bumpy, and cause some stresses in international financial markets. But according to European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg, central banks "are willing to do anything required to keep markets calm, and they are able to use all the instruments in their arsenal when it comes to adjustment and to help adjustment to go along smoothly and gradually."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 G-7 finance ministers stressed that they weren't singling out China or any other country for individual criticism. They need cooperation rather than confrontation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There wasn't a focus entirely on what we should do about a particular exchange rate," Bank of England Governor Mervyn King said. "There wasn't a focus on a particular country."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And the G-7 attempted to show it's doing its share to rebalance the global economy by issuing a statement pledging policies designed to spread responsibility for increasing demand more evenly among its members - it's easier to divvy up the pie if it's getting bigger.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The policies included tort reform in the U.S., more investment spending in Europe and tax cuts in Canada, but didn't include deficit reduction in any of the G-7 economies, which many observers see as necessary for sustained growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Higher economic growth throughout the G-7 will redress global imbalances that arise from uneven growth within the G-7," the finance ministers said. "Economic growth has been too low for too long in the G-7, and while there are notable recent policy changes, it is time to bolster our efforts."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For all the talk of shared responsibility, the euro zone has to undertake the most radical reforms, since it is lagging the U.S., the U.K. and Japan in the nascent recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The focus will be on the imbalances that are due to the different growth rates of different countries and how economic reform in Europe in particular can resolve some of these imbalances over time," U.K. Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown said.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market Reaction Seen As Key \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reaction of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Monday will give an immediate indication of how smooth the adjustments in the global economy are likely to be. It may take some time before traders are convinced the G-7 statement marks a new departure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This could be a turning point in the G-7, or a reflection of different personalities," Marc Chandler, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at HSBC USA, said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets will be particularly closely watched. If the bank jumps in to cap yen appreciation, the G-7 statement will look less like the beginning of a new era and more like wish fulfillment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said he believed there was "absolutely no change" to the G-7's long-standing view on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fukui acknowledged that recent international interest in exchange rates, including the Chinese yuan, were reflected in G-7 discussions. But he said the need for flexibility to promote adjustments in the international financial system "is something that applies to all major regions, not just China or Japan."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said China did not object to the language regarding the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. Eichel said there had been discussions between the G-7 and China, and "this statement did not surprise anyone and no one objected to it."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G-7 deputy finance ministers met with their Chinese counterparts here on Friday, ahead of the G-7 ministerial meeting.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "One thing we take away from this is a process by which China is part of the participation of the G-7," HSBC's Chandler said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Paul Hannon, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 777 620 0927; paul.hannon@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Brian Blackstone, David Pearson, Miyako Takebe, Elizabeth Price and Daniel Schwammenthal contributed to this story.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030920dz9k0009d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
