\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Sep 19  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2003\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: With Japan under intense pressure within the Group of Seven to curb its yen-weakening campaign, many in the market are betting that Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is poised for a sharp rise in coming days, especially after the dollar slid to a two-and-a-half-year year low overnight with no sign of the BOJ. But betting on Japan to let the yen rise could be a dangerous gamble. Yen bulls may have set themselves up for the slaughter as defiance by Tokyo could spark an abrupt yen sell-off. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Stretched Positions May Be Danger For Yen Bulls" ran at 0231 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: Investor demand for protection against Japanese firms defaulting on debt continues to dwindle as the economy shows signs of recovery and stock prices surge, disappointing many security firms that hoped to make juicy profits from the new market. ("=JAPAN CREDIT: CDS Prices Fall, Few Signs Of Turnaround" ran at 0515 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: People's Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiaochuan said Friday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is conducting research on the issue of allowing Hong Kong banks to trial run services with the yuan for individual customers. ("China's PBOC: Researching Trial Yuan Business By HK Banks" ran at 0839 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China is battling a war of words on trade as U.S. labor unions and manufacturers attack both the yuan's fixed exchange rate and Beijing's compliance with its commitment to opening its market. (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Risk that China's yuan might rise against the U.S. dollar weakened demand for the country's first issue of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds. (WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese investors bought a large amount of foreign bonds last week, weekly portfolio flows data showed Friday. It marked the second consecutive week of net buying as previously volatile U.S. Treasury yields started to calm. ("Japan/Weekly Portfolio" ran at 0018 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  secured a major court victory Friday, thwarting a challenge to its credit card reforms from Visa and Mastercard. Ruling that the two companies failed to make a case, the Federal Court dismissed their applications to have a judicial review of decisions taken by the Reserve Bank. ("=WRAP: Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Has Credit Card Win" ran at 0806 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's external liabilities totaled $159 billion at the end of June, up from $150.2 billion at the end of March, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy said Friday. ("S Korea Foreign Debt At $159B End-June Vs $150.2B End-Mar" ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean consumers' outlook for the economy remained sluggish in the third quarter, but not enough to prompt them to cut back their future spending plans, according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey Friday. ("REPEAT:Korea 3Q Consumer Outlook Gloomy;Spending Holds Up" ran at 0314 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday slashed its forecast for this year's current account surplus, as data showed imports recovered in July even as exports continued to dawdle. ("=WRAP: Philippine Imports Rise; Outlook For Exports Cut" ran at 0838 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: European Union discussions with China's quarantine authority have resulted in the easing of a controversial ban on E.U. cosmetics containing animal byproducts, a visiting E.U. official said recently. ("=INTERVIEW: China Easing Ban On E.U. Cosmetics Imports" ran at 0451 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: As part of efforts to streamline control of China's burgeoning financial sector, the country's banking, securities, and insurance regulators have agreed to define their supervisory responsibilities. ("China's Financial Regulators To Strengthen Cooperation" ran at 0413 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Qualcomm chairman and chief executive officer Irwin Jacobs Friday enbdorsed China's stable yuan policy, saying it created a better environment for financial and investment planning. ("Qualcomm CEO Jacobs Endorses China's Stable Yuan Policy" ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The Australian government Friday sought to temper concerns the domestic housing bubble will burst and stymie an expected rebound in economic growth. ("=WRAP: Australian Govt Tries To Damp Housing Hot Potato" ran at 0319 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Government-owned New Zealand Post is likely to issue around NZ$50 million of bonds to institutional investors early next year, the mail company's chief financial officer, Peter Schuyt, said Friday. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Post Eyes Bond To Refinance 2004 Maturity" ran at 0644 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The seasonally adjusted number of visitors to New Zealand picked up in August in what was the first year-on-year increase since SARS began denting visitor figures in April, official data showed Friday. ("DATA SNAP: Aug First On-Year NZ Visitor Rise Since SARS" ran at 2255 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar held its ground against the yen in Asia Friday, as traders nervously awaited a weekend G-7 meeting at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is likely to come under fire. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0534 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Facing strong pressure from financial regulators to quickly restructure their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 15 Japanese banks that have received public funds revealed their revised business reform plans Friday with additional restructuring pledges. (WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Commonwealth Bank of Australia said Friday it will spend A$1.5 billion and ax about 11% of its work force in a three-year restructure program aimed at lifting customer service and protecting its market share. ("=WRAP: Australia's CBA Restructures, To Ax 11% Of Staff" ran at 0534 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Chartered Semiconductor Manufacturing, the world's third-largest chip foundry, has formed an alliance that will allow it to secure third-party know-how and move up the chip technology ladder. The alliance, based on several individual deals with companies, should help Chartered narrow the yawning technology gap that has prevented it from competing effectively with larger Taiwanese rivals. ("=WRAP: Singapore Chartered Adds Know-How For New Chips" ran at 0709 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's largest cotton textile producer, Weiqiao Textile, will raise HK$2.12 billion through an offering of H shares priced at HK$8.50 apiece, a banker close to the deal said Friday. The price is at the top end of the HK$6.15-HK$8.55 indicative price range and subscriptions are still being counted, but the issue is said to be "well oversubscribed." ("China Weiqiao/IPO" first take ran at 0334 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Shanghai Pudong Development Bank said Friday the Chinese government has approved the planned purchase of a 4.62% stake in the bank by Citigroup's Citibank. ("China/ Pudong Bk/Citigroup" first take ran at 2358 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Japan Securities Dealers Association and Jasdaq Market Inc. are considering transforming the Jasdaq over-the-counter market into an exchange under the Securities and Exchange Law as early as the year starting April 2004, the Jasdaq Market said Friday. The possible move is aimed at enhancing the attractiveness of the Jasdaq market, Japan's largest stock market for startups, by increasing liquidity. ("JSDA, Jasdaq Mull Turning Jasdaq Market Into Exchange" ran at 0305 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FOCUS: Investors have poured into Asian equities in recent months, betting that a global economic recovery will take hold and lift the earnings and valuations of Asia's cyclically-sensitive companies. ("=ASIA FOCUS: Econ Recovery To Spur Asian Shrs Even Higher" ran at 0747 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Cathay Financial Holding said Friday it aims to acquire more banks after merging its two banking units next month, in order to more than double its market share eventually. Cathay Financial's two bank units, United World Chinese Commercial Bank and Cathay United Bank, will merge Oct. 27 and the new entity - to be renamed Cathay United Bank - will have assets valued at NT$888.3 billion.("Taiwan Cathay Fincl To Buy More Bks After 2 Units Merge" ran at 0500 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Airways International's board has approved a major management reshuffle, which involves the transfer of a key executive vice president, according to a company statement late Thursday. ("Thai Airways Bd OKs Management Shake-Up; Effective Oct 1" ran at 0227 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Boeing expects rapid growth in the global airline industry over the next two decades and hopes its proposed 7E7 jet will meet the needs of airlines that seek to offer customers more comfort and flexibility, the Chicago-based aircraft manufacturer's director of product and services marketing, Randy Tinseth, said Friday. ("Boeing Sees Rapid Airline Indus Growth, Pins Hopes On 7E7" ran at 0720 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: U.S. investment firm Lone Star Funds will buy 268.75 million new common shares of Korea Exchange Bank Sept. 30 as part of the pact reached in late August to buy a 51% stake in the mid-sized South Korean bank, KEB said Friday. ("Lone Star To Buy 268.75M Shrs Of Korea Exch Bank Sept 30" ran at 0142 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Woori Finance Holdings said Friday it will buy the entire 128 million new common shares of unit Woori Credit Card to be offered in a rights offering Sept. 29-30 to recapitalize the card unit. ("S Korea Woori To Buy 128M Shrs Of Card Unit In Late Sept" ran at 0126 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: A shareholder in Techtronic Industries is selling 36.258 million existing shares in the power tool maker at HK$16.625 each, people close to the deal said Friday. ("HK Techtronic/Shares" first take ran at 0247 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Samsung Electronics said Friday it is looking to shift some personal-computer manufacturing facilities to China, but denied a local report that said it will move all of its facilities to Suzhou in a move to cut costs. ("Samsung Elec To Shift Some PC Mfg Facilities To China" ran at 0135 GMT; note "CORRECT: Samsung Elec Has 1 Million PC Capacity In China" ran at 0159 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The Australian Customs Service has found that South Korean-made washing machines by Samsung Electronics and Daewoo Electronics were exported at below domestic market prices and have caused "material injury" to Australian industry. The customs service recommended that anti-dumping action be taken against the goods exported by the two companies. ("Australia Finds Samsung, Daewoo "Dumped" Washing Machines" ran at 0407 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Hongkong & Shanghai Hotels is in discussions to open one of its Peninsula-branded luxury hotels in the eastern coastal city of Shanghai, a process that could lead to the company's return to one of its namesake cities after an almost 50-year absence. (INTERVIEW will run by around 0920 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Guorun Holdings says it plans to issue 680 million additional shares and will use HK$450 million of the proceeds to expand a sedan manufacturing joint venture in China. China's unlisted Geely Holding will also provide HK$450 million of assets to the venture, which will give unlisted mainland Chinese automaker Zhejiang Geely Automobiles exposure to overseas investors. ("REPEAT: HK Guorun To Sell Shrs,Use Funds For Car Mfg JV" ran at 0424 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Newly merged Australian broking and investment house Goldman Sachs JBWere said Friday it created 27 new equity partners. Goldman Sachs JBWere was officially launched this week after 163-year-old Australian brokerage JBWere announced a plan in March to merge with the Australian arm of U.S. investment bank Goldman Sachs Group. ("Australia's Goldman Sachs JBWere Creates 27 New Partners" ran at 0107 GMT; "=INTERVIEW: Australia Goldman Sachs JBWere Upbeat On Mkts" first take ran at 0350 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Coles Myer Managing Director John Fletcher said Friday the Australian retailer's rise in annual profit shows its turnaround strategy is on track. Its net profit for the year ended July 27 rose 21% to A$429.5 million. ("Australia's Coles Myer/Profit" first take ran at 2246 GMT; WRAP will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Its parent may have just clinched a luxury hotel deal with the likes of Hyatt International of the U.S., but Shanghai-listed Yintai Holding isn't likely to reap any of the short-term benefits, at least as far as its share price is concerned. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Hyatt Deal No Boon For China's Yintai" ran at 0307 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Two shareholders of TPV Technology have sold 278 million existing shares, or a total stake of 20.58%, in the computer monitor maker at HK$3.555 per share. Fields Pacific, which is wholly owned by TPV non-executive director Stanley Pen, sold a 15.58% stake, while Brilliant Way Investment sold a 5% holding. ("Two HK TPV Holders Sell Total 20.6% Stake At HK$3.555/Shr" ran at 0346 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Mid-sized Indian tire company Ceat says it's shelving a 1-billion rupee capacity expansion and the company plans to import tires from China to meet local demand, as financial constraints at home are preventing it from expanding. ("INTERVIEW: India Ceat" first take ran at 0534 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian investment holding company Tanjong to release first-half results after market close. (story will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand vodka maker and exporter 42 Below Ltd. plans to raise NZ$15 million through the issue of 30 million shares at 50 NZ cents each, financial services company Dorchester Pacific confirmed Friday. ("NZ Vodka Maker 42 Below To Raise NZ$15M From Share Offer" ran at 2152 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Efforts by a Japanese government-led consortium to develop a major oilfield in Iran have taken a blow, thanks to an announcement by Tehran that it is to invite oil majors to bid for the project. ("=Japan Still Hoping To Win Iran Oil Deal Despite Rivals" ran at 0804 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: It may just be a diamond that stirs up some much needed investor interest in Japan's sagging gold market - a baseball diamond to be precise. Several Japanese precious metals companies have been rolling out new products to commemorate the winning of the Central League championship by the hugely popular Hanshin Tigers baseball club - something the team hasn't done for 18 years. Some market watchers say this presents a good chance to attract first-time buyers to the gold market at a time when investor demand appears to be on the wane. ("=Hanshin Tigers Scratch Up New Interest In Japan Gold Mkt" ran at 0523  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030919dz9j000er\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
