\par\par \b \uc2 New Taiwan Dlr Ends Up As Japanese Yen Strengthens  \b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2003\par 10:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI (Dow Jones)--The New Taiwan dollar ended higher against its U.S. counterpart Friday, as the Japanese yen continued to strengthen against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.058, down from NT$34.091 Thursday. Dealings were valued at US$779 million, compared with US$433 million Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "A lower dollar-yen and QFII (qualified foreign institutional investors) inflows put pressure on the U.S. dollar, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the Taiwan dollar above NT$34.050," said a dealer.  \par \par \uc2 "The crucial thing right now is whether the Japanese yen breaches Y115," said another dealer, adding, "if that happens the Taiwan dollar can go anywhere."  \par \par \uc2 The New Taiwan dollar tends to move in line with the yen because their exports compete head-on in markets like the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said despite the incremental nature of the New Taiwan dollar's gains against the U.S. dollar, a clear, appreciative trend was evident, and all the central banks of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Taiwan could do was to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations.  \par \par \uc2 Views were mixed as to whether the New Taiwan dollar would open below the psychologically important NT$34.000 level on Monday, but all agreed the Japanese yen's direction over the weekend would set the course for the New Taiwan dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "If the yen's up further on Monday, we might well see the Taiwan dollar testing NT$33.500, but if the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and brings the yen back to Y117 we might see the Taiwan dollar back at around NT$34.150," said a trader.  \par \par \uc2 Money market rates rose, with a deficit in banks' daily reserves at NT$5.6 billion. The overnight interbank offered rate was 1.024% compared with 1.020% Thursday, while the 30-day bill rate 0.912% against 0.909% in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a total NT$10 billion in CDs/NCDs, while NT$13.46 billion in similar paper matured.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan government bonds were weak on a modest 0.7% gain in the stock market. The 10-year bond yield was 2.808% compared with 2.796% Thursday, but off its intraday high of 2.830%.  \par \par \uc2 -By Chris Taylor, Dow Jones Newswires; (8862) 2502-2557; chris.taylor@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 1061 1860  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030919dz9j000ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
