\par\par \b \uc2 Pushing yen up is a risky game to play with BoJ.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2003\par 00:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 19 (Reuters) - The stakes are getting higher in the daredevil game that traders have been playing with the Bank of Japan to push the dollar lower against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Tempted by the elusive possibility that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might tone down its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of subtle pressure from the United States, investors have been selling dollars against yen.  \par \par \uc2 But it's a dangerous game.  \par \par \uc2 When dealers hit their desks at dawn on Monday in Tokyo - if not before - chances are mounting that after an atypical hiatus, the BoJ may return to yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a vengeance. That could whipsaw the dollar higher against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , burning some investors' fingers.  \par \par \uc2 "There could be a lot of pain if the BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Putnam Investments in Boston. Japan could "look for the best time ... to wait until everyone is long (yen)," he said, with investors betting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 The BoJ is widely considered to have stayed on the sidelines ahead of the Group of Seven meetings in Dubai as the yen has firmed. As a result, the possibility of a heavy round of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now looms larger.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the dollar crashed below the 114.0 yen area against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  , sagging to fresh 2-1/2-year troughs of 113.58 yen. That is well below the 115.0 yen per dollar "line in the sand" ruptured on Thursday, which Japan had been expected to defend.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, it seems that the BoJ reluctantly stood back and and gritted its teeth on the sidelines.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar/yen is dominating the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today. We are through (below) major support" for the dollar, said Cyrus Whitney, head dealer with Commerzbank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "With this meeting (in Dubai) .... the BoJ is keeping a low profile. A close below 115.0 yen (of the dollar against the yen) is very bearish and could lead to some interesting developments next week," Whitney said. Late on Friday in New York, the dollar bought 114.0 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The BoJ will be back," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York. "The BoJ has a history of stepping away in front of major meetings. They did that ahead of (U.S.) Treasury Secretary (John) Snow's visit to Japan and China. They're doing that now," Sinche added.  \par \par \uc2 By the time Japanese officials head back to Tokyo from Dubai, the gloves will come off for a round of heavyweight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that by now is overdue, traders fear.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to curb the yen's appreciation in order to keep its exports competitive as the world's second-biggest economy picks up its pace. By contrast, the United States would prefer a stronger yen to lend U.S. exporters a hand in selling their goods to Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Rather than indicating that the BoJ's tolerance for dollar weakness against the yen has moved a notch or two lower, Whitney expects that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's no-show over the past couple of days has, by default, turned into an opportune tactic to confuse the market before stepping in again.  \par \par \uc2 The BoJ's "whole strategy is an unknown. Normally they (the Bank of Japan) don't pick lines in the sand. When you pick levels like that, it gives the market a target to go at," Whitney said.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, by allowing traders to think dollar/yen's traditional floor has crumbled, that may tempt many to accumulate bets that the greenback will decline further against the yen, leaving the market slanted heavily in one direction.  \par \par \uc2 Such skewed positioning typically gives a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the opportunity to deliver a high voltage jolt to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sending it shooting in the other direction.  \par \par \uc2 Still, Upadhyaya is among a group of analysts who think there's a chance that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is mulling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  less aggressively in future.  \par \par \uc2 "I think what the BoJ are trying to do is to prevent any type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the G7 meeting and after the meeting ... they will try to come in to try to set a new trading range for the yen. That range might be between 112.0 and 117.0 yen (per dollar), while the previous one was 115.0 yen to 120.0 yen," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The main reason Japan may be willing to countenance a lower trading range for the dollar against the yen is that behind the scenes, the U.S. administration has started to put \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure to brake its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Upadhyaya said.  \par \par \uc2 "That makes it very difficult for the Japanese to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken (the yen) dramatically, if there is little support from a key ally," he said.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez in New York).   