\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-China warns G7 off yuan on eve of Dubai talks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Love and Gilbert Le Gras  \par \uc2 902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2003\par 18:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  DUBAI, Sept 19 (Reuters) - China warned the United States on Friday not to make it a scapegoat for its economic woes by pressing for a revaluation of the yuan at this weekend's meeting of the world's leading industrial powers.   \par \par \uc2  As speculation swirled that Group of Seven finance ministers would call for market forces to play a greater role in setting exchange rates, traders put aside fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) and drove the yen up to a 2-1/2 year peak.   \par \par \uc2  Whether China, Japan and other Asian nations should stop meddling in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  float higher to help iron out major imbalances threatening the global economy will dominate Saturday's G7 gathering in Dubai.   \par \par \uc2  With an eye on the U.S. presidential election in November 2004, Washington in particular has been cajoling Beijing to act to deflect pressure from U.S. manufacturers who blame job losses on what they say is an unfairly undervalued Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  But the China Daily, in a commentary seen as reflecting the views of the government, dismissed repeated U.S. calls for a stronger yuan, also known as the renminbi, as futile.   \par \par \uc2  "Making China the scapegoat can perhaps help some U.S. politicians score cheap political points, (but) it has nothing to do with the solution of their real problems," the paper said.   \par \par \uc2  China won support for its position from the No. 2 of the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who echoed concerns expressed a day earlier by the International Monetary Fund about the risks posed by America's gaping current account and budget deficits.   \par \par \uc2  "We should not let the discussion about the yuan distract from the main problem of the U.S.'s twin deficits," Bundesbank Vice-President Juergen Stark told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2  BLIND EYE   \par \par \uc2  In contrast to its pressure on China, Washington has turned a blind eye so far this year to record yen selling by the BOJ worth about $80 billion to avert a rise in the yen that could kill an unfolding recovery in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-dependent economy.   \par \par \uc2  Until this week, that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had kept the dollar above 115 yen, widely deemed to be a make-or-break level for the profitability of many Japanese exporters.   \par \par \uc2  But on Friday traders summoned up the confidence to test the BOJ's resolve and, finding it wanting, swiftly pushed the dollar down to a low of 113.60 yen. It later recovered to 113.85 yen.   \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui played down talk that, since Japan's economy grew at a brisk 3.9 percent clip between April and June, Tokyo would come under pressure to let the yen rise.   \par \par \uc2  "The G7 is not a place to criticise specific policies," Fukui said. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be discussed in the framework of putting the global economy on a sustainable growth track."   \par \par \uc2  The G7 will meet on the sidelines of the annual meeting of the 184-member IMF and World Bank, which is being held in the Middle East for the first time.   \par \par \uc2  Taking a softer line than the United States, Fukui said he hoped for constructive talks with China about the yuan, which is pegged in a tight band around 8.28 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2  "China has clearly said that it will work out ways to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system more flexible. It is important that there is constructive discussions to take it in the right direction," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said.   \par \par \uc2  NO BULLYING, PLEASE   \par \par \uc2  China's long-standing policy is to ease its grip on the yuan and gradually make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  convertible for capital transactions, but not till it has strengthened its ailing banks.   \par \par \uc2  In the latest warning by policymakers that overt pressure on Beijing would be counterproductive, IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler praised China on Friday for its "thoughtful and gradual" progress in opening its capital markets and said Beijing could not be expected to cut the yuan loose overnight.   \par \par \uc2  "I would urgently advise against using the issue as an excuse for bullying," Koehler told a news conference.   \par \par \uc2  His remarks were aimed squarely at Washington, where some legislators and manufacturing groups are clamouring for trade sanctions against China, which chalked up a $103 billion trade surplus with the United States last year.   \par \par \uc2  Cheap Chinese imports cap inflation and make American paychecks go further, but that is less important politically for President George W. Bush in an election year than a vibrant global economy generating demand for U.S. goods.   \par \par \uc2  To that end, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow chided other G7 members on Friday for not pulling their weight.   \par \par \uc2  Snow said European economies were "stagnant basically" and that Japan had gone through "10 years of very anaemic growth". In contrast, the United States was entering what he called a period of sustained and long-term growth.   \par \par \uc2  "The United States can't be the only engine of growth," Snow said in Islamabad before flying to Dubai.   \par \par \uc2  Not all G7 ministers will make Saturday's meeting.   \par \par \uc2  France's Francis Mer is staying home to focus on future of ailing engineering firm Alstom, while Japan's Masajuro Shiokawa, is ill. The other members are the United States, Germany, Britain, Italy and Canada.   \par \par \uc2  (Reporting by Dubai team, Glenn Somerville in Islamabad, Kevin Yao in Beijing and Ashley Seager in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030919dz9j009ta\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
