\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Falls Nearly One Yen, but Climbs vs. Euro \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Barkley \par \uc2 1,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 23:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar dove to 2 1/2-year lows against the yen Thursday, but then recovered back above the key 115-yen level, apparently without active support from Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  Investors have been heavily buying up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions to see how much yen strength would be tolerated, with Thursday's price action reinforcing the suspicion that Japan would be unwilling to manipulate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of this weekend's meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations in Dubai. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has a history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to keep the yen at a competitive level for Japan's exporters, spending an estimated $100 billion so far this year to stem the yen's appreciation. In light of the Bank of Japan's past record, many market participants had expected a fierce defense around the 115-yen level, as the 114.70-114.80 yen area is considered to be a break-even exchange rate for Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the dollar was trading around 115.36 yen, down from 116.21 yen late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.1249, below $1.1286 late Wednesday. It was also at 129.75 yen, down from 131.14 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide began overnight, when it broke through a series of stop-loss sales orders in the 115.50-115.80 yen zone. The selling accelerated in the New York session, as the dollar slipped through the 115-yen level to an intraday bottom at 114.73 yen, its lowest point since February 2001. \par \par \uc2  Though traders saw no convincing evidence of bids placed on behalf of the Bank of Japan, the slide ran out of steam by midmorning and the dollar slowly began to recover to spend the afternoon back above 115 yen. \par \par \uc2  A sharp pullback in the euro apparently did the Bank of Japan's job for it, with traders saying that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a little ahead of itself on a morning rally to a one-month high of $1.1345. \par \par \uc2  Late in the New York afternoon, verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat provided additional if modest support for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We regard the movements over the past few days as being rapid," Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi told reporters as he arrived in Dubai. \par \par \uc2  He added that there was no change to Japan's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in that it will act in markets when it feels it is appropriate to do so. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  going into the weekend would be politically sensitive, market participants think the Bank of Japan may also be biding its time to spring when the market is caught off guard. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan may be easing off in order to appease the U.S. ahead of the G7, but I don't believe there will be a wholesale abandonment by the Japanese of their \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said Alex Beuzelin, senior market analyst at Ruesch International in Washington, D.C. \par \par \uc2  While it may seem surprising that the move below 115 yen didn't trigger the expected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants are increasingly wary that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be setting a "bear trap" to cause a short squeeze among dollar bears. \par \par \uc2  "It's much more effective when the market is short of dollars, and essentially they get more bang for their buck," he said. \par \par \uc2  ABN-Amro's Speculative Positioning Index, which surveys the bank's trading desk positions around the world, is approaching its high for the year for short-dollar positions against the yen, which Peter Frank, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-Amro Bank in Chicago, calls "pretty close to extreme." \par \par \uc2  However, Mr. Frank doesn't think the Bank of Japan would attempt to spring a big trap ahead of this weekend's meeting. \par \par \uc2  "They don't want to cause controversy going into this key meeting if they can help it," he said. "Once the G7 is out of way, they will be free to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they will." \par \par \uc2  Of course, what will come out of the G7 discussions about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unclear. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's position "isn't going to change" from the message he took with him on the recent Asian tour, said Treasury spokesman Tony Fratto Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "In both Tokyo and Beijing, Snow made a strong statement on the U.S. position on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that they should be set at open competitive markets," said Mr. Fratto. \par \par \uc2  Yet while Mr. Snow pushed China to begin to loosen its exchange-rate peg and has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "should be kept to a minimum," analysts doubted that much of that would language would be included in the final communique. \par \par \uc2  Officials from Germany as well as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have expressed an unwillingness to push China too hard on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue. In a speech in Hong Kong Thursday, China's top central banker, People's Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiaochuan, dismissed calls to revalue the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying there is no compelling reason for immediate change in an exchange-rate system that works. \par \par \uc2  However, comments out of Dubai from the International Monetary Fund did underscore the difficulty Japanese authorities will have in trying to stem the yen's strength, which has been driven largely by strong flows into its equities. \par \par \uc2  IMF Chief Economist Kenneth Rogoff, in presenting the World Economic Outlook, said that while the U.S. recovery "is charging ahead," it has come at the cost of "twin deficits" in the fiscal budget and current account balance. \par \par \uc2  This is unsustainable in the long run and will eventually lead to a sharp fall in the dollar, Mr. Rogoff said. That is likely to hit Europe's export-led economy the hardest, unless there are reforms to improve domestic demand and Asian countries loosen their exchange-rate policies, he added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar also clawed back ground against the Swiss franc, on a day the Swiss National Bank kept its key interest rate near zero, as expected. The Swiss National Bank's three-month Swiss franc Libor rate of 0.25% was left unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank also reiterated its pledge to take action to fight against any excessive appreciation of the Swiss franc, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if need be. \par \par \uc2  The rate decision didn't have an immediate impact on the Swiss franc's exchange rate against the euro, on which the Swiss National Bank focuses more than the exchange rate with the dollar. Indeed, the Swiss franc is still hovering around the three-year lows against the euro hit earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Near the close of New York trading Thursday, the euro was at 1.5588 francs, up from 1.5566 francs late Wednesday, while the dollar was at 1.3866 francs, up from 1.3781 francs. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling was at $1.6147, up from $1.6074 late Wednesday but off Thursday's high of $1.6217, which had been its strongest level in nearly two months. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385\par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Jamie McGeever contributed to this story.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 09-18-03 1716ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030918dz9i005k2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
