\par\par \b \uc2  IMF Affirms Upgraded Japan GDP View; 2.0% Growth In 2003 \b0\par\par\uc2 1,013 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 08:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Reflecting surprising resilience in the world's second-largest economy, the International Monetary Fund on Thursday affirmed its recently upgraded forecast for economic growth in Japan at 2.0% this year. \par \par \uc2  But it warned that the country's economic rebound may fizzle unless Tokyo takes more aggressive steps to end persistent price falls, clean up the debt-clogged financial system and rein in ballooning public borrowing. \par \par \uc2  "Looking forward, although risks have become more balanced, the outlook remains clouded by entrenched deflation and by persistent weakness in corporate, financial and public sector balance sheets," the IMF said in its latest World Economic Outlook. \par \par \uc2  The forecast growth in Japan's gross domestic product this year, initially released in a report two weeks ago, is more than double the 0.8% rise the IMF predicted in April. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said a surprising burst of growth in the second quarter, combined with buoyant Tokyo share prices and rebounding demand for Japanese goods overseas, would help fuel growth into next year. \par \par \uc2  Japan's real GDP grew at a blazing annualized rate of 3.9% in the April-June quarter, outpacing growth in the U.S. and Europe, while the Nikkei 225 Stock Average has rallied 44% from its 20-year nadir in April. \par \par \uc2  The IMF predicted Japan's GDP would grow 1.4% in 2004, better than the 1.0% expansion it had previously forecast. \par \par \uc2  But despite the brighter view, the IMF said Japan's economy, which pulled out of its worst postwar recession last year, "remains vulnerable to a range of domestic and external shocks." \par \par \uc2  Among the risks cited by the IMF was a sustained rise in the value of the yen, which could damp exports, cut into corporate profits and exacerbate deflation. \par \par \uc2  The yen has gained a modest 2% against the dollar since the start of the year, but it has been under near-constant pressure to appreciate. Further rises have been forestalled only by an unprecedented campaign by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength through yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Other potential dangers cited by the IMF included \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growing public debt, which could trigger a jump in real interest rates, and rising yields on Japanese government bonds, which could weaken balance sheets at Japan's JGB-hoarding banks, causing them to cut lending. \par \par \uc2  Yields on 10-year JGBs peaked earlier this month at 1.675%, nearly four times a record low of 0.43% set in June. \par \par \uc2  "To contain these risks, a bold strategy is needed that tackles the underlying weaknesses in the economy," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Bank Reforms, BOJ Deflation Steps Key\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such a strategy would include "rapid and forceful measures" to more\par quickly reduce the vast pile of bad loans weighing on the financial\par system, the IMF said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While Japan's major banks have been chiseling away at their mountains\par of bad loans, the Financial Services Agency recently said the amount of\par nonperforming loans at major banks as of the end of March stood at\par Y43.728 trillion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The IMF warned "the quality of bank capital remains poor," with\par deferred tax assets - future tax credits that can only be realized if\par the bank claiming them makes a profit - making up a sizable amount of\par top-tier capital.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The IMF noted approvingly the publicly funded bailout in May of Resona\par Holdings Inc. (J.RSN or 8308), Japan's fifth-largest bank, which had\par suffered a crippling fall in its capital.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But it said the effective nationalization of Resona needed to be\par "supported by broader reforms to strengthen bank and corporate\par governance," reduce nonperforming loans and encourage more struggling\par lenders to merge.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Accelerated disposal of NPLs and targeted use of public funds to\par recapitalize weak but systemically important banks remain essential to\par ending the sector's decade-long deterioration," the IMF said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The IMF also called on the Bank of Japan to take more aggressive steps\par to end six years of persistent price falls, which have eaten into\par corporate profits and pushed up firms' real debt loads despite\par historically low interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the BOJ's quantitative easing policy, which targets short-term\par interest rates near zero, has helped stabilize the financial system, "it\par has been unable to end deflation, ease deflationary expectations, or\par promote growth in broad money and bank credit," the IMF said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In view of these difficulties, a more aggressive policy approach is\par clearly needed."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The IMF predicted declines in consumer prices would accelerate to an\par average of 0.6% next year after easing somewhat in 2003 to an average of\par 0.3%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It repeated its call on the BOJ to set "a transparent medium-term\par inflation target" and lay out a specific timeframe for ending deflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It also urged the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy more JGBs and target a broader\par array of assets for purchase.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Concerns about potential Bank of Japan losses on JGB holdings," it\par said, "should not be allowed to detract from the pursuit of price\par stability."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             Stay The Course On Fiscal Consolidation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Assessing Japan's deepening fiscal troubles, the IMF warned that gross\par public debt - already the highest in the developed world as a percentage\par of the economy - is on track to exceed a staggering 174% of GDP next\par year and hit 183.2% of output by 2008.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The IMF called "appropriate" the relatively tight fiscal policy\par pursued by Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who has pledged to keep\par core government spending flat next fiscal year from April, following a\par similar policy this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But it said Japan "may need to go beyond the official targets," which\par also include eliminating the primary budget deficit - the budget gap\par minus debt financing and other non-discretionary items - by the early\par 2010s.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Specific measures," the IMF said, "could include cutting back further\par on low-value public investment projects, reforming public pension and\par medical systems, strengthening the corporate and personal income tax\par bases, and raising the consumption tax."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par jonathan.sobledowjones.com\par    -Edited by Alastair Gale\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030918dz9i0009y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
