\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr/Yen Falls As Exporters, Speculators Sell \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar fell against the yen in Asia Thursday as Japanese exporters took advantage of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise early in the session to sell dollars for yen to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. \par \par \uc2  Initially, Japanese banks bought the dollar to fulfill corporate demand toward the 0100 GMT Tokyo fixing, but exporters quickly sold the U.S. unit, dragging it down near the psychologically key Y116.00 level. \par \par \uc2  Ongoing jitters that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might sell yen via the Bank of Japan helped to put a floor under the dollar around Y116.00, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar remains dangerously close to the Y115s, so the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is there," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Some market participants believe the Japanese authorities could quietly step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell yen if the dollar retests its Wednesday low of Y115.85, as the authorities are still perceived as opposing a sharp dollar slide that could break below the psychologically and technically important Y115 level. \par \par \uc2  But speculators continued to probe the dollar/yen downside amid lingering notions that the Finance Ministry may scale back its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid possible criticism from its trading partners ahead of this weekend's meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers in Dubai. \par \par \uc2  Some traders think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets may be discussed at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y115.96, below Y116.21 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was at Y116.09 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1276, lower than $1.1286 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.1148 in Tokyo Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y130.74, down from Y131.14 in New York late Wednesday. The euro was at Y129.42 in Tokyo late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6075, marginally up from $1.6074 in New York and higher than $1.5874 in Tokyo Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar hovering nervously near Y116, U.S. funds later sold the greenback, gunning for a slew of stop-loss sales orders in the Y115.50-Y115.80 zone. \par \par \uc2  Although dip-buying interest in the high-Y115s stopped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from triggering those orders, the dollar could fall toward them again later in the global session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "If all of these stop-loss sales orders are executed, the dollar may take a stab at the Y115.20-30 area, where there's supposed to be buying interest," said a Tokyo dealer. \par \par \uc2  Improving prospects for Japan's economy may also attract more foreign capital into the Japanese stock market in the medium term, thereby weighing on the dollar and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen, and making the Finance Ministry's task of preventing an excessive yen rise more difficult. \par \par \uc2  Reflecting concerns over the euro's downside risk against the yen, some Japanese investors sold euros for yen to hedge the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure on their euro-denominated securities holdings. \par \par \uc2  While the euro/yen fell slightly, it later got a modest boost from a cross-trading tail wind caused by speculator-led buying of euros for dollars, possibly to create new euro-long positions ahead of the weekend G7 meeting, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030918dz9i0008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
