\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: BOJ Absence Keeps Mkt Guessing Ahead Of G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 18:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever and Tom Barkley \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--By allowing the dollar to sink through Y115.00 Thursday to its lowest level against the yen since February 2001, the Bank of Japan has surprised the market by its absence.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Certainly, the lack of any clear indication of BOJ action to halt the dollar's slide - or more pertinently, the yen's rise - has \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts scratching their heads.  \par \par \uc2 After all, by most market estimates, the BOJ, on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has sold up to Y2 trillion to buy between $15 billion and $20 billion this month alone to keep the yen at a competitive level for Japan's exporters. That comes on top of the $80 billion spent in the first seven months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected in September have when the dollar was at higher levels - as high as Y116.80 - than where it's trading now. At 12:45 p.m. EDT (1645 GMT), the dollar was trading at Y115.25, a recovery from the morning's low at Y114.73.  \par \par \uc2 The last time the dollar had weakened that far against the yen was February 2001. The Y114.70-80 area is also considered to be a break-even exchange rate for Japanese exporters, which is why most observers had expected the BOJ to fiercely defend that level or the Y115.00 "line in the sand."  \par \par \uc2 But the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't shown its hand so far Thursday, and observers give three main reasons that could explain its absence.  \par \par \uc2 The first is the meeting this weekend of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrialized nations in Dubai. The U.S. is a loud proponent of letting free market forces determine exchange rates, so ahead of the meeting - with \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to be high on the agenda - Japan may be indulging in diplomatic niceties.  \par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Japan may be easing off in order to appease the U.S. ahead of the G7, but I don't believe there will be a wholesale abandonment by the Japanese (authorities) of their \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said Alex Beuzelin, senior market analyst at Ruesch International in Washington D.C.  \par \par \uc2 "Once the G7 is out of way, they will be free to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they will," said Peter Frank, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-Amro Bank in Chicago. He added, however, that he would be surprised to see any major action by the BOJ going into this weekend's meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Secondly, against this backdrop, it could be a question of strategy. By letting the market build up huge short dollar and long yen positions, the effectiveness of any yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be greatly enhanced.  \par \par \uc2 "It could be ... a bear trap and (the BOJ) will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the market's overextended," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Beuzelin at Ruesch International agrees, saying: "Ultimately that could inflict greater pain on traders, and would maybe dissuade the market from renewing its test of BOJ resolve, at least in the near term."  \par \par \uc2 Or thirdly, with the Japanese economy showing clearer signs of recovery and the stock market in particular in rare health, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities might be beginning to realize aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's appreciation simply won't work. Huge flows are going into Japanese stocks, economic data generally continues to surprise on the upside, and growth estimates for Japan are being raised.  \par \par \uc2 As a result, the pressure on the yen is firmly upward, and virtually the entire \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - from short-term speculators and technical traders to long-term investors such as fund managers - are betting on further yen gains.  \par \par \uc2 Even the BOJ, with its impressive and aggressive track record in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will find it tough to stem the tide. Any future \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will be aimed at smoothing out the yen's rise rather than reversing it, according to analysts. Since the beginning of 2003, the BOJ has mainly chosen to make its presence in markets felt through orders placed with commercial banks, so-called covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096;        jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    -Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 43??;            tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020030918dz9i00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
