\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ IMF Affirms Upgraded Japan GDP View; 2.0% Growth In 2003  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,023 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 14:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Reflecting surprising resilience in the world's second- \par largest economy, the International Monetary Fund on Thursday affirmed its \par recently upgraded forecast for economic growth in Japan at 2.0% this year. \par  \par But it warned that the country's economic rebound may fizzle unless Tokyo \par takes more aggressive steps to end persistent price falls, clean up the debt- \par clogged financial system and rein in ballooning public borrowing. \par  \par "Looking forward, although risks have become more balanced, the outlook \par remains clouded by entrenched deflation and by persistent weakness in \par corporate, financial and public sector balance sheets," the IMF said in its \par latest World Economic Outlook. \par  \par The forecast growth in Japan's gross domestic product this year, initially \par released in a report two weeks ago, is more than double the 0.8% rise the IMF \par predicted in April. \par  \par The IMF said a surprising burst of growth in the second quarter, combined with \par buoyant Tokyo share prices and rebounding demand for Japanese goods overseas, \par would help fuel growth into next year. \par  \par Japan's real GDP grew at a blazing annualized rate of 3.9% in the April-June \par quarter, outpacing growth in the U.S. and Europe, while the Nikkei 225 Stock \par Average has rallied 44% from its 20-year nadir in April. \par  \par The IMF predicted Japan's GDP would grow 1.4% in 2004, better than the 1.0% \par expansion it had previously forecast. \par  \par But despite the brighter view, the IMF said Japan's economy, which pulled out \par of its worst postwar recession last year, "remains vulnerable to a range of \par domestic and external shocks." \par  \par Among the risks cited by the IMF was a sustained rise in the value of the yen, \par which could damp exports, cut into corporate profits and exacerbate deflation. \par  \par The yen has gained a modest 2% against the dollar since the start of the year, \par but it has been under near-constant pressure to appreciate. Further rises have \par been forestalled only by an unprecedented campaign by the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par authorities to curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength through yen-selling market \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par Other potential dangers cited by the IMF included \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's growing public debt, \par which could trigger a jump in real interest rates, and rising yields on \par Japanese government bonds, which could weaken balance sheets at Japan's JGB- \par hoarding banks, causing them to cut lending. \par  \par Yields on 10-year JGBs peaked earlier this month at 1.675%, nearly four times \par a record low of 0.43% set in June. \par  \par "To contain these risks, a bold strategy is needed that tackles the underlying \par weaknesses in the economy," the IMF said. \par  \par  \par            Bank Reforms, BOJ Deflation Steps Key \par  \par Such a strategy would include "rapid and forceful measures" to more quickly \par reduce the vast pile of bad loans weighing on the financial system, the IMF \par said. \par  \par While Japan's major banks have been chiseling away at their mountains of bad \par loans, the Financial Services Agency recently said the amount of nonperforming \par loans at major banks as of the end of March stood at Y43.728 trillion. \par  \par The IMF warned "the quality of bank capital remains poor," with deferred tax \par assets - future tax credits that can only be realized if the bank claiming \par them makes a profit - making up a sizable amount of top-tier capital. \par  \par The IMF noted approvingly the publicly funded bailout in May of Resona \par Holdings Inc. (J.RSN or 8308), Japan's fifth-largest bank, which had suffered \par a crippling fall in its capital. \par  \par But it said the effective nationalization of Resona needed to be "supported by \par broader reforms to strengthen bank and corporate governance," reduce \par nonperforming loans and encourage more struggling lenders to merge. \par  \par "Accelerated disposal of NPLs and targeted use of public funds to recapitalize \par weak but systemically important banks remain essential to ending the sector's \par decade-long deterioration," the IMF said. \par  \par The IMF also called on the Bank of Japan to take more aggressive steps to end \par six years of persistent price falls, which have eaten into corporate profits \par and pushed up firms' real debt loads despite historically low interest rates. \par  \par While the BOJ's quantitative easing policy, which targets short-term interest \par rates near zero, has helped stabilize the financial system, "it has been \par unable to end deflation, ease deflationary expectations, or promote growth in \par broad money and bank credit," the IMF said. \par  \par "In view of these difficulties, a more aggressive policy approach is clearly \par needed." \par  \par The IMF predicted declines in consumer prices would accelerate to an average \par of 0.6% next year after easing somewhat in 2003 to an average of 0.3%. \par  \par It repeated its call on the BOJ to set "a transparent medium-term inflation \par target" and lay out a specific timeframe for ending deflation. \par  \par It also urged the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy more JGBs and target a broader array of \par assets for purchase. \par  \par "Concerns about potential Bank of Japan losses on JGB holdings," it \par said, "should not be allowed to detract from the pursuit of price stability." \par  \par             Stay The Course On Fiscal Consolidation \par  \par Assessing Japan's deepening fiscal troubles, the IMF warned that gross public \par debt - already the highest in the developed world as a percentage of the \par economy - is on track to exceed a staggering 174% of GDP next year and hit \par 183.2% of output by 2008. \par  \par The IMF called "appropriate" the relatively tight fiscal policy pursued by \par Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who has pledged to keep core government \par spending flat next fiscal year from April, following a similar policy this \par year. \par  \par But it said Japan "may need to go beyond the official targets," which also \par include eliminating the primary budget deficit - the budget gap minus debt \par financing and other non-discretionary items - by the early 2010s. \par  \par "Specific measures," the IMF said, "could include cutting back further on low- \par value public investment projects, reforming public pension and medical \par systems, strengthening the corporate and personal income tax bases, and \par raising the consumption tax." \par  \par    -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; \par jonathan.sobledowjones.com \par    -Edited by Alastair Gale \par Copyright 2003 OsterDowJones Commodity News (ODJ). All rights reserved. \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020030918dz9i0006y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
