\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 - Y 4-month high vs dollar tests BOJ resolve.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 13:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The yen hit four-month highs against the dollar on Thursday as markets tested the Japanese monetary authorities' resolve to cap its strength ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven industrialised nations this weekend.  \par \par \uc2 Markets are wary of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect a fragile export-led recovery, after record levels of official yen sales this year, but are reluctant to be short yen amid a Tokyo equities rally that has shares at 15-month peaks.  \par \par \uc2 Investors are also wary that the United States will chastise \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  for its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at the G7 meeting in Dubai, and that if Japan stands away from the market, the yen will rally sharply.  \par \par \uc2 "Markets have really pushed back expectations of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of the G7," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 "It does seem that this G7 meeting has taken on a focus of its own, probably greater than it has on previous meetings. Normally we wouldn't expect to see much from the G7."  \par \par \uc2 By 1130 GMT, the yen was up two-thirds of a percent on the dollar and near the day's highs of 115.27 - its strongest showing since May. Japan's Nikkei stock average ended above 11,000 for the first time in 15 months.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was also half a percent higher on the euro at 130.35 but broadly within recent ranges.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rise was helped by growing speculation that overseas hedge funds would increase their exposure to Japanese stocks, Tokyo dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Financial sources said several major European and U.S. hedge funds were in Tokyo this week meeting politicians and influential analysts, seeking information about the next Japanese administration in order to decide on potential investments.  \par \par \uc2 BRACING FOR U.S. JOBS  \par \par \uc2 Markets were bracing for U.S. weekly jobless claims at 1230 GMT, which are expected to come in at 410,000, from the previous 422,000.  \par \par \uc2 The numbers will likely be watched closely after nasty surprises recently from reports on the labour market - which is increasingly seen as the weakest link in the U.S. recovery.  \par \par \uc2 However, the G7, and its implications for the yen, was casting the longest shadow over the market.  \par \par \uc2 "The G7 and the yen are going to be driving markets in the very short term," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS.  \par \par \uc2 "If the G7 results in a communique to the effect that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alignments need to be shared more broadly, or some sort of code meaning that Asia needs to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate, that would put more downward pressure on dollar/yen and euro/yen."  \par \par \uc2 The September Philadelphia Fed survey, due at 1600 GMT, is forecast to weaken to a reading of 17.3, from the previous 22.1.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling hit its highest level against the dollar since early August, $1.6155, extending gains made after minutes of the Bank of England's (BoE) latest monetary meeting released on Wednesday fuelled talk of a UK rate rise.  \par \par \uc2 BoE Governor Mervyn King said there might be a further upward revision to first-half growth and that third quarter would be stronger than the first six months of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 EURO OUTLOOK  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was meeting on Thursday, with an announcement expected at 1230 GMT, but it was not expected to discuss monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was trading at $1.1285, flat versus the New York close but still near the top of recent ranges, after breaking through key technical levels in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF said in its World Economic Outlook on Thursday that the ECB may need to cut rates again if the euro goes up significantly and that Japan's economy was still vulnerable.  \par \par \uc2 Its chief economist, Kenneth Rogoff, also said the U.S. current account deficit must some day unwind, causing a sharp fall in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss National Bank issues its quarterly monetary policy review around 1200 GMT, and is widely expected to maintain the generous stance that has kept short-term Swiss rates near zero.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc was steady against the euro at 1.5573, compared with three-year lows of 1.5625 set on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc was flat against the dollar at 1.3800 but close to its strongest levels since mid-August.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030919dz9i004ci\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
