\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options - Dollar drop triggers yen options.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 21:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The dollar's sharp decline against the yen on Thursday accelerated as it crashed through options barriers, and dealers reported lingering options triggers near 114.50 yen and below.  \par \par \uc2 Options below the current dollar level near 115.30 yen could slow the pace of decline. But if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls through the options triggers, losses could pick up steam, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback sagged to 2-1/2-year lows below 115.0 yen earlier on Thursday, shedding more than 1 percent as traders challenged the Bank of Japan's willingness to sell the yen to support that nation's economic recovery. The Japanese export sector relies on a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make goods more attractive to overseas consumers.  \par \par \uc2 Options traders noted large barriers at 115 yen. Barrier options give the holder a right to trade an amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a set price for a specified time. But they become worthless if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits an agreed-upon price barrier.  \par \par \uc2 "There are others; we hit some at 115.50 and 115.25 beginning the (dollar's) move down as well. I think we have sort of passed the peak in terms of the number of barriers," said Jim Kamphoefner, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options strategist at Bank of America in San Francisco.  \par \par \uc2 He noted additional yen call options with strikes likely at 114.50 yen. Calls give the holder the right, but not the obligation, to buy the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it reaches a predetermined strike price. Put options give the holder the right to sell.  \par \par \uc2 The sudden dollar decline triggered a rally in implied volatility, a measure of how much the options market expects the price of the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move during the life of the option. Volatility for one-month yen options rose to near 11.20 percent on Thursday from around 9.3 percent reported earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar stabilized in the New York afternoon, inching back above 115 yen, with some traders suspecting Japanese monetary authorities may soon step in to sell the yen. Japan's vice finance minister, Zembei Mizoguchi, warned that the dollar's fall below 115 yen had been "a bit rapid" and signaled that Japan was ready to take action as needed.  \par \par \uc2 In the event of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "There were definitely barriers at 115, and then you had some people trying to front-run ahead of the BoJ, but it doesn't look like we got any of that," said one East Coast options trader. "There probably are barriers stacked all the way down to 110."  \par \par \uc2 Kamphoefner said that after Thursday's steep losses, traders had yet to buy many options with topside strikes, or. strikes above the current price level.  \par \par \uc2 "For the short term, you could look to place a range trade with a topside at 118.0 yen," he said. "But it's probably not that exciting yet, given the uncertainty."  \par \par \uc2 The one-month risk reversal, a measure of the options market's appetite for puts compared with calls, rose to near 2.5 percent favoring yen calls.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0