\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls vs yen - little impact from data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 18:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The dollar weakened against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, falling to a 2-1/2 year low earlier in the session, as traders continued to challenge the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to undertake yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The market's focus on the dollar/yen axis was intense and left considerations of fairly positive U.S. economic data releases in the shade. Traders dared the BoJ to redefine the "line in the sand", or level at which Japan is willing to tolerate dollar weakness, as the Group of Seven meeting in Dubai gets underway.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 0.73 percent to to 115.19 yen , after slipping to a 2-1/2 year low earlier, below 115 yen. The euro also declined 1.05 percent on the day to 129.59 yen against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the BoJ is in the market, although it is a possibility they may come in later," said Tommy Molloy, a trader at Bank Leumi in New York. "The bounce we saw above 115 yen was just prudent short-covering, more than anything else," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Both BoJ and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials in New York declined to comment when asked whether the BoJ was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The BoJ, which acts on behalf of the MoF in the case of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sells yen for dollars when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in an effort to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's competitive trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Molloy expects investors to test the 115 yen level again during the day, taking advantage of the BoJ's seeming reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market at the risk of public rebuke from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's G7 partners.  \par \par \uc2 Markets are wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect a fragile export-led recovery after record levels of official yen sales this year, but are reluctant to be short yen amid a Tokyo equities rally that has shares at 15-month peaks.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though we have seen a very conspicuous lack of BoJ action, it still takes a very gutsy trader to short dollar/yen and ride it all the way down," said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. DATA  \par \par \uc2 The focus on the yen and G7 meeting overshadowed the release of mostly firmer U.S. economic data.  \par \par \uc2 Initial weekly applications for U.S. jobless benefits fell to 399,000, below the widely watched 400,000 mark and offering the dollar a temporary, but fleeting respite.  \par \par \uc2 This was followed by a rise in a key U.S. economic forecasting gauge in August - the index of leading indicators - suggesting economic growth will strengthen further in the second half of the year.  \par \par \uc2 But this was followed by a weaker-than-expected Philadelphia Fed survey, an indicator of mid-Atalantic manufacturing activity. The index declined to 14.6, compared with expectations of 17.3.  \par \par \uc2 "More than anything else, the dollar is being affected by the G7 meeting," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "In terms of the jobless data, 399,000 and expectations of 410,000 were not that significant. What we should look at is the continuing claims which are still rising," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.35 percent on the day at $1.1246 . Sterling gained 0.25 percent against the dollar to $1.6143 , continuing to benefit from expectations of an early monetary policy tightening by the Bank of England.  \par \par \uc2 SNB HOLDS RATES  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss National Bank left key short-term rates unchanged as expected on Thursday, saying its near-zero rate policy is appropriate given low inflation in the Swiss economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Switzerland, which is mired in a double-dip recession, is unlikely to recover until next year.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.46 percent against the Swiss franc to 1.3850 francs .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030918dz9i00340\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
